/* Generated by scripts/check.mjs --write from the lesson pages and glossary-terms.js. Don't edit by hand. */
window.SEARCH_INDEX = {"lessons":[{"slug":"core-how-the-web-works","num":"1.1","title":"How the Web Works","desc":"Clients and servers, DNS, HTTP and URLs: what happens between typing an address and seeing a page. Plus how to organize a site's files.","props":["client","DNS","HTTP 404","URL"],"intro":"A website is a folder of files on someone else's computer. Follow what happens between typing an address and seeing a page, then set up the folder your own site will live in.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-clients-and-servers","title":"Clients and servers","kind":"Read","text":"Every computer on the web plays one of two roles. A client is the device you browse on, and the browser running on it. A server is a computer somewhere else that stores websites and sends out their files when asked. ClientYour laptop or phone, running a browser request →← response ServerStores the site's files and sends copies on request The client always starts the conversation by sending a request. The server answers with a response: the file, or a message explaining why it can't send it. A server never sends you anything you didn't ask for. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-other-parts-of-the-toolbox","title":"The other parts of the toolbox","kind":"Read","text":"A client and a server need more than each other. Picture the client as your house and the server as a shop: Your internet connection is the street between them. TCP/IP are the rules for how data travels along that street, like the rules for how a delivery is packed and addressed. DNS is the address book. You know the shop's name; DNS finds its street address. HTTP is the language the client and server use to talk: \"Can I have this file?\" \"Here it is.\" Component files are what the shop sells: code files (HTML, CSS, JavaScript) and assets (images, fonts, video). Play"},{"id":"sec-so-what-happens-exactly","title":"So what happens, exactly?","kind":"Play","text":"Step through what the browser does when you type trailclub.github.io and press Enter. Then break something and run it again. Everything works Typo in the domain Page doesn't exist Stylesheet path is wrong Next step → Show every step Start over What happens Read"},{"id":"sec-dns-explained","title":"DNS explained","kind":"Read","text":"Computers find each other by IP address, a string of numbers like 185.199.108.153. Nobody wants to remember those, so websites also get a domain name, like github.io. The Domain Name System (DNS) is a worldwide set of servers that look up which IP address goes with which domain name. Your browser asks DNS first, every time it meets a domain it doesn't already know. Read"},{"id":"sec-packets-explained","title":"Packets explained","kind":"Read","text":"Files don't travel in one piece. They're split into thousands of small chunks called packets. Each packet can take a different route, and the browser puts them back together when they arrive. If one gets lost, only that small piece is sent again, and many people can download the same site at once without waiting in line. Read"},{"id":"sec-http-basics","title":"HTTP basics","kind":"Read","text":"HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol) is the set of rules for requests and responses. Every response starts with an HTTP status code, a three-digit number that says how the request went. You'll meet these all the time: Code Means You'll see it when 200 OK The file was found and sent. You never notice it. 301 Moved permanently The page has a new address; the browser follows it automatically. 404 Not found Nothing exists at that path: a typo, or a capital letter in the wrong place. 500 Server error Something broke on the server's side, not yours. Today almost every site uses HTTPS, the same rules sent over an encrypted connection, so nobody in between can read or change what's sent. Play"},{"id":"sec-components-of-a-url","title":"Components of a URL","kind":"Play","text":"Every URL has the same parts. Click a part to see what it does, or paste any address on the right. Take apart any URL This uses the same URL parser your browser uses. Read"},{"id":"sec-dealing-with-files","title":"Dealing with files","kind":"Read","text":"When you build a site, you build that folder of files yourself. Keep everything for one site in one project folder, with the home page, index.html, at the top. When someone visits the folder without naming a file, the server sends index.html. What structure should a website have? trail-club/ ├── index.html ├── about.html ├── images/ │ └── mesa-trail.jpg ├── styles/ │ └── style.css └── scripts/ └── main.js File names Your laptop may forgive sloppy names; servers don't. Most servers treat Mesa.jpg and mesa.jpg as two different files, and spaces in names turn into %20 in addresses. So name every file and folder in lowercase, use hyphens instead of spaces, and stick to letters, numbers and hyphens. File paths To use one file from another, you write its path: images/mesa-trail.jpg from index.html, for example. You'll learn every kind of path in Creating Links. Tip Set your computer to always show file extensions. A file that looks like photo.jpg may really be photo.jpg.png, and the path won't match. Play"},{"id":"sec-will-this-file-name-work","title":"Will this file name work?","kind":"Play","text":"Type file names, one per line. Each one is checked against the rules that keep names working on every server. File names Verdict Practice"},{"id":"sec-build-a-full-url","title":"Build a full URL","kind":"Practice","text":"A short path only works inside your own site. To link to another site, you need every part of the address. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-file-names","title":"Fix the file names","kind":"Practice","text":"The files were renamed to follow the rules. Update the page so its paths match."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"The client (your browser) sends requests; the server sends responses. DNS turns a domain name into an IP address before anything else happens. HTTP carries requests and responses. Every response has a status code: 200 OK, 404 Not found. A page is many files. The browser asks for each one separately. A URL is protocol, domain, path, and sometimes a query and a fragment. Name files in lowercase with hyphens, and keep one site in one folder with index.html at the top."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"A capital letter in a file name that the code spells in lowercase. It works on your laptop and 404s on the server. Spaces in file names. Naming the home page Home.html or index.htm instead of index.html. Linking to another site without https://, so the browser treats the domain as a file in your folder. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-how-the-web-works/quiz"],"exercises":["core-how-the-web-works/ex-url","core-how-the-web-works/ex-files"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-basic-html-syntax","num":"1.2","title":"Basic HTML Syntax","desc":"Elements, attributes and documents: the grammar every page is written in, and the characters that need escaping.","props":["<p>","attributes","<!doctype html>","&lt;"],"intro":"Every web page is written in the same small grammar: elements made of tags, attributes that add details, and a document that wraps it all up. Learn the parts, see how the browser reads them, and write a page of your own.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-is-html","title":"What is HTML?","kind":"Read","text":"HTML (HyperText Markup Language) isn't a programming language. It's a markup language: you wrap pieces of content in labels that tell the browser what each piece is. Take this line of text: The Mesa Trail is 6.7 miles long. On its own it's just text. Wrap it in a paragraph element and the browser knows it's a paragraph: <p>The Mesa Trail is 6.7 miles long.</p> HTML lives in plain text files that end in .html. A site's home page is usually called index.html, because that's the file a server sends when someone visits a folder without naming a file. Tip Browsers don't care about capital letters in tag names, so <P> and <p> mean the same thing. Write them in lowercase anyway: it's what everyone else does, and it's easier to read. Read"},{"id":"sec-anatomy-of-an-html-element","title":"Anatomy of an HTML element","kind":"Read","text":"Most elements have three parts. The opening tag is the element's name between angle brackets. Then comes the content. The closing tag repeats the name with a forward slash in front of it: <p>Pack water and a map.</p> Forgetting the closing tag is the most common beginner mistake. Sometimes the page still looks fine, because the browser guesses where the element should end. Often it guesses wrong, and the rest of the page ends up bold, or a link swallows three paragraphs. Nesting elements Elements can go inside other elements. This is called nesting. To stress the word very in a paragraph, put a <strong> element inside the <p>: <p>The last mile is <strong>very</strong> steep.</p> Think of nested elements as boxes inside boxes. You close the inner box before the outer one, so the tags close in reverse order. <p><strong>…</p></strong> overlaps, and the browser has to repair it. Void elements A few elements can't hold any content, so they have no closing tag. These are void elements. A line break is one: <br>. So are <img>, <input>, <meta>, <link> and <hr>. You may see them written with a slash, as in <br />. The slash is allowed but does nothing. Play"},{"id":"sec-name-every-part","title":"Name every part","kind":"Play","text":"Click any part of the element, or pick a name below. Play"},{"id":"sec-how-the-browser-reads-your-nesting","title":"How the browser reads your nesting","kind":"Play","text":"Type some HTML, or pick an example. The tree shows the elements the browser actually builds. Compare what you wrote with what it made. Correct nesting Overlapping tags Missing closing tag Void element What you write What the browser builds Text inside an element is shown in italics. Void elements are marked as void, and nothing is ever inside them. Practice"},{"id":"sec-your-first-elements","title":"Your first elements","kind":"Practice","text":"Mark up one sentence with emphasis and strong importance. Read"},{"id":"sec-attributes","title":"Attributes","kind":"Read","text":"Attributes add information about an element that you don't want to show as content. They always go in the opening tag. A class attribute, for example, gives the element a name you can target with CSS: <p class=\"trail-note\">Closed after heavy rain.</p> Every attribute follows the same pattern: a space after the element name (or the previous attribute), the attribute's name, an equals sign, and the value in quotes. Adding attributes to an element Some elements need attributes to work at all. An image needs src to say which file to show. It should also have alt, the alt text read aloud by screen readers and shown if the image fails to load. width and height let the browser save the right amount of space before the image arrives: <img src=\"royal-arch.jpg\" alt=\"Sandstone arch above a pine forest\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\"> Boolean attributes Some attributes are simply on or off. Writing the name turns them on; leaving it out turns them off. disabled is one: it greys out a form control so nobody can use it. <input type=\"text\" disabled> You might see disabled=\"disabled\" or even disabled=\"false\". Both still turn it on. Only leaving the attribute out turns it off. Omitting quotes around attribute values A value with no spaces works without quotes, like href=trails.html. But as soon as a value has a space, the browser thinks the value has ended and treats each extra word as a new attribute. That's why you should always use quotes. Single or double quotes? Single and double quotes both work. Pick one style and stick with it. Just don't mix them in one value, like title=\"Mesa Trail'. To put a quote inside a value, wrap the value in the other kind of quote, or write the quote as &quot;. Play"},{"id":"sec-test-an-attribute","title":"Test an attribute","kind":"Play","text":"Type the attributes for a tag and see which attributes the browser finds, and what value each one gets. Try an example first. Quoted No quotes, with spaces Boolean Quotes inside quotes Mixed quotes Your opening tag < a img input > What the browser finds Attribute Value Read"},{"id":"sec-anatomy-of-an-html-document","title":"Anatomy of an HTML document","kind":"Read","text":"Elements on their own aren't a web page yet. A full page wraps them in a few standard pieces that every HTML file starts with: <!doctype html> <html lang=\"en-US\"> <head> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"> <title>Boulder Trails</title> </head> <body> <p>This is my page.</p> </body> </html> The first line, the DOCTYPE, is a leftover from the early web. It used to point to a set of rules the page had to follow. Today it does one job: it tells the browser to use modern, standard behavior instead of an old compatibility mode. Write <!doctype html> and forget about it. Everything else sits inside the <html> element. The head holds information about the page that isn't shown on it, such as the title in the browser tab. The <body> holds everything visitors see. Click each line below to see what it does. Play"},{"id":"sec-walk-through-a-document","title":"Walk through a document","kind":"Play","text":"Click a line to see what it's for. Practice"},{"id":"sec-mark-up-a-trailhead-page","title":"Mark up a trailhead page","kind":"Practice","text":"Turn plain text into a page body with a heading, a paragraph, links and an image. Play"},{"id":"sec-whitespace-in-html","title":"Whitespace in HTML","kind":"Play","text":"Type extra spaces, tabs and blank lines. The browser squashes every run of whitespace into a single space. Then switch on <pre>, the one element that keeps it. What you type in your HTML Put it in a <pre> element What the browser shows Because spaces collapse, you're free to indent your code to show its nesting. Indent each level by two spaces. To start a new paragraph, use a new <p>, and use CSS for spacing. Play"},{"id":"sec-character-references-special-characters","title":"Character references: special characters","kind":"Play","text":"Five characters mean something to HTML itself. To show them as text, write a character reference instead. Type below to see what needs escaping. Text you want to show Write this in your HTML The five special characters Shows Write Why < &lt; Would start a tag > &gt; Would end a tag \" &quot; Would end a double-quoted value ' &apos; Would end a single-quoted value & &amp; Would start a reference In ordinary text only < and & really have to be escaped; quotes matter inside attribute values. Other symbols like © or é don't need references at all, as long as the page has <meta charset=\"utf-8\">. Read"},{"id":"sec-html-comments","title":"HTML comments","kind":"Read","text":"A comment is a note in your code that the browser ignores. Start it with <!-- and end it with -->. Use comments to explain why something is there, or to switch off a piece of the page while you test: <p>Open daily, sunrise to sunset.</p> <!-- Hidden until the winter hours are confirmed: <p>Winter hours: 7am to 5pm.</p> --> Watch out Comments don't show on the page, but anyone can read them with View Source. Never put passwords, private notes or anything you wouldn't want public in a comment. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-broken-markup","title":"Fix the broken markup","kind":"Practice","text":"The browser guesses its way around each of these mistakes. Fix them so it doesn't have to."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"An element is an opening tag, content and a closing tag. Void elements like <img> and <br> have only the opening tag. Nested elements close in reverse order: the inner one first. Attributes go in the opening tag as name=\"value\". Boolean attributes are on when present, off when absent. Every page starts with <!doctype html>, then <html> containing a <head> and a <body>. Whitespace collapses to one space, except inside <pre>. Write &lt; and &amp; to show < and & as text."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Leaving out a closing tag, so formatting leaks into everything after it. Closing nested tags in the wrong order, like <p><em>…</p></em>. Unquoted attribute values with spaces. Only the first word counts. Writing disabled=\"false\" and expecting it to switch the attribute off. Curly “smart quotes” pasted from a word processor. Browsers don't treat them as quotes. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-basic-html-syntax/quiz"],"exercises":["core-basic-html-syntax/ex-first","core-basic-html-syntax/ex-page","core-basic-html-syntax/ex-fix"],"questions":8},{"slug":"core-webpage-metadata","num":"1.3","title":"What's in the Head?","desc":"Titles, character sets, descriptions, icons, stylesheets and scripts: the page information nobody sees.","props":["<title>","<meta>","<link>","defer"],"intro":"Nothing in the head shows on the page, but it decides what the browser tab says, how the text is decoded, what search results and shared links look like, and which CSS and JavaScript load.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-is-the-html-head","title":"What is the HTML head?","kind":"Read","text":"Every page has two parts. The <body> holds everything visitors see. The head holds information about the page, called metadata, that the browser uses but doesn't display: <!doctype html> <html lang=\"en-US\"> <head> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"> <title>Royal Arch Trail | Boulder Trail Club</title> </head> <body> <h1>Royal Arch Trail</h1> </body> </html> Real pages often have a dozen or more lines in the head. This lesson covers the ones you'll use on every page. Read"},{"id":"sec-adding-a-title","title":"Adding a title","kind":"Read","text":"Two elements sound alike but do different jobs. The <h1> is the big heading on the page. The <title> goes in the head and names the page everywhere else: the browser tab, bookmarks, history, and the blue link in search results. Put the most specific words first, so they still show in a crowded tab bar: Royal Arch Trail | Boulder Trail Club beats Boulder Trail Club | Hikes | Royal Arch. And never leave it as \"Untitled\" or \"Home\". Play"},{"id":"sec-see-your-head-everywhere","title":"See your head everywhere","kind":"Play","text":"Edit the title and description, and switch the icon and share tags on and off. Watch the tab, the search result and the shared link change. The page's own <h1> never changes. <head> <title> meta description link rel=\"icon\" Open Graph tags Browser tab, with the page's h1 below New Tab Royal Arch Trail Search result Shared in a chat or social post Read"},{"id":"sec-metadata-the-meta-element","title":"Metadata: the <meta> element","kind":"Read","text":"Most metadata goes in <meta> elements. They're void elements: no content, no closing tag. Most have a name saying what kind of information it is and a content holding the information. Specifying your document's character encoding A file is just bytes. <meta charset=\"utf-8\"> tells the browser how to turn those bytes back into letters. UTF-8 covers nearly every writing system, so put it first in every head. With the wrong encoding, anything beyond plain English letters turns into gibberish. Try it below. Adding an author and description <meta name=\"author\" content=\"Boulder Trail Club\"> <meta name=\"description\" content=\"A steep 3.4-mile round trip to a sandstone arch above Chautauqua, with views over Boulder.\"> The meta description often becomes the grey text under your link in search results, so write it for people: one or two sentences, about 70 to 160 characters. You may see a keywords meta tag in old code. Search engines ignore it. Other types of metadata Sites like Facebook, Slack and Discord read Open Graph tags to build the preview card when someone shares your link. They use property instead of name: <meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Royal Arch Trail\"> <meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://trailclub.github.io/images/royal-arch.jpg\"> Watch out og:image needs a full https:// address. Another company's server fetches the image, and it doesn't know where your page lives, so a relative path like images/arch.jpg won't work. Play"},{"id":"sec-when-the-character-set-is-wrong","title":"When the character set is wrong","kind":"Play","text":"Type some text with accents, curly quotes or another alphabet. The right panel shows what visitors see if the browser decodes your UTF-8 file with the wrong character set. Your text, saved as UTF-8 The browser decodes it as utf-8 windows-1252 (Western) iso-8859-5 (Cyrillic) What visitors see Read"},{"id":"sec-adding-custom-icons-to-your-site","title":"Adding custom icons to your site","kind":"Read","text":"A favicon is the little picture in the browser tab and in bookmarks. One SVG covers most browsers; add a PNG for phones that save your site to their home screen. 1. Make a square image Use a simple shape or one or two letters. Fine detail disappears at 16 × 16 pixels. Save it as favicon.svg, or as a 32 × 32 favicon.png. 2. Link it in the head <link rel=\"icon\" href=\"favicon.svg\" type=\"image/svg+xml\"> rel says how the linked file relates to the page. 3. Add a home-screen icon <link rel=\"apple-touch-icon\" href=\"icon-180.png\" sizes=\"180x180\"> A 180 × 180 PNG, used when someone adds your site to a phone's home screen. Read"},{"id":"sec-applying-css-and-javascript-to-html","title":"Applying CSS and JavaScript to HTML","kind":"Read","text":"Styles and scripts usually live in their own files, linked from the head: <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"css/style.css\"> <script src=\"js/trail.js\" defer></script> <link> is a void element. <script> isn't: it always needs its closing tag, even when it's empty. The browser reads a page from top to bottom. When it reaches a plain <script>, it stops and runs the script straight away, before the rest of the page exists. A script that tries to change a heading further down can't find it. The defer attribute fixes that: the script downloads in the background and runs once the whole page has been read. Play"},{"id":"sec-when-does-the-script-run","title":"When does the script run?","kind":"Play","text":"The script below looks for the page's <h1> and changes its text. Move it around and switch defer on and off, then step through what the browser does. Where is the script? In the head At the end of the body Add defer What the browser does, in order Read"},{"id":"sec-setting-the-primary-language-of-the-document","title":"Setting the primary language of the document","kind":"Read","text":"The lang attribute on <html> says what language the page is written in. Screen readers use it to pick the right voice and pronunciation, and search engines and translation tools use it too. Use a short language code like en, or add a region: en-US, es-MX. When one phrase is in a different language, mark just that phrase. A screen reader then switches pronunciation for it and back again: <p>The garden's name is <span lang=\"ja\">日本庭園</span>, \"Japanese garden\".</p> Practice"},{"id":"sec-fill-in-the-head","title":"Fill in the head","kind":"Practice","text":"This trail page has a nearly empty head. Give it everything from this lesson. Practice"},{"id":"sec-mark-the-languages","title":"Mark the languages","kind":"Practice","text":"Tell browsers and screen readers which language each part of the page is in."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"The head holds metadata: information about the page that isn't shown on it. <title> names the page in tabs and search results; <h1> is the heading on the page. Put <meta charset=\"utf-8\"> first in every head. <meta name=\"…\" content=\"…\"> adds an author, a description and more. Open Graph tags use property. Link CSS with <link rel=\"stylesheet\"> and JavaScript with <script src defer></script>. Set lang on <html>, and on any phrase in another language."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Putting visible content, like a heading or an image, in the head. Leaving the title as \"Untitled\" or \"Home\", which says nothing in a row of tabs. Writing <script src=\"…\"> without </script>. Everything after it is swallowed. A script in the head without defer that can't find elements on the page. A relative path for og:image. It must be a full https:// URL. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-webpage-metadata/quiz"],"exercises":["core-webpage-metadata/ex-head","core-webpage-metadata/ex-lang"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-headings-paragraphs","num":"1.4","title":"Headings and Paragraphs","desc":"The six heading levels, building an outline that never skips a level, and why structure matters to readers and screen readers.","props":["<h1>–<h6>","<p>","outline","semantics"],"intro":"Headings and paragraphs give text its shape. Learn the six heading levels, how to nest them into an outline, and why that outline matters to readers, screen readers and search engines.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-headings-and-paragraphs","title":"Headings and paragraphs","kind":"Read","text":"Most text on the web is headings and paragraphs. A paragraph goes in a <p> element: <p>The Mesa Trail runs along the foot of the Flatirons for 6.7 miles.</p> A heading goes in one of six heading elements, <h1> through <h6>. The number is the heading's level: <h1> is the main heading of the page, <h2>s are its sections, <h3>s are sections inside those, and so on. <h1>Mesa Trail</h1> <h2>Getting there</h2> <h3>Parking</h3> Read"},{"id":"sec-implementing-structural-hierarchy","title":"Implementing structural hierarchy","kind":"Read","text":"Headings nest like a table of contents. A trail guide might look like this: <h1>Boulder Trail Guide</h1> <p>Twelve hikes within half an hour of town.</p> <h2>Easy hikes</h2> <h3>Bobolink Trail</h3> <p>Flat, shady and good for kids.</p> <h2>Hard hikes</h2> <h3>Bear Peak</h3> <p>A steep 2,800-foot climb.</p> Three rules keep an outline easy to follow: One <h1> per page. It's the page's title. Don't skip levels. An <h2> section's subsections are <h3>s, never <h4>s. Keep it shallow. More than three levels gets hard to follow. If you need <h5>, split the page instead. Play"},{"id":"sec-see-your-outline","title":"See your outline","kind":"Play","text":"This is the list of headings a screen reader offers its users. Edit the HTML and watch the outline, and the warnings, update. Good outline Skipped level Two h1s Fake heading Your HTML Headings list Read"},{"id":"sec-why-do-we-need-structure","title":"Why do we need structure?","kind":"Read","text":"Browsers don't guess. Text typed into an HTML file without elements comes out as one long run of words, because whitespace collapses. Headings and paragraphs fix that, and they help in ways you can't see: People skim. Readers look for the heading that matches what they want and jump straight there. Screen readers navigate by headings. A screen reader user can list a page's headings and jump between them, the way you'd glance down a page. Search engines read headings to work out what a page is about. CSS and JavaScript need something to target. You can style every <h2> at once, but you can't style \"the line that's sort of a title\". Play"},{"id":"sec-the-same-text-with-and-without-structure","title":"The same text, with and without structure","kind":"Play","text":"Switch between plain text and marked-up text. The words are identical. Add headings and paragraphs What the browser shows Practice"},{"id":"sec-giving-content-structure","title":"Giving content structure","kind":"Practice","text":"Turn a block of plain text into a page with a heading outline. Read"},{"id":"sec-why-do-we-need-semantics","title":"Why do we need semantics?","kind":"Read","text":"You could make any text look like a heading with CSS: big and bold. But looking like a heading isn't the same as being one: <span style=\"font-size: 32px; font-weight: bold\">Mesa Trail</span> Sighted visitors see a heading. A screen reader sees an ordinary bit of text: it isn't in the headings list, so nobody can jump to it. Search engines don't treat it as important either. That's what semantic HTML means: choosing elements for what the content is, then using CSS for how it looks. Watch out The opposite mistake is just as common: picking <h4> because it's the size you want. Choose the level that fits the outline, then change the size with CSS. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-outline","title":"Fix the outline","kind":"Practice","text":"This page has three structural problems. The outline playground above can help you find them."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Paragraphs go in <p>; headings go in <h1>–<h6>. One <h1> per page, then <h2> sections, <h3> subsections. Never skip a level going down. Jumping back up (h3 to h2) is fine. Screen reader users navigate by headings, and search engines read them. Pick elements for meaning; change their look with CSS."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Choosing a heading level for its size instead of its place in the outline. Big bold text in a <span> or <p> instead of a real heading. Several <h1>s on one page. Using empty <p></p> or <br>s to add space. That's CSS's job. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-headings-paragraphs/quiz"],"exercises":["core-headings-paragraphs/ex-structure","core-headings-paragraphs/ex-fix"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-emphasis-advanced-text","num":"1.5","title":"Emphasis and Advanced Text","desc":"Emphasis and importance, then quotes, abbreviations, code, superscripts and dates: an element for every kind of text.","props":["<em>","<strong>","<blockquote>","<time>"],"intro":"HTML has an element for almost every kind of text: stressed words, important warnings, quotes, abbreviations, code, dates. Learn which to reach for, and why the meaning matters more than the look.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-emphasis-and-importance","title":"Emphasis and importance","kind":"Read","text":"When you speak, you stress certain words to change what a sentence means. <em> (emphasis) does that in HTML. Browsers show it in italics, and screen readers can say it with a different tone: <p>I <em>said</em> the trail was closed.</p> <strong> marks text as important: something the reader mustn't miss. Browsers show it in bold: <p><strong>Warning:</strong> the bridge washed out in May.</p> They can nest: <strong>Do <em>not</em> cross the creek.</strong> Read"},{"id":"sec-italic-bold-underline","title":"Italic, bold, underline…","kind":"Read","text":"<i>, <b> and <u> come from the early web, when HTML described looks. Today each has a narrower meaning, for text that's set apart but not stressed or important: <i>: foreign words, scientific names, titles of ships, a thought. <i lang=\"la\">Pinus ponderosa</i> <b>: keywords or product names you want to draw the eye to, without extra importance. <u>: text marked as wrong, like a misspelling. Readers expect underlined text to be a link, so use it rarely. If you only want the look, use CSS. If none of these meanings fit, you probably want <em> or <strong>. Play"},{"id":"sec-meaning-or-just-looks","title":"Meaning or just looks?","kind":"Play","text":"For each highlighted phrase, choose the element that matches its meaning. You'll see the result and the reasoning. Next sentence → Result Practice"},{"id":"sec-let-s-play-with-emphasis-and-importance","title":"Let's play with emphasis and importance","kind":"Practice","text":"Add meaning to a trail warning. Read"},{"id":"sec-quotations","title":"Quotations","kind":"Read","text":"A long quote that stands on its own goes in a <blockquote>; a short one inside a sentence goes in a <q>, which adds quotation marks for you. Both can say where the quote came from with a cite attribute holding a URL: <blockquote cite=\"https://www.nps.gov/romo/\"> <p>Take only pictures, leave only footprints.</p> </blockquote> <p>The ranger called it <q>the best view on the Front Range</q>.</p> Browsers don't show the cite attribute anywhere. To show a source, name it on the page with <cite>, which is for the title of a work: a book, an article, a film. <p>From <cite>Colorado Hiking Guide</cite>.</p> Read"},{"id":"sec-abbreviations-contact-details-superscript-and-subscript","title":"Abbreviations, contact details, superscript and subscript","kind":"Read","text":"<abbr> marks an abbreviation. Spell it out in full the first time it appears, or give the full form in a title attribute: <abbr title=\"Rocky Mountain National Park\">RMNP</abbr>. (You may see <acronym> in old code. It's been removed from HTML.) <address> holds contact details for the page or article it's in, not just any postal address. <sup> and <sub> raise and lower text: the 2 in 14,259 ft2 or in H2O. Read"},{"id":"sec-representing-computer-code","title":"Representing computer code","kind":"Read","text":"Five elements cover code and computers. <code> for a piece of code; <pre> around it to keep its spacing; <var> for a variable's name; <kbd> for keys the reader presses; <samp> for what a program prints: <p>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>S</kbd> to save.</p> Play"},{"id":"sec-the-advanced-text-elements","title":"The advanced text elements","kind":"Play","text":"Pick an element to see an example, what the browser shows, and what it's for. What the browser shows Read"},{"id":"sec-marking-up-times-and-dates","title":"Marking up times and dates","kind":"Read","text":"People write dates in many ways: Saturday, June 14, 6/14, 14 June 2026. Computers can't reliably read those. <time> wraps the human version and adds one standard version in its datetime attribute, so calendars, search engines and scripts can understand it: <p>Next group hike: <time datetime=\"2026-06-14T07:30\">Saturday, June 14 at 7:30am</time>.</p> Play"},{"id":"sec-build-a-datetime","title":"Build a datetime","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a date and time. Each line shows a valid datetime value, from the most general to the most precise. Date Time Time zone Mountain (summer) −06:00 Mountain (winter) −07:00 UTC (Z) datetime values Practice"},{"id":"sec-who-said-that","title":"Who said that?","kind":"Practice","text":"Mark up a trail newsletter with quotes, a source, an abbreviation and a date."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"<em> is stress emphasis; <strong> is importance. <i>, <b> and <u> have narrow meanings now. For looks alone, use CSS. <blockquote> for long quotes, <q> for short ones, <cite> for a work's title. <abbr title=\"…\"> expands abbreviations. <time datetime=\"…\"> gives dates a form computers understand."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Using <b> or <i> just to make text bold or italic. Using <blockquote> to indent text that isn't a quote. Putting a person's name in <cite>. It's for the title of a work. Typing quotation marks around a <q>, so they appear twice. A datetime in the wrong format, like datetime=\"June 14\". Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-emphasis-advanced-text/quiz"],"exercises":["core-emphasis-advanced-text/ex-emphasis","core-emphasis-advanced-text/ex-advanced"],"questions":7},{"slug":"core-lists","num":"1.6","title":"Lists","desc":"Unordered, ordered and description lists, nesting a list the right way, and the attributes that change how a list counts.","props":["<ul>","<ol>","<li>","<dl>"],"intro":"Packing lists, directions, glossaries, navigation menus: lists are everywhere. HTML has three kinds. Learn which to use, how to nest them, and the attributes that change how they count.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-unordered-lists","title":"Unordered lists","kind":"Read","text":"When the order doesn't matter, like a packing list, use an unordered list. The list is a <ul>, and each item is an <li> (list item) inside it. Browsers show a bullet before each item: <ul> <li>Water</li> <li>Snacks</li> <li>Rain jacket</li> </ul> A <ul> may only contain <li>s. Text, headings and paragraphs go inside the list items. Read"},{"id":"sec-ordered-lists","title":"Ordered lists","kind":"Read","text":"When order matters, like directions or a ranking, use an ordered list, <ol>. The items are still <li>s, and the browser numbers them for you: <ol> <li>Park at Chautauqua.</li> <li>Take the Bluebell Road uphill.</li> <li>Turn left at the Royal Arch sign.</li> </ol> Never type the numbers yourself. Add a step later and the browser renumbers everything. Three attributes change the counting: start=\"5\" begins at 5, reversed counts down, and type=\"a\", \"A\", \"i\" or \"I\" uses letters or Roman numerals. Play"},{"id":"sec-build-a-list","title":"Build a list","kind":"Play","text":"Type one item per line, pick the kind of list, and try the counting options. Items <ul> unordered <ol> ordered start reversed 1, 2, 3 a A i I type What the browser shows Practice"},{"id":"sec-marking-up-our-recipe-page","title":"Marking up our recipe page","kind":"Practice","text":"MDN's classic exercise, trail edition: a recipe with a heading outline, a shopping list and numbered steps. Read"},{"id":"sec-nesting-lists","title":"Nesting lists","kind":"Read","text":"A step can have sub-steps, and a category can have items. To nest a list, put the inner list inside an <li> of the outer one, after that item's text: <ol> <li>Pack your bag: <ul> <li>Water</li> <li>Snacks</li> </ul> </li> <li>Drive to the trailhead.</li> </ol> Watch out Putting the inner <ul> directly inside the outer list, between two <li>s, looks almost right but is invalid. Screen readers lose track of which item the sub-list belongs to. Play"},{"id":"sec-check-your-nesting","title":"Check your nesting","kind":"Play","text":"Compare the two versions, or edit the code. The tree shows what's inside what. Inside an li Between two lis Your HTML The tree Read"},{"id":"sec-description-lists","title":"Description lists","kind":"Read","text":"A description list pairs terms with descriptions, like a glossary. The list is a <dl>; each term is a <dt> (description term) and each description a <dd> (description details): <dl> <dt>Switchback</dt> <dd>A zigzag in a trail that makes a steep slope easier to climb.</dd> <dt>Cairn</dt> <dd>A pile of stones that marks the route.</dd> <dd>Don't build your own: they can mislead other hikers.</dd> </dl> A term can have several descriptions, as Cairn does above, and several terms can share one description. Browsers indent the descriptions. Practice"},{"id":"sec-marking-up-a-set-of-definitions","title":"Marking up a set of definitions","kind":"Practice","text":"Turn a trail glossary into a description list."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"<ul> when order doesn't matter, <ol> when it does. Items are <li>s. <dl> pairs <dt> terms with <dd> descriptions. A nested list goes inside an <li>, after its text. start, reversed and type change an ordered list's counting. Navigation menus are usually lists of links."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Typing numbers or dashes into the items instead of using <ol> or <ul>. Text or a <p> directly inside <ul>, outside any <li>. Nesting a list between two <li>s instead of inside one. Choosing <ul> or <ol> for the bullet style. That's CSS's job. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-lists/quiz"],"exercises":["core-lists/ex-recipe","core-lists/ex-dl"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-creating-links","num":"1.7","title":"Creating Links","desc":"Links to pages, sites, spots on a page, files and email, with paths that find their target and text that says where it goes.","props":["<a href>","../","#fragment","mailto:"],"intro":"Links are what make the web a web. Write links to other pages, other sites, spots on a page, files and email addresses, with paths that find their target and text that says where it goes.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-anatomy-of-a-link","title":"Anatomy of a link","kind":"Read","text":"A hyperlink connects a page to any other document or resource that has a URL. You make one by wrapping content in an <a> (anchor) element and putting the destination in its href (hypertext reference) attribute: <p>Start with the <a href=\"https://www.nps.gov/romo/\">Rocky Mountain National Park website</a>.</p> Block level links and image links Almost anything can be a link, not just text. Wrap a heading to make the whole heading clickable, or wrap an image to make a clickable logo. An image link's alt text becomes the link's text, so describe where it goes: <a href=\"index.html\"> <img src=\"logo.svg\" alt=\"Boulder Trail Club home page\"> </a> Adding supporting information with the title attribute A title attribute shows a tooltip when the mouse hovers over the link. Don't rely on it: keyboard and touchscreen users never see it, and many screen readers skip it. Anything important belongs in the link text. Read"},{"id":"sec-a-quick-primer-on-urls-and-paths","title":"A quick primer on URLs and paths","kind":"Read","text":"A path is directions from the page you're writing in to the file you want. Picture your site's folders: Same directory: just the file name. about.html Moving down into subdirectories: folder, slash, file. trails/mesa.html Moving back up into parent directories: ../ means \"the folder above\". ../index.html, or ../../index.html for two levels. Linking relative to the root directory: a leading slash starts at the top of the site. /images/logo.png Linking with full URLs: for another site, write the whole absolute path, starting with https://. The first four are relative paths: they work inside your own site, wherever it's hosted. Links within your site are internal; links to other sites are external and always need a full URL. Watch out Paths starting with / break on GitHub Pages project sites, which live in a folder like you.github.io/trail-club/. Stick to relative paths for your own files. Play"},{"id":"sec-find-the-path","title":"Find the path","kind":"Play","text":"Pick the file you're writing in, click the file you want to link to, then write the path yourself. I'm writing code in index.html trails/mesa.html trails/notes/week1.html css/style.css Click a file to link to it Type the path you'd write Show the answer How to read it Read"},{"id":"sec-document-fragments","title":"Document fragments","kind":"Read","text":"A link can jump to a spot on a page. Give the target element an id, then add # and that id to the end of the link. On the same page, the # part is enough: <h2 id=\"parking\">Parking</h2> … <a href=\"#parking\">Where to park</a> <a href=\"trails/mesa.html#parking\">Mesa Trail parking</a> And when a URL ends in a folder, like trails/, the server sends that folder's index.html. Play"},{"id":"sec-jump-to-a-fragment","title":"Jump to a fragment","kind":"Play","text":"Click the links in this little page. Each one jumps to the element with the matching id, and highlights it. The page Getting there · Parking · Rules Getting there Take Baseline Road west to the park. Parking The lot fills by 8am on weekends. Rules Dogs on leash. Stay on the trail. Back to the top Read"},{"id":"sec-link-best-practices","title":"Link best practices","kind":"Read","text":"Use clear link wording Screen reader users often pull up a list of every link on the page, and sighted readers skim for blue text. \"Click here\" means nothing out of context. Make the link text say where it goes: <a href=\"…\">Download the trail map</a>, not Click <a href=\"…\">here</a>. Don't use the raw URL as the text either, and keep it short. Linking to non-HTML resources: leave clear signposts If a link downloads a big file or opens a PDF, say so in the text: Trail map (PDF, 2 MB). Nobody likes a surprise download on a phone plan. Use the download attribute when linking to a download download tells the browser to save the file instead of opening it, and can suggest a file name: <a href=\"maps/mesa.pdf\" download=\"mesa-trail-map.pdf\">. When to open links in a new tab target=\"_blank\" opens a link in a new tab. Use it sparingly. It takes the choice away from the visitor, and the back button stops working. If you do, say so in the link text. Play"},{"id":"sec-is-this-good-link-text","title":"Is this good link text?","kind":"Play","text":"Type the text you'd put inside a link. It's checked against the rules above. Link text click here https://www.bouldercolorado.gov/trails Trail map (PDF, 2 MB) read more Mesa Trail guide In a screen reader's list of links Practice"},{"id":"sec-links-from-inside-a-folder","title":"Links from inside a folder","kind":"Practice","text":"Write relative paths from a page that lives in a folder, and fix vague link text. Read"},{"id":"sec-creating-a-navigation-menu","title":"Creating a navigation menu","kind":"Read","text":"A site's main menu is a list of links, usually wrapped in <nav>. The same menu goes on every page. On the current page, its own item is often left as plain text, or marked with aria-current=\"page\", so it isn't a link to where you already are: <nav> <ul> <li><a href=\"index.html\">Home</a></li> <li><a href=\"trails.html\" aria-current=\"page\">Trails</a></li> <li><a href=\"about.html\">About</a></li> </ul> </nav> Practice"},{"id":"sec-a-navigation-menu","title":"A navigation menu","kind":"Practice","text":"Build the trail club's menu for its Trails page. Read"},{"id":"sec-email-links","title":"Email links","kind":"Read","text":"A link whose href starts with mailto: opens the visitor's email app with a new message: <a href=\"mailto:hello@trailclub.org\">Email the club</a>. You can fill in more of the message after a ?: subject, cc, bcc and body, joined with &. Spaces and special characters must be encoded: a space becomes %20. Play"},{"id":"sec-build-an-email-link","title":"Build an email link","kind":"Play","text":"Fill in the message. The link is encoded for you. To Subject Cc Body Your link Practice"},{"id":"sec-downloads-and-email","title":"Downloads and email","kind":"Practice","text":"Signpost a download and add an email link with a subject."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"A link is <a href=\"destination\">content</a>. Text, headings and images can all be links. file.html stays in this folder, folder/file.html goes in, ../file.html goes up. Other sites need a full URL: https://…. #id jumps to the element with that id. Link text should make sense on its own, and warn about downloads and new tabs."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"\"Click here\" or \"read more\" as link text. Linking another site without https://, so it's treated as a file in your folder. Paths from your own computer, like file:///Users/… or C:\\…. A capital letter in the path that doesn't match the file name. Opening every link in a new tab. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-creating-links/quiz"],"exercises":["core-creating-links/ex-paths","core-creating-links/ex-nav","core-creating-links/ex-mail"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-publishing","num":"1.8","title":"Publishing Your Website","desc":"Ways to put a site online, the Git words you need, a pre-flight check for broken paths, and publishing with GitHub Pages.","props":["hosting","commit","GitHub Pages","index.html"],"intro":"A site on your laptop is only yours. Compare the ways to put it online, check your files are ready, and publish for free with GitHub Pages, so every assignment from now on is a link you can share.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-are-the-options","title":"What are the options?","kind":"Read","text":"Publishing means copying your site's folder onto a web server, so anyone can request its files. There are three main ways to do it, from most control to least effort. Getting hosting and a domain name For full control, you rent two things. Hosting is space on a web server, paid monthly or yearly. A domain name, like bouldertrailclub.org, is rented yearly from a company called a registrar. You point the domain at the server, and visitors can find you. Publishing to your server To get files onto rented hosting, you usually use an SFTP program, which shows your computer's folder on one side and the server's on the other. You drag files across. It works, but it's easy to forget a file or overwrite the wrong one. Using a web-based IDE such as CodePen Sites like CodePen and Glitch let you write HTML, CSS and JavaScript in the browser and see the result straight away, with a link to share. They're great for experiments, but they're not built for a full multi-page site that you own. Using an online publishing tool Services like GitHub Pages, Netlify and Cloudflare Pages host plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript sites for free, at an address like you.github.io. You can add your own domain later. Play"},{"id":"sec-which-option-fits","title":"Which option fits?","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a situation to see which kind of publishing suits it, and why. Best fit Read"},{"id":"sec-git-and-github-in-brief","title":"Git and GitHub, in brief","kind":"Read","text":"GitHub Pages publishes straight from a GitHub repository, so it helps to know four words: Git is version control: it records snapshots of a project, so you can see what changed and go back if something breaks. A repository (\"repo\") is a project folder that Git tracks. On GitHub, it lives online. A commit is one saved snapshot, with a short message saying what changed: \"Add trail map page\". A push sends your commits from your computer up to GitHub. GitHub Pages republishes the site after each one. You don't need to learn Git's commands yet. GitHub's website and the GitHub Desktop app can do all of this with buttons. Read"},{"id":"sec-get-the-files-ready","title":"Get the files ready","kind":"Read","text":"Most first-publish problems come from paths that only ever worked on your own computer. Before you publish, check that: The home page is called exactly index.html, at the top of the folder. Every file and folder name is lowercase with hyphens, and every path matches it letter for letter. No path starts with file:/// or C:\\: those point into your hard drive. Paths use forward slashes, and don't start with / (that breaks on GitHub Pages project sites). Play"},{"id":"sec-pre-flight-check","title":"Pre-flight check","kind":"Play","text":"List the files in the project and paste a page's HTML. Every path is checked against the files, the way a server would. Files in the project index.html Results Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-paths-before-publishing","title":"Fix the paths before publishing","kind":"Practice","text":"Use what the pre-flight check taught you. Play"},{"id":"sec-what-approach-would-we-recommend-github-pages","title":"What approach would we recommend? GitHub Pages","kind":"Play","text":"For this course, publish with GitHub Pages: it's free, it handles HTTPS for you, and it republishes every time you push. Make a GitHub accountPick your username carefully: it becomes part of your site's address. Create a new repositoryClick New, give it a short lowercase name like trail-site, set it to Public, and create it. Add your filesClick “Add file”, then “Upload files”. Drag in the contents of your project folder, so index.html sits at the top level. Write a commit message and click Commit changes. Turn on PagesOpen Settings, then Pages. Under “Build and deployment”, choose “Deploy from a branch”, pick main and / (root), and click Save. Wait, then visitIt takes a minute or two. Refresh the Pages settings to see your link. Each time you commit a change, give it another minute. Where will my site be? GitHub username Repository name If it doesn't work I get a 404 page Check there's a file named exactly index.html, all lowercase, at the top of the repository, not inside another folder. Then wait a couple of minutes and refresh. The page shows, but with no styles or images A path is wrong. Open DevTools, look at the Console and Network tabs for 404s, and compare each path with the file names in the repository, capital letters included. My changes don't show up Wait a minute after committing, then do a hard refresh (Ctrl/Cmd + Shift + R) to skip the browser's cache."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Publishing copies your folder onto a web server that anyone can reach. Options: rented hosting plus a domain, an online code editor, or a free publishing service. GitHub Pages publishes a repository at username.github.io/repository/. A commit is a saved snapshot; pushing sends commits to GitHub, which republishes the site. Check paths before publishing: lowercase, relative, forward slashes, nothing from your own disk."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Uploading the project folder instead of its contents, so index.html ends up a level too deep. Paths starting with file:///, C:\\ or /. Capital letters that worked on a Mac or Windows laptop but not on the server. Expecting changes to appear instantly. Give it a minute, then hard-refresh. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-publishing/quiz"],"exercises":["core-publishing/ex-publish"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-debugging-html","num":"1.9","title":"Debugging HTML","desc":"Syntax and logic errors, how browsers repair broken HTML, the DOM inspector, and reading a validator's messages.","props":["DOM inspector","validator","nesting","stray end tag"],"intro":"Browsers quietly repair broken HTML, so mistakes hide. Learn the kinds of errors HTML has, see what the browser does with them in the DOM inspector, and use a validator to find every one.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-debugging-isn-t-scary","title":"Debugging isn't scary","kind":"Read","text":"Everyone's code breaks, all the time. Debugging, finding and fixing what's wrong, is a normal part of the job, not a sign you've failed. Like any skill, it gets easier once you know your tools. Programming languages that need compiling stop and shout at you when something's wrong. HTML is different, and that makes it both easier and sneakier. Read"},{"id":"sec-html-and-debugging","title":"HTML and debugging","kind":"Read","text":"There are two kinds of error. A syntax error is a typo in the language itself: a missing closing tag, a missing quote. A logic error is valid code that does the wrong thing: an <h3> where you meant <h2>, a link to the wrong page. HTML is permissive. When a browser meets a syntax error, it doesn't stop: it guesses what you meant and repairs it, following rules every browser shares. That's why broken pages often look fine. But the repair isn't always what you meant, different repairs can confuse screen readers, and a page that only works by luck will break the moment you add CSS or JavaScript. <p>This is <strong>badly <em>nested</strong> text.</em></p> Read"},{"id":"sec-using-the-dom-inspector","title":"Using the DOM inspector","kind":"Read","text":"The browser turns your HTML into a tree of elements called the DOM (Document Object Model). The DOM inspector in the browser's DevTools shows that tree: not what you wrote, but what the browser built. Comparing the two shows you exactly where it had to guess. To open it, right-click anything on a page and choose Inspect. The panel is called Elements in Chrome, Edge and Safari, and Inspector in Firefox. Play"},{"id":"sec-your-turn-study-html-in-the-dom-inspector","title":"Your turn: study HTML in the DOM inspector","kind":"Play","text":"This is MDN's debugging example, trail edition. Read the source, then look at the tree the browser built. Click an example to see one mistake at a time. The whole example Unclosed <li> Unclosed <strong> Bad nesting Missing quote Source ElementsConsoleNetwork What visitors see Read"},{"id":"sec-html-validation","title":"HTML validation","kind":"Read","text":"Reading the DOM tree works for small pages. For a whole page, use a validator: a program that checks your HTML against the rules and lists every problem. The standard one is the W3C Markup Validation Service at validator.w3.org. You can give it a URL, upload a file, or paste your code into Validate by Direct Input. Interpreting the error messages Validator messages are terse, but each gives a line number and points at the code. The common ones: Unclosed element strong: an opening tag with no closing tag. End tag strong violates nesting rules: tags closed in the wrong order. Stray end tag em: a closing tag with nothing open to close. End of file reached when inside an attribute value. Ignoring tag: a missing closing quote. An img element must have an alt attribute. Tip Fix the first error, then validate again. One missing quote or tag can cause a dozen errors after it, and they all disappear together. Play"},{"id":"sec-validating-an-html-document","title":"Validating an HTML document","kind":"Play","text":"A small validator that checks the most common mistakes, with messages worded like the W3C validator's. Edit the code until it's clean. Your HTML Messages This checks a handful of common errors. Before you publish, run your real pages through validator.w3.org. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-trail-report","title":"Fix the trail report","kind":"Practice","text":"The browser repairs this page well enough to read. Fix it so it doesn't have to. Practice"},{"id":"sec-act-on-the-validator-s-messages","title":"Act on the validator's messages","kind":"Practice","text":"These are the messages the validator gave for this snippet. Fix each one."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Syntax errors break the language's rules; logic errors are valid code that does the wrong thing. Browsers repair broken HTML instead of stopping, so errors hide. The DOM inspector shows what the browser built, not what you wrote. A validator lists every syntax error, with line numbers. Fix the first error, then validate again."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Assuming the page is fine because it looks fine. Fixing errors from the bottom of the list up. Later errors are often caused by earlier ones. Editing the DOM inspector and forgetting the change isn't saved to your file. Missing a closing quote, which can swallow a whole tag or link. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-debugging-html/quiz"],"exercises":["core-debugging-html/ex-debug","core-debugging-html/ex-messages"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-structuring-documents","num":"2.1","title":"Structuring Documents","desc":"Header, nav, main, article, section, aside and footer: say what each part of the page is.","props":["<header>","<nav>","<main>","<article>"],"intro":"Most pages share the same parts: a header, navigation, main content, a sidebar and a footer. HTML has an element for each. Use them, and screen reader users can jump straight to the part they want.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-basic-sections-of-a-document","title":"Basic sections of a document","kind":"Read","text":"Look at almost any website and you'll find the same parts: Header: a strip across the top with the site's name or logo. It stays the same on every page. Navigation bar: links to the site's main sections, usually in or below the header. Main content: the big area in the middle that's different on every page. Sidebar: extra information beside the main content: related links, ads, an author bio. Footer: a strip across the bottom with small print, contact details and copyright. Read"},{"id":"sec-html-for-structuring-content","title":"HTML for structuring content","kind":"Read","text":"Each part has an element that says what it is: <header> <h1>Boulder Trail Club</h1> </header> <nav>…links…</nav> <main> <article> <h2>Royal Arch Trail</h2> <section><h3>Getting there</h3>…</section> <section><h3>What to bring</h3>…</section> </article> <aside><h2>Nearby trails</h2>…</aside> </main> <footer>© 2026 Boulder Trail Club</footer> These elements don't change how the page looks: without CSS, they stack like plain blocks. What they change is what the page means. Screen readers turn them into landmarks that users can jump between, and they make your HTML easier to read and to style. Play"},{"id":"sec-semantic-html-or-div-soup","title":"Semantic HTML or div soup?","kind":"Play","text":"Switch between the two versions of the same page, then click a region to see what it means and what a screen reader announces. Semantic HTML “Div soup” Read"},{"id":"sec-html-layout-elements-in-more-detail","title":"HTML layout elements in more detail","kind":"Read","text":"<main>: the content unique to this page. Use it once, directly inside <body>, not inside other parts. <article>: a complete piece that would make sense on its own, like a blog post, a trail write-up or a product card. <section>: a part of a page or article that groups related content, usually with its own heading. <aside>: content related to the main content but not part of it: a sidebar, a glossary box, related links. <header>: introductory content. Directly in <body> it's the site header; inside an <article> it's that article's header. <nav>: a group of main navigation links. Not every group of links needs one. <footer>: closing content for the page, or for the article it's in. Non-semantic wrappers Sometimes you need a box to style or to group things, and no element fits. That's what <div> (a block) and <span> (inline, inside text) are for. They mean nothing, so they're safe. Just reach for a semantic element first, and only use a <div> when none fits: a page built only of divs is \"div soup\". Line breaks and horizontal rules <br> breaks a line where the line break is part of the content, like an address or a poem. Don't use it to add space. <hr> marks a thematic break, a change of topic between paragraphs. Browsers draw it as a line, but its job is meaning, not decoration. Play"},{"id":"sec-which-landmarks-would-a-screen-reader-find","title":"Which landmarks would a screen reader find?","kind":"Play","text":"Screen readers list a page's landmarks so users can jump between them. Edit the HTML and watch the list. Semantic Div soup Your HTML Landmarks Practice"},{"id":"sec-replace-the-divs","title":"Replace the divs","kind":"Practice","text":"This trail page is all divs. Give each part the element that says what it is. Read"},{"id":"sec-structuring-a-basic-website","title":"Structuring a basic website","kind":"Read","text":"Before you write a site with several pages, plan it on paper: List what every page shares: the header, the navigation menu and the footer. Sketch the layout of a typical page: where the main content, sidebar and footer go. List everything the site needs to say, then group it into pages. Draw a sitemap: a diagram of the pages and how they link. Home TrailsEventsAboutContact Mesa TrailRoyal ArchBear Peak Practice"},{"id":"sec-breaks-that-belong","title":"Breaks that belong","kind":"Practice","text":"Use <br> and <hr> for meaning, and remove the ones used for spacing."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Pages share parts: header, nav, main content, sidebar, footer. Each has an element, and screen readers turn them into landmarks. One <main> per page, directly in the body. <article> stands on its own; <section> groups part of something. <div> and <span> are for when nothing else fits. <br> for line breaks that are content; <hr> for a change of topic."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Building everything from divs with class names like header and nav. More than one <main>, or a <main> inside the header or an article. Using <section> as a styling box with no heading. A div fits better. Stacks of <br>s or an <hr> just to add space or a line. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-structuring-documents/quiz"],"exercises":["core-structuring-documents/ex-soup","core-structuring-documents/ex-breaks"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-images","num":"2.2","title":"HTML Images","desc":"src paths, alt text, width and height, captions, and where images may come from.","props":["<img>","alt","<figure>","<figcaption>"],"intro":"One empty element, <img>, puts a picture on the page. Getting it right means a path that works, a size the browser knows in advance, and alt text for the people who can't see it.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-how-do-we-put-an-image-on-a-webpage","title":"How do we put an image on a webpage?","kind":"Read","text":"<img> is an empty element: no closing tag, and everything goes in attributes. The one it can't do without is src, the path to the image file: <img src=\"images/royal-arch.jpg\" alt=\"Hikers under a sandstone arch\"> src works like a link's href. A relative path starts from the folder the current page is in, ../ goes up one folder, and file names must match exactly: on most web servers Arch.JPG and arch.jpg are different files. You can also point at an absolute URL, but only use images you have permission to use, and don't \"hotlink\" to files on someone else's server. Play"},{"id":"sec-does-it-load","title":"Does it load?","kind":"Play","text":"You're editing trails/royal-arch.html. Write a src that reaches the photo in the images folder. 📁 site/ ├ index.html ├ 📁 images/ │ └ royal-arch.jpg └ 📁 trails/ └ royal-arch.html ← you are here images/royal-arch.jpg ../images/Royal-Arch.jpg ../images/royal-arch.jpg /images/royal-arch.jpg src= Read"},{"id":"sec-alternative-text","title":"Alternative text","kind":"Read","text":"The alt attribute holds a text version of the image. It's what a screen reader reads aloud, what search engines index, and what appears when the image fails to load. Good alt text describes what the image does on this page, not every detail. Ask: if I read this page aloud to a friend, what would I say about this picture? Content: a photo in a trail report. Describe what matters to the report. Link: a logo linking home. Describe where the link goes: \"Boulder Trail Club home\". Text in an image: put the same words in the alt. (Better still, use real text.) Decoration: a flourish or divider. Use an empty alt=\"\" so screen readers skip it. Don't leave alt out, or many screen readers read the file name instead. Tip Don't start alt text with \"Image of\". Screen readers already announce that it's an image. Play"},{"id":"sec-write-alt-text-for-an-article","title":"Write alt text for an article","kind":"Play","text":"Choose alt text for each image, then switch views to hear it, or to see what's left when the images fail to load. Choose alt text What you see What a screen reader says Image failed to load Read"},{"id":"sec-width-and-height","title":"Width and height","kind":"Read","text":"Set width and height to the image's real size in pixels, with no units: <img src=\"images/longs-peak.jpg\" alt=\"…\" width=\"640\" height=\"300\"> The browser draws the page before the image arrives. Without the dimensions it reserves no space, then shoves everything below down when the file lands, often just as you go to click something. With them, it holds an empty box of the right shape. CSS can still resize the image, and it keeps that shape. Don't use the attributes to shrink a huge photo, though: the browser still downloads every byte. Resize the file itself. Play"},{"id":"sec-watch-the-page-jump","title":"Watch the page jump","kind":"Play","text":"Load the image slowly, with and without its dimensions, and watch the paragraph underneath. width=\"640\" height=\"300\" Load the image slowly Read"},{"id":"sec-image-titles","title":"Image titles","kind":"Read","text":"Like links, images can take a title attribute, which shows as a tooltip when you hover. It's not a good place for information: touch screens and keyboards can't hover, and many screen readers ignore it. If something matters, put it in the alt text or a visible caption. Read"},{"id":"sec-annotating-images-with-figures-and-figure-captions","title":"Annotating images with figures and figure captions","kind":"Read","text":"When an image needs a visible caption, wrap the image and caption in <figure> and put the caption in <figcaption>. That ties them together, for screen readers and for CSS. A figure doesn't have to hold an image: a code listing, a chart, a quote or a video can be a figure too. Alt text and caption do different jobs. The alt replaces the image for people who can't see it. The caption is for everyone and adds information, like who took the photo. Don't repeat the same words in both, or a screen reader reads them twice. Play"},{"id":"sec-paragraph-or-figcaption","title":"Paragraph or figcaption?","kind":"Play","text":"The same image and caption, marked up two ways. img + p figure + figcaption The markup What a screen reader says Practice"},{"id":"sec-write-useful-alt-text","title":"Write useful alt text","kind":"Practice","text":"Each image needs alt text that fits its job on the page, and the photo needs a visible caption. Read"},{"id":"sec-css-background-images","title":"CSS background images","kind":"Read","text":"CSS can also put images on a page, with background-image. Those images are pure decoration: they have no alt text, screen readers don't know they exist, and they don't print by default. So the rule is simple: if the image means something in your content, use <img>. If it's only decoration, a background image is fine. You'll use them in Week 4. Read"},{"id":"sec-image-licensing","title":"Image licensing","kind":"Read","text":"Finding an image online doesn't mean you can use it. Images on your site should be: Yours: you made it, or you own the rights. Permission granted: the owner said yes, ideally in writing. Openly licensed: for example under a Creative Commons licence or in the public domain. Follow the licence's terms, which often means crediting the creator. Search engines and sites like Wikimedia Commons, Unsplash and Openverse let you filter by licence. When in doubt, take your own photo. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-trail-gallery","title":"Fix the trail gallery","kind":"Practice","text":"None of these images load, and the ones that would load would make the page jump."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"<img> is empty: src says where the file is, and paths work like links. File names are case-sensitive on most servers. Alt text describes what the image does on the page. Decoration gets alt=\"\". width and height stop the page jumping while images load. <figure> and <figcaption> tie a caption to its image. Meaningful images go in HTML; decorative ones can go in CSS."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"A path relative to the wrong folder, or Photo.JPG for photo.jpg. Missing alt, so screen readers read IMG_4031.jpg aloud. Alt text that starts with \"Image of\", or repeats the caption word for word. Putting important information in title. Using any image you found on a search engine. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-images/quiz"],"exercises":["core-images/ex-alt","core-images/ex-gallery"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-image-formats","num":"2.3","title":"Image File Formats","desc":"JPEG, PNG, WebP, AVIF, SVG and GIF: which to use when, and how to offer a fallback.","props":["WebP","SVG","<picture>","lossy"],"intro":"The same picture can be a 30 KB file or a 900 KB file depending on its format. Photos, logos and screenshots each have a format that suits them best.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-common-image-file-types","title":"Common image file types","kind":"Read","text":"The same photo can be a 60 KB file or a 6 MB file depending on how you save it, and that difference decides whether your page loads in a blink or makes a visitor on a phone wait. Four formats cover nearly everything: JPG for photos. It's lossy: it throws away detail your eye won't miss, which makes files small. Save it too low and you get smudgy blocks. PNG for screenshots and images with sharp edges or see-through backgrounds. It's lossless, so nothing is thrown away, but photos saved as PNG are huge. SVG for logos and icons. It isn't pixels at all, but shapes described in code, so it stays sharp at any size and is often just a few KB. WebP does the jobs of both JPG and PNG, usually in a smaller file. Every modern browser supports it. The format is only half of it. A photo straight off a phone is often 4000 pixels wide. If it shows 800 pixels wide on your page, you're making every visitor download five times more than they can see. Before uploading: Resize to about twice the width it appears on screen, so it stays sharp on high-density screens. Compress it. A free tool like Squoosh lets you compare quality settings side by side. Aim for under 200 KB for a large photo, and a few KB for icons and logos. Watch out Renaming photo.png to photo.jpg changes the name, not the format. To change format, open the image and export it again. Play"},{"id":"sec-same-image-different-files","title":"Same image, different files","kind":"Play","text":"Your browser is really encoding these images right now, so the file sizes are real. Pick a kind of image, then compare the formats. The zoom shows the damage compression does. kind of image photo logo screenshot quality (JPG and WebP) PNG is lossless: it keeps every pixel exactly. JPG and WebP are lossy: they throw away detail you probably won't notice, and lower quality throws away more. file size · 400 × 250 px Full image Zoomed in 4× Cheat sheet"},{"id":"sec-image-file-type-details","title":"Image file type details","kind":"Cheat sheet","text":"JPG .jpg · lossy Use for photos. Avoid for logos, text and anything needing a transparent background. JPG has no transparency. PNG .png · lossless Use for screenshots, flat graphics with text, and images with transparent backgrounds. Avoid for photos. They come out 5–10× bigger than a JPG. WebP .webp · either Use for almost anything. Usually 25–35% smaller than JPG or PNG at the same quality, and supports transparency. Note Every current browser supports it. Some older desktop apps can't open it. AVIF .avif · either Use for photos when you want the smallest file. Often half the size of a JPG. Note Slower to export, and not every editor can save it yet. Offer a JPG fallback with <picture>. SVG .svg · vector Use for logos, icons and simple illustrations. It's math, not pixels, so it stays sharp at any size and is often tiny. Avoid for photos. They can't be drawn as shapes. GIF .gif · old Use for almost nothing now. Only 256 colors. Instead for an animation, use a short muted MP4 in a <video>. It's often 10× smaller. Read"},{"id":"sec-choosing-an-image-format","title":"Choosing an image format","kind":"Read","text":"Start from what's in the image, not from habit: Photographs: lossy compression hides well in all that natural detail. Use WebP or AVIF, with JPEG as the safe fallback. Icons and logos: flat shapes with sharp edges. SVG if you have a vector version; otherwise PNG or lossless WebP. Screenshots: text and crisp lines that lossy formats smudge. PNG or lossless WebP. Diagrams and drawings: SVG wherever possible, so they stay sharp at any size. Animations: animated WebP or a short muted video, not GIF. Practice"},{"id":"sec-pick-the-right-format","title":"Pick the right format","kind":"Practice","text":"Each image has been exported in the wrong format. Pick the one that suits what's in it. Read"},{"id":"sec-providing-image-fallbacks","title":"Providing image fallbacks","kind":"Read","text":"Newer formats like AVIF give the smallest files, but you may want a safety net for an older browser. The picture element lets you offer several versions and lets the browser choose: <picture> <source srcset=\"arch.avif\" type=\"image/avif\"> <source srcset=\"arch.webp\" type=\"image/webp\"> <img src=\"arch.jpg\" alt=\"Hikers under a sandstone arch\" width=\"800\" height=\"600\"> </picture> The browser reads the <source> elements in order and uses the first whose type it supports. If none fit, it uses the <img>. The <img> is required, and it's where the alt text, width and height live, for every version. Practice"},{"id":"sec-add-a-modern-format-with-a-fallback","title":"Add a modern format with a fallback","kind":"Practice","text":"Offer the AVIF and WebP versions of this photo, keeping the JPEG as the fallback."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Resize before you upload. Export about twice the width the image appears on screen, so it stays sharp on high-density screens. A photo shown 600px wide needs about 1200px, not the 4000px from your phone. Then compress it. Free tools like Squoosh (squoosh.app) and ImageOptim let you compare quality settings side by side. Quality 70–80 is usually indistinguishable from 100. A good target for a large photo is under 200 KB. Most icons and logos should be a few KB. <picture> with typed <source>s offers modern formats with an <img> fallback. An SVG can go in an <img> like any other image, or be pasted into the HTML directly so CSS can recolor it."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Saving a logo as JPG, which leaves blurry smudges around the letters and a white box instead of transparency. Saving a photo as PNG \"for quality,\" and getting a file several times bigger with no visible difference. Renaming photo.png to photo.jpg. That changes the name, not the format. Re-export it instead. Re-saving the same JPG over and over. Each save loses a little more detail. Keep an original and export copies. Putting the <source>s after the <img> in a <picture>, or leaving the <img> out. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-pick-the-format","title":"Pick the format","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-image-formats/quiz"],"exercises":["core-image-formats/ex-formats","core-image-formats/ex-picture"],"questions":0},{"slug":"core-table-basics","num":"2.4","title":"Table Basics","desc":"Rows, cells and headers; spanning cells; styling columns; and when not to use a table.","props":["<table>","<tr>","<td>","colspan"],"intro":"A table is for data with rows and columns that both mean something: a schedule, a price comparison, a set of results. HTML builds one row at a time, cell by cell.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-is-a-table","title":"What is a table?","kind":"Read","text":"A table is a grid of rows and columns. You read it by looking along a row and down a column and finding where they cross: the 10:00 slot on Saturday, the price of the middle plan. Everyone already knows how to read one, which is what makes tables so useful. Without CSS, an HTML table is hard to read: no borders, no spacing, no stripes. You'll style tables properly in Week 4; this lesson uses a little CSS so you can see the structure. When should you NOT use tables? Before CSS layout existed, whole pages were built from tables. Don't do this. Layout tables are hard to change, they bloat the HTML, they don't adapt to small screens, and screen readers announce them as data, which confuses everyone. Use tables for data, and CSS (Flexbox and Grid, Weeks 5 and 6) for layout. Practice"},{"id":"sec-table-or-not","title":"Table or not?","kind":"Practice","text":"Decide for each one. Read"},{"id":"sec-creating-your-first-table","title":"Creating your first table","kind":"Read","text":"Three elements make a basic table: <table> wraps the whole thing. <tr> (\"table row\") holds one row. <td> (\"table data\") holds one cell. Cells go across, in order, inside their row. <table> <tr><td>Mesa</td><td>6.7</td></tr> <tr><td>Royal Arch</td><td>3.4</td></tr> </table> There's no element for a column. Columns form because every row has the same number of cells, so the first cell of every row lines up, then the second, and so on. Give one row too few cells and the table has a hole. Play"},{"id":"sec-build-it-row-by-row","title":"Build it row by row","kind":"Play","text":"Edit the table. The map shows how many columns each row fills. Two columns A short row With spans Your HTML Column map Read"},{"id":"sec-adding-headers-with-th","title":"Adding headers with <th>","kind":"Read","text":"The cells that label a row or a column are headers. Make them <th> (\"table header\") instead of <td>: <tr><th>Trail</th><th>Miles</th></tr> <tr><td>Mesa</td><td>6.7</td></tr> Browsers show <th> bold and centred, but that's not the point. Headers tell the browser, and screen readers, which cells are labels and which are data. That's how a screen reader can say \"Mesa, Miles: 6.7\" instead of just \"6.7\". The next lesson goes deeper. A <caption> right after <table> gives the table a title. Play"},{"id":"sec-build-a-table-one-feature-at-a-time","title":"Build a table one feature at a time","kind":"Play","text":"Turn on each feature, and click any cell to hear what a screen reader says about it. HTML Header cells use <th scope> Add a <caption> CSS border-collapse (merge double borders) padding in cells Zebra stripes with :nth-child(even) Right-align numbers Turn everything on Click a cell in the table. Read"},{"id":"sec-allowing-cells-to-span-multiple-rows-and-columns","title":"Allowing cells to span multiple rows and columns","kind":"Read","text":"Sometimes a cell needs to stretch. colspan=\"2\" makes a cell two columns wide; rowspan=\"2\" makes it two rows tall. <tr><th>12:00</th><td colspan=\"2\">Closed for trail work</td></tr> Spanning cells take up space in other rows, so those rows need fewer cells. With rowspan=\"2\", the row below must leave out one cell, or everything after it shifts one column to the right. Check that every row still adds up. Read"},{"id":"sec-providing-common-styling-to-columns","title":"Providing common styling to columns","kind":"Read","text":"Since there's no column element, how do you style a whole column? You could add a class to every cell in it. The cleaner way is <colgroup>, right after the caption, with one <col> for each column: <colgroup> <col> <col> <col class=\"today\"> </colgroup> A <col span=\"2\"> covers two columns at once. Only a few CSS properties work on columns: background, border, width and visibility. Play"},{"id":"sec-cells-that-span-and-column-groups","title":"Cells that span, and column groups","kind":"Play","text":"Turn each feature on to see the problem it solves. rowspan=\"2\" on the long hike colspan=\"2\" on the closure Add a <tfoot> <colgroup> to highlight Sunday Practice"},{"id":"sec-build-the-weekend-schedule","title":"Build the weekend schedule","kind":"Practice","text":"The data is all here, but nothing is merged, so cells repeat."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Tables are for data with meaningful rows and columns. Never for page layout. <table> holds <tr> rows; rows hold <td> data cells and <th> header cells. Columns exist only because every row has the same number of cells. colspan and rowspan merge cells; the rows they cover need fewer cells. <colgroup> and <col> style whole columns."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Using a table to lay out a page or a grid of cards. Bold <td>s instead of real <th>s. Rows with different numbers of cells, so columns shift out of line. Adding rowspan but forgetting to remove a cell from the row below. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-table-basics/table-or-not","core-table-basics/quiz"],"exercises":["core-table-basics/ex-sched"],"questions":10},{"slug":"core-table-accessibility","num":"2.5","title":"Accessible Tables","desc":"Captions, scope, headers and id, and row groups, so every cell makes sense out loud.","props":["<caption>","scope","headers","<thead>"],"intro":"A sighted reader finds a cell's meaning by glancing up to its column and across to its row. A screen reader user can't glance. Your markup has to tell their software which headers go with each cell.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-recap-of-tables-for-visually-impaired-users","title":"Recap of tables for visually impaired users","kind":"Read","text":"Look at any table and you take in its shape at once: what the columns are, where the totals are, which row you care about. A screen reader user hears a table one cell at a time, moving with keyboard shortcuts. Hearing \"1,400\" on its own is useless: 1,400 what, for which trail? Screen readers solve this by announcing the matching headers as you move: go across a row and they say the column header; go down a column and they say the row header. But they can only do that if the HTML says which cells are headers. Play"},{"id":"sec-move-through-a-table-without-looking","title":"Move through a table without looking","kind":"Play","text":"Use the arrows to move like a screen reader user would. Then turn on the header markup and do it again. Headers are <th scope> Add a <caption> Move ↑ ← ↓ → Read"},{"id":"sec-using-column-and-row-headers","title":"Using column and row headers","kind":"Read","text":"The first step is the one from the last lesson: header cells are <th>, not bold <td>s. Labels at the top of each column are column headers. The first cell of each row is often a label too, like the trail name, and that makes it a row header. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-scope-attribute","title":"The scope attribute","kind":"Read","text":"scope tells assistive technology which direction a header points: <tr> <th scope=\"col\">Trail</th> <th scope=\"col\">Miles</th> </tr> <tr> <th scope=\"row\">Mesa</th> <td>6.7</td> </tr> scope=\"col\" labels the cells below it; scope=\"row\" labels the cells beside it. For headers that sit over a group of columns or rows, there's scope=\"colgroup\" and scope=\"rowgroup\". Read"},{"id":"sec-the-id-and-headers-attributes","title":"The id and headers attributes","kind":"Read","text":"When a table has two levels of headers, scope may not be enough. The precise alternative: give every header an id, then list, on each data cell, the ids of all the headers that apply to it, separated by spaces: <th id=\"weekend\" colspan=\"2\">Weekend</th> <th id=\"sat\">Sat</th> <th id=\"mesa\">Mesa</th> <td headers=\"mesa weekend sat\">48</td> It's a lot of typing, and easy to get wrong. Prefer simpler tables where you can: often a complicated table reads better split in two. Play"},{"id":"sec-which-headers-label-this-cell","title":"Which headers label this cell?","kind":"Play","text":"Click any number. The headers its headers attribute points to light up. Read"},{"id":"sec-adding-a-caption-with-caption","title":"Adding a caption with <caption>","kind":"Read","text":"A <caption>, directly after the opening <table> tag, is the table's title. Screen readers announce it when a user enters the table, so they can decide whether to explore it or skip on. Keep it short: \"Boulder trail visits by day\", not a paragraph. Read"},{"id":"sec-adding-structure-with-thead-tbody-and-tfoot","title":"Adding structure with <thead>, <tbody> and <tfoot>","kind":"Read","text":"Larger tables benefit from grouping their rows: <thead> wraps the header row(s). <tbody> wraps the data. Browsers add one for you if you leave it out. <tfoot> wraps a summary row, like totals. They don't change how the table looks, but they make it easier to style (Week 4), they let browsers repeat the header on every printed page, and they spell out the table's structure for anyone reading your code. Practice"},{"id":"sec-structure-a-table","title":"Structure a table","kind":"Practice","text":"This table has the right data but no structure, so screen readers can't tell headers from data. Practice"},{"id":"sec-row-headers-and-a-total","title":"Row headers and a total","kind":"Practice","text":"Finish the trail-visits table so every number has both of its labels. Read"},{"id":"sec-nesting-tables","title":"Nesting tables","kind":"Read","text":"You can put a table inside a table cell. Don't, unless there's truly no other way. Screen readers struggle to explain where you are in a table inside a table, and the markup gets hard to follow. Two tables side by side, or one table with a clearer set of columns, nearly always works better."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Screen reader users hear a table one cell at a time; headers give each cell its meaning. Use <th> for every header, with scope=\"col\" or scope=\"row\". For complex tables, id on headers and headers on data cells link them exactly. A short <caption> lets users decide whether to read the table. <thead>, <tbody> and <tfoot> spell out the structure."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Styling header cells to look like headers without making them <th>. Forgetting the row headers: the first cell of each row is usually a label. A headers attribute that lists an id nobody has, or misspells one. Putting the table's title in a heading or paragraph above it instead of a caption. Nesting tables. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-table-accessibility/quiz"],"exercises":["core-table-accessibility/ex-table","core-table-accessibility/ex-rows"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-forms","num":"2.6","title":"Forms and Buttons","desc":"Button types, the anatomy of a form, labelled controls, and client-side validation.","props":["<button>","<form>","<label>","<input>"],"intro":"Links and media only go one way. Buttons and forms let visitors talk back: sign up, search, send a message. HTML does a surprising amount of the work, if you choose the right elements.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-interacting-with-users","title":"Interacting with users","kind":"Read","text":"So far your pages talk and visitors listen. To let them answer, you need two kinds of element: buttons, which do something when pressed, and forms, which collect information and send it somewhere. Both come with keyboard support and screen reader support built in, as long as you use the real elements. Read"},{"id":"sec-buttons","title":"Buttons","kind":"Read","text":"A button is made with <button>, and its text goes between the tags: <button type=\"button\">Show the map</button> On its own, a button with type=\"button\" does nothing when clicked. JavaScript gives it a job, which you'll do in Week 7. Inside a form, though, buttons already have jobs, chosen by type: type=\"submit\" sends the form. It's the default inside a form, so say it anyway to be clear. type=\"reset\" clears every field back to its starting value. Rarely a good idea: one mis-click wipes everything. type=\"button\" does nothing until JavaScript says so. Links go somewhere; buttons do something. If clicking it changes the page or the URL, it's a link. If it sends, opens, plays or toggles, it's a button. Play"},{"id":"sec-what-does-each-button-do","title":"What does each button do?","kind":"Play","text":"Type something, then press each button. Email type=\"submit\" type=\"reset\" type=\"button\" What happened Press a button. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-anatomy-of-a-form","title":"The anatomy of a form","kind":"Read","text":"A basic form has three parts: the <form> wrapper, one or more label-and-control pairs, and a submit button. <form action=\"/subscribe\" method=\"post\"> <label for=\"email\">Email</label> <input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" name=\"email\" required> <button type=\"submit\">Subscribe</button> </form> The <form> element When the form is sent, each field travels as name=value. The form's action says where to send it, and method says how: get puts it in the URL, post sends it out of sight. A field without a name isn't sent at all. Play"},{"id":"sec-where-does-the-data-go","title":"Where does the data go?","kind":"Play","text":"Fill this in and watch the request it would make. Nothing is actually sent from this page. method=\"get\" method=\"post\" Trail name=\"trail\" Mesa Trail Royal Arch Date name=\"date\" Group size name=\"size\" Notes (no name attribute) The request <input> elements <input> is an empty element that becomes many different controls depending on its type. type=\"email\" gives phones a keyboard with an @ key and checks the address before sending. type=\"date\" brings up a date picker. Use type=\"text\" for everything and you get none of that. Give each input an id (for its label), a name (for the data) and, if it can't be left empty, required. Play"},{"id":"sec-try-each-input-type","title":"Try each input type","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a type and see the keyboard, picker and validation you get for free. Pick an input type What you get for free <label> elements Every control needs a label. Connect them explicitly, by matching the label's for to the input's id, or implicitly, by putting the input inside the label: <label for=\"name\">Name</label> <input id=\"name\" name=\"name\"> <label><input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"news\"> Email me updates</label> Clicking the label focuses the control, which gives everyone a bigger target, and a screen reader reads it aloud when someone reaches the field. A placeholder is not a label: it disappears as soon as you type. Play"},{"id":"sec-click-each-label","title":"Click each label","kind":"Play","text":"Click the label text, not the control, in each version. Connected Your name Email me updates Click the label text, not the box. Not connected Your name Email me updates Click the label text, not the box. Read"},{"id":"sec-an-aside-on-accessibility","title":"An aside on accessibility","kind":"Read","text":"You could make a <div> look exactly like a button with CSS. It would still fail: you can't reach it with the Tab key, Enter and Space don't press it, screen readers don't call it a button, and it can't submit a form. Try tabbing to both of these: A real button A div “button” Native form elements come with all that behaviour for free. Use them. Read"},{"id":"sec-other-control-types","title":"Other control types","kind":"Read","text":"Radio buttons (type=\"radio\") are for picking exactly one option. All the radios in a group share the same name; each has its own value. Add checked to pre-select one, and disabled to make one unavailable. Checkboxes (type=\"checkbox\") are for zero or more options. Each one is independent and has its own name. Drop-down menus are a <select> holding <option>s. They save space when there are many choices. Multi-line text goes in a <textarea>, which has a closing tag. rows and cols set its starting size. A group of radios or checkboxes needs a question. Wrap the group in a <fieldset> and put the question in its <legend>. Play"},{"id":"sec-fieldset-and-legend","title":"fieldset and legend","kind":"Play","text":"Focus a radio button in each version to hear the difference. With fieldset and legend Trail difficulty Easy Moderate Hard Focus a radio button. Without Trail difficulty Easy Moderate Hard Focus a radio button. Practice"},{"id":"sec-make-the-form-usable","title":"Make the form usable","kind":"Practice","text":"This sign-up form works with a mouse, barely. Label it, name it, and group it so it works for everyone. Read"},{"id":"sec-form-validation","title":"Form validation","kind":"Read","text":"Attributes like required, minlength, pattern, min and max, and types like email, make the browser check answers before sending and explain what's wrong. That's client-side validation, and it helps visitors fix mistakes quickly. Note Client-side validation doesn't protect a server: anyone can switch it off or send data without your form. Real sites always check again on the server (server-side validation). You need both. Play"},{"id":"sec-let-the-browser-check-it","title":"Let the browser check it","kind":"Play","text":"Try submitting this with empty or wrong values. Name * Email * Age (13 to 120) Sign up Play"},{"id":"sec-set-the-rules-let-the-browser-explain-them","title":"Set the rules, let the browser explain them","kind":"Play","text":"Type in each field. The messages come straight from your browser. Username required minlength=\"3\" maxlength=\"15\" ZIP code pattern=\"[0-9]{5}\" Group size type=\"number\" min=\"1\" max=\"12\" Practice"},{"id":"sec-trip-feedback-form","title":"Trip feedback form","kind":"Practice","text":"Add the remaining controls with the right elements."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Every input needs a label. The label's for must match the input's id exactly. The name attribute is what the data is called when the form is sent. Radio buttons in the same group share one name, so only one can be picked. Wrap the group in a <fieldset> with a <legend>. Use <button type=\"submit\"> to send a form and type=\"button\" for anything else."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Using placeholder instead of a label. It disappears when you type and is often low-contrast. Typing type=\"text\" for everything and missing the free validation and keyboards. Reusing the same id twice, so a label points at the wrong input. Forgetting that browser validation doesn't protect a server. Real apps check on the server too. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-forms/quiz"],"exercises":["core-forms/ex-controls","core-forms/ex-form"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-video-audio","num":"2.7","title":"Video and Audio","desc":"The video and audio elements, multiple sources, playback attributes and captions.","props":["<video>","<audio>","<source>","<track>"],"intro":"The <video> and <audio> elements play media without any plugins. Their attributes decide what visitors see, what plays by itself, and which file the browser picks.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-video-and-audio-on-the-web","title":"Video and audio on the web","kind":"Read","text":"Early websites needed plugins like Flash to play video, and they were slow, insecure and didn't work on phones. Today the browser plays media itself, through two elements that work almost identically: <video> and <audio>. Video files are big. For long videos, hosting on YouTube or Vimeo and embedding them is often easier. For short clips, backgrounds and audio you own, these elements are the way. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-video-element","title":"The <video> element","kind":"Read","text":"<video src=\"mesa.webm\" controls> <p>Your browser can't play this video. <a href=\"mesa.webm\">Download it</a> instead.</p> </video> src is the path to the file, just like an image. controls adds the browser's play button, timeline and volume. Leave it out and visitors can't pause or mute, so always include it unless you build your own. Anything between the tags is fallback content, shown only by browsers that can't play video at all. Read"},{"id":"sec-using-multiple-source-formats-to-improve-compatibility","title":"Using multiple source formats to improve compatibility","kind":"Read","text":"Contents of a media file A video file is a container (MP4, WebM, Ogg) holding tracks: usually one video track and one audio track. Each track is compressed with a codec, like H.264 or AV1 for video and AAC or Opus for audio. The extension tells you the container, not the codecs inside. mesa.mp4 (container) Video track · H.264 Audio track · AAC Media file support in browsers Not every browser can decode every codec, partly because some are patented. The fix: drop src from the <video> and list several <source> elements instead. The browser tries them in order and plays the first one it can: <video controls> <source src=\"mesa.webm\" type=\"video/webm\"> <source src=\"mesa.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"> <p>Your browser can't play this video.</p> </video> The type lets the browser skip files it can't play without downloading them. MP4 with H.264 plays almost everywhere, so it makes a good last option. Play"},{"id":"sec-which-source-plays","title":"Which source plays?","kind":"Play","text":"This list is checked against your actual browser with canPlayType(). Reorder the sources, or pretend to be an older browser. AV1 first MP4 first Pretend this is an old browser (MP4 only) Typo in the MP4 type Read"},{"id":"sec-other-video-features","title":"Other <video> features","kind":"Read","text":"width and height reserve space, like on an image. poster shows an image until the video starts, instead of a black box. autoplay starts playing straight away. Browsers block autoplay with sound, so it only works together with muted. Use it sparingly. muted starts with the sound off. loop starts again at the end. preload hints how much to download before anyone presses play: none, metadata (just the length and size) or auto. Play"},{"id":"sec-try-each-attribute","title":"Try each attribute","kind":"Play","text":"Turn attributes on and off and watch how the player behaves. It follows the same rules as a real browser. <video> attributes controls poster=\"cover.jpg\" autoplay muted loop Child elements <track kind=\"captions\"> Reload the page Read"},{"id":"sec-the-audio-element","title":"The <audio> element","kind":"Read","text":"<audio> works the same way as <video>, with controls, <source>s and fallback content. Since there's no picture, it doesn't take width, height or poster. <audio controls> <source src=\"ranger-talk.mp3\" type=\"audio/mpeg\"> <source src=\"ranger-talk.ogg\" type=\"audio/ogg\"> <p><a href=\"ranger-talk.mp3\">Download the talk</a></p> </audio> For audio-only content, like a podcast, provide a written transcript on the page as well. Read"},{"id":"sec-displaying-video-text-tracks","title":"Displaying video text tracks","kind":"Read","text":"People who are deaf or hard of hearing, people watching on mute, and people who don't speak the video's language all need its words as text. Those words live in a separate WebVTT file (.vtt), linked with <track> inside the video: <track kind=\"captions\" src=\"mesa.vtt\" srclang=\"en\" label=\"English\"> kind can be subtitles (a translation), captions (the dialogue plus important sounds, like \"[thunder]\") or descriptions (what's happening on screen, for people who can't see it). Look"},{"id":"sec-the-captions-file","title":"The captions file","kind":"Look","text":"Each cue has a start time, an end time and the text. The current cue is highlighted as the simulated video plays. Practice"},{"id":"sec-embedding-your-own-audio-and-video","title":"Embedding your own audio and video","kind":"Practice","text":"This video starts blaring on its own and can't be paused. Make it polite and accessible."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Always include controls unless you build your own play button. Without it, people can't pause or adjust volume. Browsers block autoplay with sound. Autoplay only works with muted, and should be rare. List more than one <source> (like MP4 and WebM) and the browser plays the first one it supports. Add captions for videos and a transcript for audio, for people who can't hear it and everyone watching on mute. <audio controls> works the same way, without the picture."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Uploading a huge video file straight from a phone. Compress it, or host it on YouTube or Vimeo and embed it. Expecting autoplay to work without muted. No poster, so the player shows a black box until it loads. Background videos that loop forever with no way to pause them. Getting the type wrong on a <source>, so the browser skips a file it could have played. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-video-audio/quiz"],"exercises":["core-video-audio/ex-media"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-svg-embeds","num":"2.8","title":"SVG and Embedded Content","desc":"Vector graphics with SVG, and iframes for videos, maps and other pages, safely.","props":["<svg>","viewBox","<iframe>","sandbox"],"intro":"Two ways to bring more than text and photos into a page: SVG, images written as code that stay sharp at any size, and iframes, windows that show a whole other page, like a YouTube video or a map.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-are-vector-graphics","title":"What are vector graphics?","kind":"Read","text":"Images come in two kinds: Raster images (JPEG, PNG, WebP) are grids of pixels. Blow one up and you see the squares. Vector images are instructions: \"a circle here, this big, this colour\". The browser draws them fresh at whatever size is needed, so they're always sharp, and simple ones are tiny files. Play"},{"id":"sec-zoom-in","title":"Zoom in","kind":"Play","text":"The same badge, as a 40-pixel PNG and as an SVG. Zoom: 1× badge.png · pixels badge.svg · shapes Read"},{"id":"sec-what-is-svg","title":"What is SVG?","kind":"Read","text":"SVG (Scalable Vector Graphics) is a markup language for drawing, written in tags just like HTML: <svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\"> <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"45\" fill=\"#0b6b5e\" /> <path d=\"M20 72 L45 35 L58 52 L66 42 L82 72 Z\" fill=\"#fff\" /> </svg> viewBox sets up the drawing's own coordinate system, here 100 × 100 units; the shapes use those units whatever size the SVG is shown at. Elements like <rect>, <circle>, <line>, <text> and <path> draw shapes. You'll rarely write complex SVG by hand: design tools like Figma, Illustrator and Inkscape export it. SVG is great for logos, icons and diagrams. It's no good for photos, which can't be described as shapes. Play"},{"id":"sec-edit-an-svg","title":"Edit an SVG","kind":"Play","text":"Change numbers and colours and watch it redraw. Badge Shapes Text SVG Drawn Read"},{"id":"sec-adding-svg-to-your-pages","title":"Adding SVG to your pages","kind":"Read","text":"The quick way: <img> <img src=\"badge.svg\" alt=\"Boulder Trail Club\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\"> Familiar, cached by the browser, and it takes alt text. But CSS and JavaScript can't reach inside, so you can't change its colours on hover. Troubleshooting Every current browser shows SVG. If an SVG in an <img> doesn't appear, check the path, and check the file has xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" on its <svg> tag: files need it, inline SVG doesn't. Including SVG code inside your HTML Paste the <svg> straight into your page. Now CSS can restyle its parts and there's no extra download. The cost: it isn't cached separately, and it clutters your HTML. For accessibility, give it role=\"img\" and a <title> as its first child, which works like alt text. Embedding an SVG with an <iframe> Possible, but it has all the drawbacks of iframes and none of the benefits of the other two. Don't. Practice"},{"id":"sec-inline-a-trail-badge","title":"Inline a trail badge","kind":"Practice","text":"Finish this inline SVG and make it accessible. Read"},{"id":"sec-a-short-history-of-embedding","title":"A short history of embedding","kind":"Read","text":"The early web tried many ways to put one thing inside another: frames that split the window into separate pages, then plugins like Java applets and Flash for anything interactive. Frames broke bookmarks and the back button; plugins were slow and insecure, and didn't work on phones. They're gone. What survived is the iframe: a window in your page that shows another web page. Read"},{"id":"sec-playing-with-classic-embedding-uses","title":"Playing with classic embedding uses","kind":"Read","text":"The services you'll most often embed give you the code from a Share or Embed button: <iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/VIDEO_ID\" title=\"Hiking the Mesa Trail, a 3-minute tour\" width=\"560\" height=\"315\" loading=\"lazy\"></iframe> The src must be the embed address, like youtube.com/embed/…, not the page you'd visit, like youtube.com/watch?v=…. That code arrives with a fixed width and height, which sticks out of the page on a phone. Fix it in CSS: iframe { width: 100%; height: auto; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; } Play"},{"id":"sec-build-an-embed","title":"Build an embed","kind":"Play","text":"Make the page narrow like a phone, then fix the embed so it fits and is accessible. embed YouTube video Google Map CodePen demo page width fix it title=\"…\" responsive CSS loading=\"lazy\" The embeds here are pictures of the real thing, so this page works offline. Hike of the week: Royal Arch Copy Look"},{"id":"sec-where-to-get-the-code","title":"Where to get the code","kind":"Look","text":"YouTube Open the video and click Share. Choose Embed and copy the code. Paste it into your HTML, then add a title and the responsive CSS. Google Maps Search for the place and click Share. Pick the Embed a map tab and copy the HTML. It already has loading=\"lazy\". Change the title and remove the fixed width. When embedding is refused Many sites, including most banks, social sites and Google Search, tell browsers not to show them inside iframes. You'll see a blank box or \"refused to connect.\" Link to them instead. Read"},{"id":"sec-using-iframes-to-embed-webpages","title":"Using iframes to embed webpages","kind":"Read","text":"src: the page to show. title: what's inside. A screen reader announces it, so people can decide whether to go in. Always add one. width and height: the frame's size, unless CSS sets it. loading=\"lazy\": wait until the visitor scrolls near. A YouTube player downloads close to 1 MB before anyone presses play. allowfullscreen: lets the embedded page go full screen, as video players do. sandbox: locks the embedded page down. More below. Because an iframe holds a separate document, your CSS can't reach inside it, and its CSS can't leak out. Play"},{"id":"sec-a-page-inside-a-page","title":"A page inside a page","kind":"Play","text":"Style the outer page and watch what happens inside the iframe. your page Trail Club This paragraph is on your page. Embedded page This paragraph lives in a different document, inside the iframe. \"> Apply p, h3 { color: red; text-transform: uppercase } Click the button to add a rule to your page's stylesheet. This is why you can't restyle a YouTube player with your own CSS. You can only size and position the iframe box itself. Security concerns An iframe runs someone else's code inside your page, and that cuts both ways: Clickjacking: an attacker can load a real site in an invisible iframe over a decoy button, so you click something you didn't mean to. That's why banks, social sites and many others send headers (X-Frame-Options or a Content Security Policy) telling browsers not to show them in frames. You'll see a blank box or \"refused to connect\". Link to them instead. Use HTTPS: embed only https:// addresses; browsers block insecure content inside a secure page anyway. Only embed what you need, and add sandbox to content you don't fully trust. An empty sandbox blocks scripts, forms, pop-ups and more; you then allow just what's needed, like sandbox=\"allow-scripts\". Read"},{"id":"sec-the-embed-and-object-elements","title":"The <embed> and <object> elements","kind":"Read","text":"<embed> and <object> are older general-purpose elements for embedding external content, once used for plugins like Flash. With plugins gone, you'll rarely need them: <img>, <video>, <audio> and <iframe> cover nearly everything. Read"},{"id":"sec-embedding-pdfs","title":"Embedding PDFs","kind":"Read","text":"To show a PDF preview in a page, an <iframe src=\"trail-map.pdf\"> works in most desktop browsers. Phones often won't display it, though, so always include an ordinary link to download it, and consider whether the content would be better as an HTML page. Practice"},{"id":"sec-make-the-embed-responsive","title":"Make the embed responsive","kind":"Practice","text":"This map is a fixed 600 by 450 box. Make it accessible, lazy, and fit any screen."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"SVG is shapes described in code, so it stays sharp at any size. Use it for logos, icons and diagrams. <img> is the simplest way to add an SVG file; inline <svg> lets CSS and JavaScript reach inside. Give every iframe a title that says what's in it. Screen readers announce it so people can decide whether to go in. To make an embed responsive, set width: 100%, height: auto and an aspect-ratio that matches the content, like 16 / 9 for video. loading=\"lazy\" waits until the visitor scrolls near the embed. A YouTube player downloads close to 1 MB before anyone presses play. Each embed is a full web page with its own scripts and tracking. Use them where they add real value."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Pasting the code with width=\"560\" and never testing on a phone, so the video sticks out of the page. Pasting the page address (youtube.com/watch?v=…) as the src. It must be the embed address (youtube.com/embed/…). Trying to style what's inside the iframe with your own CSS. Saving a photo as SVG, or an SVG icon as a large PNG. Inline SVG with no <title> or label, so screen readers can't describe it. Embedding a video you could simply host yourself with <video>, or vice versa: uploading a huge video file to GitHub Pages instead of YouTube. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-svg-embeds/quiz"],"exercises":["core-svg-embeds/ex-svg","core-svg-embeds/ex-embed"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-what-is-css","num":"3.1","title":"What is CSS?","desc":"Browser defaults, the parts of a rule, three places CSS can live, and a first look at selectors, @rules and shorthands.","props":["selector","property","<link>","/* */"],"intro":"HTML says what each part of a page is. CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) says how it looks: colours, fonts, spacing, layout, even animation. This lesson covers how a rule is written and the three places it can live.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-browser-default-styles","title":"Browser default styles","kind":"Read","text":"Even a page with no CSS at all isn't plain text. Headings are big and bold, lists have bullets, links are blue and underlined. That's because every browser has a built-in default stylesheet that makes unstyled HTML readable. Those defaults are deliberately plain, and they differ slightly between browsers. Your CSS builds on top of them. Play"},{"id":"sec-turn-the-stylesheet-off","title":"Turn the stylesheet off","kind":"Play","text":"The same HTML, with and without a stylesheet. Browser defaults only With our stylesheet Read"},{"id":"sec-what-is-css-for","title":"What is CSS for?","kind":"Read","text":"CSS controls everything about how a page looks: Text: fonts, sizes, colours, spacing between lines and letters. Boxes: backgrounds, borders, rounded corners, shadows and the space around everything. Layout: columns, grids, sidebars, and how all of it rearranges on a phone. Movement: transitions and animations. This week covers the language itself. Weeks 4 to 6 use it for styling, layout and responsive design. Read"},{"id":"sec-css-syntax-basics","title":"CSS syntax basics","kind":"Read","text":"A stylesheet is a list of rules, and every rule has the same shape: h1 { color: navy; font-size: 2rem; } The selector, h1, picks which elements the rule applies to. Inside the braces are declarations, each a property and a value separated by a colon and ending with a semicolon. This rule says: make every h1 navy and twice the normal text size. Play"},{"id":"sec-anatomy-of-a-rule","title":"Anatomy of a rule","kind":"Play","text":"Click any part of the rule, or pick a name below. Read"},{"id":"sec-how-is-css-applied-to-html","title":"How is CSS applied to HTML?","kind":"Read","text":"When a browser loads a page, it reads the HTML into a tree of elements (the DOM), reads the CSS, and works out which rules apply to which elements. Then it lays out and paints the result. Every element can be matched by many rules at once, and each rule can match many elements. So one rule, p { color: navy; }, colours every paragraph on the page, and adding a paragraph later means it's styled automatically. Try it. Play"},{"id":"sec-write-some-css","title":"Write some CSS","kind":"Play","text":"Edit either side. Add a paragraph, then an h2 and a rule for it. HTML CSS Result Read"},{"id":"sec-adding-css-to-our-document","title":"Adding CSS to our document","kind":"Read","text":"CSS can live in three places: External stylesheets A separate .css file, linked from the <head>: <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\"> The href is a path, just like a link's: css/styles.css if it's in a folder, ../styles.css from a page one folder down. One file can style every page of a site, so changing a colour once changes it everywhere, and browsers download it only once. Use this for real projects. Internal stylesheets A <style> element in the head. It styles only that one page, so a multi-page site would repeat it in every file. Inline styles A style attribute on a single element: <span style=\"color: purple\">. It mixes styling into your content, is hard to find later, and beats your stylesheet, which makes it hard to override. Avoid it. Play"},{"id":"sec-three-places-to-put-css","title":"Three places to put CSS","kind":"Play","text":"All three try to colour the same heading. Turn them on and off and swap the order of the two in the head. In the <head>, the Swap order Trail Report Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-stylesheet","title":"Fix the stylesheet","kind":"Practice","text":"One missing character can wipe out a whole rule. Find the three mistakes. Read"},{"id":"sec-using-common-selectors","title":"Using common selectors","kind":"Read","text":"Next lesson covers selectors in depth. Here's a quick tour of the ones you'll use most: Elements: p { } styles every paragraph. List several with commas: p, li { }. Classes: add class=\"special\" in the HTML, then select it with a dot: .special { }. Location: li em { } selects ems inside list items; h1 + p { } selects a paragraph straight after an h1. State: a:hover { } styles a link while the pointer is over it; a:visited { } once it's been visited. Practice"},{"id":"sec-style-the-trail-page","title":"Style the trail page","kind":"Practice","text":"Write four rules using the selectors above. Read"},{"id":"sec-other-css-syntax-features","title":"Other CSS syntax features","kind":"Read","text":"Functions Some values are calculated by a function, written as a name and brackets: width: calc(90% - 30px); or transform: rotate(0.8turn);. @rules Rules starting with @ give CSS special instructions. The most common, @media, applies styles only when a condition is true, like a wide enough screen (Week 6): @media (width >= 30em) { body { background-color: lightblue; } } Shorthand properties Some properties set several others at once. padding: 10px 15px 15px 5px; sets the top, right, bottom and left padding, going clockwise from the top. Play"},{"id":"sec-expand-the-shorthand","title":"Expand the shorthand","kind":"Play","text":"Type one to four values for padding. padding: 10px 10px 20px 5px 20px 30px 10px 15px 15px 5px content Comments Comments are notes the browser ignores, written between /* and */. Use them to label sections of a stylesheet, or to switch a rule off while you test. /* Trail cards */ .card { padding: 1rem; } White space CSS ignores extra spaces and line breaks between parts, so put one declaration per line for readability. But spaces inside a property name or between a number and its unit break it: margin: 0auto; and padding- left: 10px; are both invalid."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Browsers have default styles; your CSS builds on them. A rule is a selector plus a declaration block; each declaration is property: value;. Use an external stylesheet for real projects. One file can style every page of the site. Shorthands like padding go clockwise: top, right, bottom, left. Comments look like /* like this */."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"A <link> with the wrong path, so no styles load at all. A missing semicolon, colon or brace, which breaks the next declaration or rule. Using inline style=\"\" everywhere: hard to change, and it beats your stylesheet. HTML comments (<!-- -->) or // in a CSS file. A space between a number and its unit: 16 px. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-what-is-css/quiz"],"exercises":["core-what-is-css/ex-rules","core-what-is-css/ex-selectors"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-selectors","num":"3.2","title":"Selectors","desc":"Type, class and ID selectors, selector lists, attribute selectors and combinators: picking exactly the elements you mean.","props":[".class","#id","[href$=\".pdf\"]","ul > li"],"intro":"Every CSS rule starts with a selector: the part that says which elements to style. Choose them by type, by class or id, by attribute, or by where they sit in the page.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-is-a-selector","title":"What is a selector?","kind":"Read","text":"A selector is the first part of a rule, before the braces. The elements it matches are called its subject, and those are the elements the declarations style. h1 { color: navy; } /* subject: every h1 */ .box { padding: 1em; } /* subject: every element with class=\"box\" */ Read"},{"id":"sec-type-class-and-id-selectors","title":"Type, class and ID selectors","kind":"Read","text":"Type selectors A type selector is just an element name: span, strong, em. It matches every element of that type. Class selectors A class selector is a dot followed by a class name. .highlight matches every element with class=\"highlight\", whatever its type. Classes are reusable, so they're what you'll use most. On a particular element: span.highlight matches only spans with that class; h1.highlight only headings. More than one class: an element can have several, separated by spaces: class=\"notebox warning\". Chain them with no space to match elements with both: .notebox.warning. ID selectors An ID selector is a hash followed by an id: #one matches the element with id=\"one\". An id should appear only once per page, so an ID selector styles one element. Classes are usually the better choice: they're reusable and easier to override. Read"},{"id":"sec-selector-lists","title":"Selector lists","kind":"Read","text":"When several selectors share the same declarations, list them with commas instead of repeating the rule: h1, .special { color: blue; } Careful: if any selector in a list is invalid, the whole rule is thrown away. h1, ..special { } styles nothing at all, not even the h1s. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-universal-selector","title":"The universal selector","kind":"Read","text":"An asterisk, *, matches everything. On its own, * { margin: 0; } removes the margin from every element. Inside a selector it can make your meaning clearer: article :first-child (with a space) and article *:first-child mean the same thing, \"any first child inside an article\", but the second is much harder to misread as article:first-child. Practice"},{"id":"sec-type-class-id-and-lists","title":"Type, class, ID and lists","kind":"Practice","text":"Write four rules. Read"},{"id":"sec-attribute-selectors","title":"Attribute selectors","kind":"Read","text":"An attribute selector matches elements by their attributes, written in square brackets. Presence and value selectors [attr] has the attribute at all: a[title] [attr=\"value\"] the value is exactly this: input[type=\"email\"] [attr~=\"value\"] one of its space-separated words is exactly this [attr|=\"value\"] exactly this, or this followed by a hyphen: [lang|=\"en\"] matches en and en-US Substring matching selectors [attr^=\"value\"] starts with: a[href^=\"https\"] [attr$=\"value\"] ends with: a[href$=\".pdf\"] [attr*=\"value\"] contains anywhere: [class*=\"card\"] Play"},{"id":"sec-which-items-match","title":"Which items match?","kind":"Play","text":"Each list item has a different class value. Pick a selector. Practice"},{"id":"sec-style-links-by-their-address","title":"Style links by their address","kind":"Practice","text":"Use attribute selectors only: no classes. Read"},{"id":"sec-combinators","title":"Combinators","kind":"Read","text":"A combinator joins two selectors and says how the elements relate: Descendant (a space): .box p matches a p anywhere inside .box, however deeply nested. Child (>): ul > li matches only lis that are direct children of a ul, not ones in a nested list. Next-sibling (+): h1 + p matches a p that comes straight after an h1, with the same parent. Subsequent-sibling (~): h1 ~ p matches every p anywhere after an h1, with the same parent, even with other elements in between. Combining combinators with selectors Any selector can sit on either side: ul > li.a, .card h2 + p. Read them right to left: \"a p, straight after an h2, inside a .card\". Watch the spaces .tag .hard (with a space) means \"a .hard inside a .tag\". .tag.hard (no space) means \"one element with both classes\". That one space is behind a lot of confusing bugs. Play"},{"id":"sec-type-a-selector","title":"Type a selector","kind":"Play","text":"Type any selector and the matching elements light up. Try the suggestions, then write your own. Selector Try these Mesa Trail 6.7 miles Moderate Water Bear spray Map Details PDF map Royal Arch 3.4 miles Hard Details Trail data updated Tuesday Read"},{"id":"sec-quick-reference","title":"Quick reference","kind":"Read","text":"Combinators: how elements relate .card p space Descendant: a p anywhere inside .card .card > a > Child: an a directly inside .card h2 + p + Adjacent sibling: the p right after an h2 h2 ~ a ~ General sibling: any a after an h2, same parent Attribute selectors [href] has the attribute [type=\"email\"] exactly equals [href^=\"https\"] starts with [href$=\".pdf\"] ends with [class*=\"card\"] contains anywhere Practice"},{"id":"sec-target-exactly-the-right-elements","title":"Target exactly the right elements","kind":"Practice","text":"The declarations are done. Write a selector for each."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Type selectors match an element name; .class matches a class; #id matches one element. Chain with no space (.a.b) for elements that have both; separate with commas for a list. Attribute selectors use square brackets: ^= starts, $= ends, *= contains. Combinators: space (inside), > (direct child), + (right after), ~ (any later sibling). Prefer classes. IDs and long chains are hard to override later."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"A stray space: .tag .hard when you meant .tag.hard. Forgetting the dot: card { } looks for a <card> element. One typo in a selector list, which throws away the whole rule. Reusing an id on several elements instead of using a class. Expecting h1 + p to match a paragraph that isn't directly after the heading. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-selectors/quiz"],"exercises":["core-selectors/ex-basic","core-selectors/ex-attr","core-selectors/ex-select"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-cascade","num":"3.3","title":"The Cascade","desc":"Source order, specificity and inheritance: how CSS settles conflicts, and how to win without !important.","props":["specificity","inherit","!important","source order"],"intro":"Several rules often try to style the same element. CSS settles every argument the same way, with three ideas: the cascade, specificity and inheritance. Once you know them, \"why isn't my CSS working?\" usually answers itself.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-conflicting-rules","title":"Conflicting rules","kind":"Read","text":"Cascade When two rules with equally strong selectors set the same property, the one that comes later wins: h1 { color: red; } h1 { color: blue; } /* headings are blue */ Specificity But a more specific selector beats a less specific one, wherever it is. A class is more specific than an element name, so this heading is red even though the h1 rule comes later: .main-heading { color: red; } h1 { color: blue; } Inheritance Some values set on a parent are passed down to its children. Set color: blue on body and every paragraph and span inside turns blue, unless a rule targets them directly. Understanding how the concepts work together These three ideas together decide every property of every element. The rest of this lesson takes each in turn. Read"},{"id":"sec-understanding-inheritance","title":"Understanding inheritance","kind":"Read","text":"Inheritance is why you can set a font once and have the whole page use it. Roughly: text properties inherit (color, font-*, line-height, text-align), and box properties don't (border, padding, margin, width, background). That's sensible: if a border on a <section> put a border on every paragraph inside it, nobody would use borders. Play"},{"id":"sec-what-gets-inherited","title":"What gets inherited?","kind":"Play","text":"Set a property on the article. The badges read what the browser actually computed for each child. Set on article button, input { font: inherit; color: inherit } article h3 Mesa Trail p Rolling foothills with views of the Flatirons. aTrail map button Save trail input Controlling inheritance Every property also accepts special values that control inheritance: inherit: take the parent's value, even for a property that normally doesn't inherit. initial: use the property's starting value from the CSS specification (for color, that's black). revert: go back to the browser's default style for this element (for a link, blue). unset: act like inherit for properties that inherit, and like initial for the rest. Play"},{"id":"sec-inheritance-control-values","title":"Inheritance control values","kind":"Play","text":"The paragraph is firebrick. Choose a value for the link's color. (no rule) inherit initial revert unset Read the trail report before you go. Resetting all property values The shorthand all applies one of those values to (almost) every property at once. blockquote { all: unset; } wipes out every style on the element, so you can start it from scratch. Practice"},{"id":"sec-let-it-inherit","title":"Let it inherit","kind":"Practice","text":"This stylesheet sets the same font and colour three times. Set them once and let inheritance do the rest. Read"},{"id":"sec-understanding-the-cascade","title":"Understanding the cascade","kind":"Read","text":"When rules disagree about the same property on the same element, the browser checks three things, in order of importance: Importance: is a declaration marked !important? Specificity: which selector is more specific? Source order: which rule comes later? Source order If importance and specificity tie, later wins. That includes the order of your <link> tags: link a general stylesheet after a specific one, and the general one can win. Specificity The browser scores each selector in three columns: IDs: each #id scores one in the first column. Classes, attributes and pseudo-classes: each .class, [attr] or :hover scores one in the second. Elements and pseudo-elements: each h2 or ::before scores one in the third. Scores are compared column by column, from left to right, so one ID outranks any number of classes, and one class outranks any number of elements. * and combinators score nothing. Play"},{"id":"sec-score-two-selectors","title":"Score two selectors","kind":"Play","text":"Both rules color the same heading. Type selectors to compare their scores. Rule A (written first) Rule B (written second) Mesa Trail Inline styles A style=\"\" attribute beats every selector in every stylesheet. That's one reason to avoid inline styles: the only way to override them is !important. !important Adding !important to a declaration makes it beat every normal declaration, whatever the specificity. It's tempting as a quick fix, but the next time you need to change that property, only another !important will do, and soon nobody can tell which rule wins. Fix the specificity instead. Play"},{"id":"sec-which-rule-wins","title":"Which rule wins?","kind":"Play","text":"Three rules try to colour the same heading, <h3 class=\"title\" id=\"top\">. Change the selectors, reorder the rules, or add !important. Mesa Trail The browser checks in this order !important beats everything without it. Then higher specificity wins (IDs, then classes, then elements). If it's still a tie, the rule that comes later in the CSS wins. Practice"},{"id":"sec-win-without-important","title":"Win without !important","kind":"Practice","text":"Someone forced a rule to win. Undo it properly. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-effect-of-css-location","title":"The effect of CSS location","kind":"Read","text":"Your stylesheets aren't the only ones. The browser has its default (user-agent) stylesheet, and users can add their own, for example to force larger text. They're layered in this order, each overriding the ones before: Browser defaults User styles Your styles (the author) Your !important styles User !important styles Browser !important styles Notice the flip: for !important, the user beats the author. That's deliberate, so people who need large text or high contrast always get it."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Later rules beat earlier ones, but only when specificity ties. Specificity: IDs, then classes/attributes/pseudo-classes, then elements. Compare column by column. Text properties inherit; box properties don't. inherit, initial, revert and unset control inheritance for any property. Inline styles beat selectors, and !important beats both. Avoid them."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Fighting specificity with !important instead of a better selector. Expecting a border or padding on a parent to apply to its children. Linking stylesheets in the wrong order, so a general file overrides a specific one. Styling with IDs, which makes later overrides need even stronger selectors. Forgetting that buttons and inputs don't inherit the page's font. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-cascade/quiz"],"exercises":["core-cascade/ex-inherit","core-cascade/ex-important"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-debugging-css","num":"3.4","title":"Debugging CSS","desc":"DevTools: the DOM versus view source, the Styles pane, live edits, and a checklist for CSS that will not apply.","props":["DevTools","Inspect","Styles pane","CSS.supports()"],"intro":"When CSS doesn't do what you expect, don't guess and reload. Your browser's developer tools show exactly which rules apply to each element, which lost, and why, and let you try fixes live.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-how-to-access-browser-devtools","title":"How to access browser DevTools","kind":"Read","text":"Every desktop browser has DevTools built in. You'll use them every day from now on. Inspect one elementRight-click it on the page and choose Inspect. DevTools opens with that element selected. Open DevToolsF12 or Ctrl+Shift+I on Windows. Cmd+Option+I on a Mac. In Safari, turn on \"Show features for web developers\" in Settings first. Test on a phone sizeClick the device toolbar icon (a phone and tablet) at the top of DevTools to preview any screen width. Changes aren't savedEdits in DevTools disappear when you reload. Once you've found the fix, copy it into your CSS file. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-dom-versus-view-source","title":"The DOM versus view source","kind":"Read","text":"View source (Ctrl/Cmd+U) shows the HTML file exactly as the server sent it. DevTools' Elements panel shows something different: the DOM, the tree the browser actually built. They can differ, because the browser repairs mistakes, adds elements the rules require (like <tbody> in a table), and JavaScript can change the page after it loads. When debugging, the DOM is the truth: it's what your CSS is matched against. Play"},{"id":"sec-source-in-dom-out","title":"Source in, DOM out","kind":"Play","text":"Pick an example. Green lines are things the browser added that aren't in the source. A table Unclosed paragraphs A list in a paragraph View source DOM (Elements panel) Read"},{"id":"sec-inspecting-the-applied-css","title":"Inspecting the applied CSS","kind":"Read","text":"Right-click an element and choose Inspect. On the right of the Elements panel, the Styles pane (called Rules in Firefox) lists every rule that matches the selected element, most specific at the top, with the file name and line number of each. Declarations that lost are crossed out; look higher up to find what beat them. Rules marked user agent stylesheet are the browser's defaults: you can't edit them, but your CSS can override them. Shorthands like margin can be expanded to see their longhands. Each declaration has a checkbox: untick it to switch it off and see what changes. Editing values Click any value to edit it. Colours open a colour picker. Changes apply instantly, but only in your browser, until you reload. Adding a new property Click just inside the closing brace of a rule (or in the element.style box at the top) to add a declaration. DevTools autocompletes property names and values as you type. Understanding the box model The Computed or Layout tab shows a diagram of the selected element's content, padding, border and margin, with real pixel sizes. It's the fastest way to find out where unexpected space comes from (more on that in the next lesson). Solving specificity issues If a declaration is crossed out, the rule above it won. Compare the two selectors' specificity, then fix your CSS: a more specific selector, a later rule, or less specific competition. Don't reach for !important. Watch out Changes made in DevTools disappear when you reload. Once a fix works, copy it into your real CSS file. Play"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-trail-card","title":"Fix the trail card","kind":"Play","text":"This practice panel works like the real Elements panel. Complete the three missions. localhost/trail.html Royal Arch 3.4 miles · Hard · 1,400 ft of gain to a sandstone arch above Boulder. Save trail ElementsConsoleSourcesNetwork Read"},{"id":"sec-debugging-problems-in-css","title":"Debugging problems in CSS","kind":"Read","text":"When something won't work, work through these in order: Take a step back from the problem Stuck for a while? Take a break, or explain the problem out loud to someone (or something). The fix is often obvious with fresh eyes. Do you have valid HTML and CSS? Browsers silently ignore CSS they don't understand: a misspelled property, a missing unit, a missing semicolon. Nothing turns red on the page. Run your files through the W3C HTML validator and CSS validator, and look for declarations DevTools shows with a warning icon. Are the property and value supported? Newer features may not work in every browser. Check the browser compatibility table at the bottom of any MDN property page. DevTools also flags unsupported declarations. Is something else overriding your CSS? Look for your declaration crossed out in the Styles pane, and see which rule beat it. Make a reduced test case If you still can't find it, rebuild the problem from scratch in as little code as possible: Copy just the HTML that shows the problem into a new file, or a playground like CodePen. Add only the CSS that affects it. Remove things one at a time until the problem disappears. The last thing you removed is involved. If it's still a mystery, you now have a tiny example to ask someone about. Play"},{"id":"sec-does-this-browser-understand-it","title":"Does this browser understand it?","kind":"Play","text":"Type a declaration. Your browser's own CSS.supports() says whether it would apply it, or silently ignore it. Declaration Common culprits Practice"},{"id":"sec-debug-it-with-devtools","title":"Debug it with DevTools","kind":"Practice","text":"Three styles aren't applying. Right-click the preview and choose Inspect to find out why. Practice"},{"id":"sec-silently-ignored","title":"Silently ignored","kind":"Practice","text":"Every declaration here is valid-looking but ignored. Fix them."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"The DOM in DevTools is what your CSS matches against, not your source file. The Styles pane lists every matching rule; crossed-out declarations lost. The file name and line number next to each rule tell you where to make the fix. Browsers silently ignore invalid or unsupported CSS. Validate, and check MDN's compatibility tables. A reduced test case isolates a bug, and makes it easy to ask for help."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Fixing the problem in DevTools and forgetting to copy the fix into the real file. Searching your CSS for a rule that's actually the browser's default. A rule that's missing completely, from a selector typo or a stylesheet that didn't load. Typos the browser ignores without complaint: colour, 16 px, a missing semicolon. Debugging the wrong element. Check which element is selected in the tree. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-debugging-css/quiz"],"exercises":["core-debugging-css/ex-devtools","core-debugging-css/ex-invalid"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-box-model","num":"3.5","title":"The Box Model","desc":"Block and inline boxes, content, padding, border and margin, box-sizing, margin collapsing and inline-block.","props":["padding","margin","box-sizing","inline-block"],"intro":"Everything on a web page is a box: headings, paragraphs, links, even a single word in bold. How big each box is, and how much space it takes up, follows a set of rules called the box model.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-block-and-inline-boxes","title":"Block and inline boxes","kind":"Read","text":"Boxes come in two main kinds, and the kind decides how a box behaves in the page's flow: A block box starts on a new line, stretches to fill the width of its container, and respects width, height, padding and margin. Headings, paragraphs and <div>s are block by default. An inline box flows within a line of text. It doesn't start a new line, and width and height don't apply. <a>, <span>, <em> and <strong> are inline by default. Read"},{"id":"sec-inner-and-outer-display-types","title":"Inner and outer display types","kind":"Read","text":"The display property actually sets two things. The outer display type decides how the box behaves among its neighbours: block or inline. The inner display type decides how the box lays out its own children. Normally the children just flow, but display: flex makes them a flexbox layout (Week 5). So display: flex means \"block on the outside, flex on the inside\", and display: inline-flex means \"inline on the outside, flex on the inside\". Play"},{"id":"sec-examples-of-different-display-types","title":"Examples of different display types","kind":"Play","text":"The list's outer type decides how it sits in the paragraph; its inner type decides how the items sit inside it. block flex inline-flex Pack water snacks a map before you leave the trailhead. Read"},{"id":"sec-what-is-the-css-box-model","title":"What is the CSS box model?","kind":"Read","text":"Parts of a box Every block box has four layers, from the inside out: Content: the text or image itself, sized with width and height. Padding: space between the content and the border. It's inside the box, so it shows the background. Border: the line around the padding. Margin: space outside the border that pushes other boxes away. It's always transparent. The standard CSS box model By default, width and height set only the content; padding and border are added on top. Margin isn't counted in the box's size, but it does take up space. .box { width: 350px; height: 150px; margin: 10px; padding: 25px; border: 5px solid black; } /* the box is 350 + 25 + 25 + 5 + 5 = 410px wide, and 210px tall */ The alternative CSS box model With box-sizing: border-box, width sets the whole visible box, padding and border included, and the content shrinks to fit. That's much easier to plan with. Many developers switch every element to it at the top of their stylesheet: html { box-sizing: border-box; } *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: inherit; } Use DevTools Select an element and open the Computed or Layout tab: it draws the box model with the size of every layer. When a box is bigger than you expected, look there first. Play"},{"id":"sec-playing-with-box-models","title":"Playing with box models","kind":"Play","text":"Change each layer and watch the math. Then switch box-sizing and see the same settings give a different size. .box box-sizing content-box border-box width padding border-width margin content Read"},{"id":"sec-margins-padding-and-borders","title":"Margins, padding, and borders","kind":"Read","text":"Each of the three has a shorthand that sets all four sides, and longhands for one side at a time: margin-top, padding-left, border-bottom. Shorthands with several values go clockwise from the top: margin: 10px 20px is 10px top and bottom, 20px left and right. Margin Margin pushes other boxes away. It can be negative, which pulls things closer or makes boxes overlap. margin: 0 auto on a block with a width centres it: the left and right margins share the leftover space. Margin collapsing: when the bottom margin of one block meets the top margin of the next, they don't add up. They collapse into the larger of the two. A heading with margin-bottom: 30px above a paragraph with margin-top: 20px leaves 30px between them, not 50px. Play"},{"id":"sec-margin-collapsing","title":"Margin collapsing","kind":"Play","text":"Set each box's margin, and measure the real gap between them. Box A: margin-bottom Box B: margin-top Box A Box B Borders border: 2px solid teal sets width, style and colour on all four sides. Each side and each part has a longhand: border-top, border-width, border-left-color and so on. Without a style (solid, dashed, dotted…) there's no border at all. Padding Padding is the space inside the border. Unlike margin, it can't be negative. Use it to stop text from touching the edge of a coloured box. Practice"},{"id":"sec-size-and-space-two-cards","title":"Size and space two cards","kind":"Practice","text":"Fix the cards' size without touching their width, padding or border, then space and centre them. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-box-model-and-inline-boxes","title":"The box model and inline boxes","kind":"Read","text":"Inline boxes follow only part of the box model. width and height are ignored. Padding, borders and margins do apply, but only left and right ones push other content away: top and bottom padding and borders spread over the lines above and below without moving them. Read"},{"id":"sec-using-display-inline-block","title":"Using display: inline-block","kind":"Read","text":"display: inline-block is the middle ground. The box sits in a line of text like a word, doesn't force a new line, but respects width, height and padding, pushing other content out of the way. It's perfect for giving links a bigger, clickable area. Play"},{"id":"sec-playing-with-inline-block","title":"Playing with inline-block","kind":"Play","text":"These nav links have padding: 1em 2em. Switch their display. inline inline-block Trail Club Trails Events Join Next hike: Saturday. Practice"},{"id":"sec-bigger-link-targets","title":"Bigger link targets","kind":"Practice","text":"Turn these links into comfortable, tappable buttons."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Block boxes start a new line and fill their container; inline boxes flow in the text and ignore width and height. display sets an outer type (block or inline) and an inner type (normal flow, flex, grid). Standard box model: width sets the content; padding and border are added on top. box-sizing: border-box makes width include padding and border. Touching vertical margins collapse into the larger one. inline-block flows like text but respects width, height and padding."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Setting width on a link or span and wondering why nothing happens. Forgetting that padding and border make a content-box element wider than its width. Expecting 30px + 20px margins to make a 50px gap. Using padding where you meant margin: padding fills with the background colour. A border with a width and colour but no style, so it doesn't appear. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-box-model/quiz"],"exercises":["core-box-model/ex-cards","core-box-model/ex-links"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-display-flow","num":"3.6","title":"Display and Normal Flow","desc":"How the browser lays out boxes by default, changing box types with display, and the tools that override normal flow.","props":["normal flow","display","inline-block","display: none"],"intro":"Before you write any layout CSS, the browser already has a plan for where every box goes: normal flow. Understanding it, and the ways CSS can override it, is the foundation for every layout you'll build.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-normal-layout-flow","title":"Normal layout flow","kind":"Read","text":"Normal flow is how the browser lays out a page when you haven't told it otherwise. It puts elements on the page in the same order as your HTML, one after another. Because of that, a well-structured HTML document is already readable before any layout CSS. Start from good HTML and change the flow only where you need to. Read"},{"id":"sec-how-are-elements-laid-out-by-default","title":"How are elements laid out by default?","kind":"Read","text":"Each element's content sits inside its padding, border and margin: the box model from the last lesson. Then: Block boxes stack one below another, in the block direction (top to bottom for English). Each fills the width of its parent, and is as tall as its content. Their vertical margins collapse where they touch. Inline boxes sit side by side in the inline direction (left to right for English), like words. When a line runs out of room, they wrap onto the next line. Play"},{"id":"sec-normal-flow-example","title":"Normal flow example","kind":"Play","text":"Every block box has a blue dashed outline; inline boxes have orange ones. Change the width of the container. body width Basic document flow I am a basic block level element. My adjacent block level elements sit on new lines below me. By default we span 100% of the width of our parent element, and we are as tall as our child content. Our total width and height is our content + padding + border width/height. Inline elements like this one and this one sit on the same line as each other and adjacent text nodes, if there is space on the same line. Overflowing inline elements wrap onto a new line if possible, like this one containing text. The display property changes which kind of box an element generates: block, inline, or inline-block (inline in the flow, but sized like a block). That decides which properties it listens to. Play"},{"id":"sec-switch-the-display-type","title":"Switch the display type","kind":"Play","text":"Change display on three boxes, then give them a width, height, margin and padding to see which ones listen. .box (three of them) display inline block inline-block width height margin (top and bottom) padding Pack water and snacks before you set out, and check the forecast because afternoon storms roll in fast above treeline. Read"},{"id":"sec-overriding-normal-flow","title":"Overriding normal flow","kind":"Read","text":"Normal flow can't put things side by side in columns, or pin a header to the top of the screen. For that, CSS has several layout tools, each covered in a later week: The display property: besides block and inline, display: flex and display: grid switch on whole layout systems for an element's children. Floats: float: left moves a box to one side and lets text wrap around it, like an image in a magazine. Positioning: position moves a box from where normal flow put it, or pins it to the screen. Flexbox (Week 5): lays items out in one direction, a row or a column, and shares space between them. Grid (Week 5): lays items out in rows and columns at once. Responsive design (Week 6): changes the layout to suit the screen size. Other layout techniques Two more exist but are less common: table layout (display: table, from before flexbox existed) and multi-column layout (column-count: 3), which flows text into columns like a newspaper. Play"},{"id":"sec-same-html-different-layouts","title":"Same HTML, different layouts","kind":"Play","text":"Three boxes in one container. Each button applies a different layout tool to the same HTML. Box 1: some text that runs a little longer Box 2 Box 3 Trailing text in the container, flowing after the boxes. It shows how other content reacts to each layout. Read"},{"id":"sec-hiding-things","title":"Hiding things","kind":"Read","text":"display: none is the most drastic display value: the element isn't drawn at all, and normal flow closes up the space it would have taken. Two other properties hide elements but keep their place in the flow. Play"},{"id":"sec-display-none-vs-visibility-hidden-vs-opacity-0","title":"display: none vs visibility: hidden vs opacity: 0","kind":"Play","text":"All three make card 2 invisible. Watch what happens to the space it took up. Visible display: none visibility: hidden opacity: 0 1 2 3 display: noneGone completely. The space closes up and screen readers skip it. visibility: hiddenInvisible, but its space stays. Screen readers skip it. opacity: 0Invisible but still there: it keeps its space, can be clicked, and screen readers still read it. Practice"},{"id":"sec-change-how-boxes-flow","title":"Change how boxes flow","kind":"Practice","text":"Three elements, three different display values. Practice"},{"id":"sec-a-first-layout","title":"A first layout","kind":"Practice","text":"Use one display value to put the cards side by side."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Normal flow places elements in source order: blocks stack, inline boxes sit side by side and wrap. Good HTML in normal flow is already readable; layout CSS changes it only where needed. display chooses block, inline or inline-block, and switches on flex and grid. Floats, positioning, flexbox and grid all override normal flow in different ways. display: none removes the box; visibility: hidden and opacity: 0 keep its space."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Setting width on an inline element and wondering why it's ignored. Reaching for floats or positioning to build a whole layout: use flexbox or grid. Changing the visual order so it no longer matches the HTML order, confusing keyboard and screen reader users. Hiding with opacity: 0 when you meant to remove: it's still clickable and still read aloud. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-display-flow/quiz"],"exercises":["core-display-flow/ex-display","core-display-flow/ex-layout"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-values-units","num":"3.7","title":"Values and Units","desc":"Lengths, ems and rems, percentages, colours in every format, images, positions and math functions.","props":["rem","%","hsl()","calc()"],"intro":"Every declaration ends in a value: a colour, a length, a percentage, an image, a function. Knowing the kinds of value CSS accepts, and what each unit is measured against, is what makes a layout adapt instead of break.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-is-a-css-value","title":"What is a CSS value?","kind":"Read","text":"On MDN's reference pages, the kinds of value a property accepts are written in angle brackets: <color>, <length>, <percentage>. These are value types, not HTML elements. So when the page for color says it accepts a <color>, any of the colour formats below will work: h1 { color: black; /* a keyword */ background-color: rgb(197 93 161); /* a function */ } Read"},{"id":"sec-numbers-lengths-and-percentages","title":"Numbers, lengths, and percentages","kind":"Read","text":"Lengths A length is a number with a unit, with no space between them. There are two families: Absolute units are always the same size: px (pixels) is the one you'll use. Print units like cm, in and pt exist, but don't belong on screens. Relative units are measured from something else, so they adapt: em: the font size of the element itself (for font-size, the parent's). rem: the font size of the root element, <html>, usually 16px. vw and vh: 1% of the browser window's width and height. Play"},{"id":"sec-units-how-big-is-12","title":"Units: how big is “12”?","kind":"Play","text":"Each bar below is the same number with a different unit. Change the settings and watch which bars move. html font-size (sets rem) parent font-size (sets em) parent width (sets %) ems and rems em sizes compound. In a nested list where each li is 1.3em, every level is 1.3 times its parent, so text grows as you go deeper. rem always measures from the root, so every level is the same size. Play"},{"id":"sec-ems-compound-rems-don-t","title":"ems compound, rems don't","kind":"Play","text":"The same nested list, with li { font-size: 1.3em } on the left and 1.3rem on the right. Add levels. nesting levels 1.3em 1.3rem Percentages A percentage is always a share of something else, usually the parent's size. width: 40% is 40% of the parent's width; font-size: 80% is 80% of the parent's font size. Numbers Some properties take a plain number with no unit: opacity: 0.6 (0 is invisible, 1 solid) or line-height: 1.5 (1.5 times the font size). Which unit? Use rem for font sizes: people who set a larger default text size in their browser get bigger text, while px ignores that setting. Use % or max-width for widths so boxes fit small screens. Keep px for things that should stay fixed, like borders. Practice"},{"id":"sec-pick-better-units","title":"Pick better units","kind":"Practice","text":"These pixel values ignore the reader's settings and the screen size. Swap in units that adapt. Read"},{"id":"sec-color","title":"Color","kind":"Read","text":"Color keywords CSS has around 150 named colours, like teal, antiquewhite and rebeccapurple. Handy for quick tests, but you can't fine-tune them. Hexadecimal RGB values A hex color like #02798b is three pairs of hexadecimal digits for red, green and blue, each from 00 to ff. Designers hand you hex codes, so you'll see them everywhere. RGB values rgb(2 121 139) is the same idea in decimal: red, green and blue from 0 to 255. Add a slash and a fourth value for transparency (the alpha channel): rgb(2 121 139 / 30%) is 30% opaque. Using hues to specify a color Mixing red, green and blue in your head is hard. Hue-based formats start from a position on the colour wheel, in degrees: 0 is red, 120 green, 240 blue. HSL: hsl(188 97% 28%) is hue, saturation (grey to vivid) and lightness (black to white). Keep the hue and change the lightness for a matching darker or lighter shade. Transparency works the same way: hsl(188 97% 28% / 30%). HWB: hwb(188 0% 45%) is hue, then how much white and black to mix in. Play"},{"id":"sec-mixing-colors-with-hsl","title":"Mixing colors with HSL","kind":"Play","text":"Keep the hue and change the lightness to get a darker or lighter version of the same colour. Check the contrast for text. hue (the color wheel) saturation (gray → vivid) lightness (black → white) Named colors Aa on white Aa on black The same color, written three ways hex rgb hsl Contrast for text (needs 4.5 : 1 or more) Practice"},{"id":"sec-one-colour-many-formats","title":"One colour, many formats","kind":"Practice","text":"Write each colour in the format asked for. Read"},{"id":"sec-images-position-and-strings","title":"Images, position, and strings","kind":"Read","text":"Images An <image> value can be a file, with url(), or a gradient that CSS draws for you: background-image: url(\"images/arch.jpg\"); background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, rgb(119 0 255 / 39%), rgb(0 212 255 / 25%)); Position A <position> places something, like a background image, in a box. It takes keywords (top, right, center…), lengths, or both: background-position: right 60px means against the right edge, 60px from the top. Strings and identifiers Keywords like red or center are identifiers: written without quotes. Some places need a string of text in quotes, like generated content: content: \"New!\";. Play"},{"id":"sec-images-and-position","title":"Images and position","kind":"Play","text":"A gradient, positioned in a box. background-image linear-gradient(…) radial-gradient(…) repeating-linear-gradient(…) background-position center top left right 60px bottom right 20px 30px Read"},{"id":"sec-functions","title":"Functions","kind":"Read","text":"A function is a name followed by brackets, which CSS works out for you. You've already met rgb(), hsl(), url() and gradients. Math functions calc() mixes units: width: calc(20% + 100px). min() picks the smallest of its values, max() the largest: width: min(100%, 600px) is never wider than 600px or its parent. clamp() takes a minimum, a preferred value and a maximum: font-size: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 2rem). Play"},{"id":"sec-math-functions","title":"Math functions","kind":"Play","text":"Change the wrapper's width and watch each box recalculate. wrapper width"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Value types like <length> and <color> tell you what a property accepts. px is fixed; em, rem, %, vw and vh adapt. em compounds through nested elements; rem always measures from the root. Hex, rgb() and hsl() can all describe the same colour; add / 50% for transparency. calc(), min(), max() and clamp() do the math for you."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"A space between number and unit (16 px), or no unit at all on a length. Font sizes in px, which ignore the reader's text-size setting. Nested em font sizes that grow (or shrink) out of control. Low-contrast text, like light grey on white. calc(20%+100px) without spaces around the +: it's invalid. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-values-units/quiz"],"exercises":["core-values-units/ex-units","core-values-units/ex-colors"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-text-styling","num":"3.8","title":"Fundamental Text Styling","desc":"Font stacks, size, weight, style and decoration; alignment, line height and spacing; and the font shorthand.","props":["font-family","line-height","text-transform","font"],"intro":"Most of what's on a web page is text, so how it reads matters more than almost any other design choice. CSS splits text styling in two: the font itself, and how the text is laid out.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-is-involved-in-styling-text-in-css","title":"What is involved in styling text in CSS?","kind":"Read","text":"Text sits inside its element's content box and flows from the start of the line to the end, wrapping onto new lines. You can't select a single word or line with CSS, only whole elements (with two exceptions, ::first-line and ::first-letter). The properties fall into two groups: Font styles change the letters themselves: which font, how big, bold, italic, colour. Text layout styles change the space around them: alignment, line spacing, letter and word spacing. Read"},{"id":"sec-fonts","title":"Fonts","kind":"Read","text":"Color color sets the colour of the text (and of text decorations like underlines). It's named color, not font-color or text-color. Font families font-family names the font to use. It only works if the font is available: installed on the visitor's computer, or downloaded as a web font (next lesson). A few web safe fonts, like Arial, Verdana, Georgia and Times New Roman, are on nearly every computer. Because you can't be sure, list several fonts in order of preference. That's a font stack: the browser uses the first one it has. Always end with a generic family name, which every browser maps to some font it does have: p { font-family: \"Trebuchet MS\", Verdana, sans-serif; } Quote names with spaces in them. The generic families look like this in your browser: Play"},{"id":"sec-try-a-font-stack","title":"Try a font stack","kind":"Play","text":"Type a font stack. The browser draws the sample in the first font it has. font-family: \"Trail Sans\", Georgia, serif \"Trebuchet MS\", Verdana, sans-serif Courier New, monospace \"Made Up Font\" The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog Font size font-size takes any length. Browsers default to 16px. Set sizes in rem so they follow the visitor's text-size setting (1rem is 16px by default, 1.5rem 24px). Watch out for em on font sizes: it's relative to the parent's font size, so it compounds through nested elements. Heading sizes look best when they belong together. A type scale multiplies each level by the same ratio. Play"},{"id":"sec-heading-sizes-that-belong-together","title":"Heading sizes that belong together","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a base size and a ratio. base size ratio 1.125 (subtle) 1.25 (balanced) 1.333 (clear) 1.5 (bold) 1.618 (dramatic) Font style, font weight, text transform, and text decoration font-style: italic or normal. Use it to un-italicise, say, an <em>. font-weight: normal, bold, or a number from 100 to 900 (400 is normal, 700 bold), if the font has those weights. text-transform: uppercase, lowercase or capitalize. It changes how text looks, not the text itself, so a screen reader still reads the original. text-decoration: underline, overline, line-through, or none (often used on links). It's a shorthand, so underline wavy red works too. Text drop shadows text-shadow: 4px 4px 5px red sets the shadow's horizontal offset, vertical offset, blur and colour. Separate several shadows with commas. Play"},{"id":"sec-style-the-letters","title":"Style the letters","kind":"Play","text":"Mix and match. The CSS appears below. font-style normal italic font-weight 300 400 700 900 text-transform none uppercase lowercase capitalize text-decoration none underline underline wavy #b4410c line-through overline text-shadow none 2px 2px 0 #9ec2e6 4px 4px 5px red 1px 1px 1px red, 2px 2px 1px red 0 0 8px #2340d9 the Royal Arch trail Read"},{"id":"sec-text-layout","title":"Text layout","kind":"Read","text":"Text alignment text-align: left, right, center or justify. Left-align long paragraphs (in English); centred text is fine for a short heading but hard to read for more than a line or two, and justify can leave rivers of gaps. Line height line-height sets the height of each line. Write it without a unit, like 1.6, so it's a multiple of the font size and scales with it. Around 1.4 to 1.8 is comfortable for body text. Letter and word spacing letter-spacing and word-spacing add (or remove, with negative values) space between letters and words. A little extra letter spacing helps small uppercase text. Line length Not a property of its own, but it matters most: lines of about 45 to 75 characters read best. Limit paragraphs with max-width: 65ch, where ch is roughly one character wide. Play"},{"id":"sec-tune-the-text","title":"Tune the text","kind":"Play","text":"Adjust each setting and watch the readability checks respond. font-family Atkinson Hyperlegible Lora (serif) Georgia (serif) system-ui (your OS font) Arial Times New Roman monospace font-size line-height max-width (line length) font-weight 300 400 700 letter-spacing text-align left center justify right Why the trail closes in spring Every April, the lower section of the Mesa Trail closes for about six weeks. Snowmelt turns the clay soil into deep mud, and hikers who step around the puddles widen the trail and trample the wildflowers growing at its edges. Rangers reopen the trail once the ground can hold a footprint without sinking. Until then, the upper loop stays open and drains much faster. Other properties worth looking at There are many more: font-variant (small caps), text-indent, text-overflow (an ellipsis for cut-off text), white-space (whether text wraps), word-break, text-wrap: balance (evens out the lines of a heading) and writing-mode (vertical text). Look them up on MDN when you need them. Practice"},{"id":"sec-make-it-readable","title":"Make it readable","kind":"Practice","text":"The text is set in one long, cramped line. Fix the font, the spacing and the line length. Read"},{"id":"sec-font-shorthand","title":"Font shorthand","kind":"Read","text":"The font shorthand sets most font properties in one line. The order is strict: optional style, variant and weight first, then the required size (with an optional /line-height), then the required family last: font: italic bold 1.25rem/1.5 Georgia, serif; Leave out the size or the family and the whole declaration is ignored. And anything you leave out is reset to its default, so put font before any longhands you want to keep. Play"},{"id":"sec-expand-the-shorthand","title":"Expand the shorthand","kind":"Play","text":"Type a font value. The browser shows the longhands it sets, or ignores it. font: italic bold 1.25rem/1.5 Georgia, serif 1rem system-ui, sans-serif bold Georgia 300 2rem/1.1 \"Trebuchet MS\", sans-serif Royal Arch, 3.4 miles Practice"},{"id":"sec-a-trail-page-header","title":"A trail page header","kind":"Practice","text":"Style the heading and introduction."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"End every font stack with a generic family: serif, sans-serif or monospace. Use rem for font sizes; at least 1rem (16px) for body text. Unitless line-height around 1.4 to 1.8; lines of 45 to 75 characters. text-transform changes how text looks, not what it says. The font shorthand needs a size and a family, in that order, and resets what you leave out."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"A font name with spaces and no quotes, or a stack with no generic fallback. font-color or text-color: the property is color. Centring or justifying long paragraphs. Typing headings in capitals in the HTML instead of using text-transform. A font shorthand without a size, which throws the whole declaration away. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-text-styling/quiz"],"exercises":["core-text-styling/ex-type","core-text-styling/ex-header"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-web-fonts","num":"3.9","title":"Web Fonts","desc":"@font-face, self-hosting and Google Fonts, font-display, fallbacks, and keeping font downloads small.","props":["@font-face","woff2","font-display","fallback"],"intro":"You can only count on a handful of fonts being installed on every computer. Web fonts are downloaded with your page, so every visitor sees the type you chose, as long as you load them carefully.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-font-families-recap","title":"Font families recap","kind":"Read","text":"Last lesson's font-family only works with fonts on the visitor's machine, so a font stack lists alternatives, ending with a generic family: p { font-family: \"Helvetica\", \"Trebuchet MS\", \"Verdana\", sans-serif; } That limits you to the few web safe fonts, unless the font comes with the page. Read"},{"id":"sec-web-fonts","title":"Web fonts","kind":"Read","text":"A web font is a font file the browser downloads along with your CSS. You declare it with an @font-face rule, which gives the font a name and says where the file is, and then use that name like any other: @font-face { font-family: \"Trail Sans\"; src: url(\"fonts/trail-sans.woff2\") format(\"woff2\"); } html { font-family: \"Trail Sans\", \"Trebuchet MS\", sans-serif; } Two things to know: Use WOFF2. It's the compressed web font format every current browser supports. You don't need older formats like TTF or EOT. Fonts have licences. Fonts are software. A font installed on your computer isn't necessarily licensed for use on the web; check before you upload one. Free fonts from Google Fonts or Font Squirrel are licensed for the web. A web font is a download, and downloads take time or fail. Until it arrives, the browser uses the next font in your stack, so pick a fallback that's similar. Play"},{"id":"sec-always-include-a-fallback","title":"Always include a fallback","kind":"Play","text":"If the font fails to load, or hasn't loaded yet, the browser uses the next font in your list. Pick one that's similar. web font Merriweather Playfair Display Roboto Slab Source Sans 3 Space Mono Pacifico fallback serif sans-serif monospace cursive Pretend the font failed to load Weekend Hikes Near Boulder Pack water, layers and a map. Afternoon storms roll in fast above treeline, so start early and turn back by noon. Read"},{"id":"sec-adding-your-own-web-fonts","title":"Adding your own web fonts","kind":"Read","text":"Finding fonts Free fonts: Google Fonts, Font Squirrel, and the open-source fonts many foundries publish. Paid fonts come from foundries and services like MyFonts. Read the licence either way. Generating the required code If you have a font file in another format, a converter like Transfonter turns it into WOFF2 and writes the @font-face rules for you. Download only the weights and styles you'll use. Implementing the code in your demo Put the .woff2 files in a fonts/ folder in your project. Add an @font-face rule for each file at the top of your stylesheet. The src path is relative to the CSS file. Use the name in font-family, with fallbacks. @font-face { font-family: \"Zantroke\"; src: url(\"fonts/zantroke-webfont.woff2\") format(\"woff2\"); font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-display: swap; } h1 { font-family: \"Zantroke\", serif; } Practice"},{"id":"sec-self-host-a-font","title":"Self-host a font","kind":"Practice","text":"This project has a real font file in its fonts/ folder. Read"},{"id":"sec-using-an-online-font-service","title":"Using an online font service","kind":"Read","text":"Services like Google Fonts host the files and write the CSS for you. Choose a font and its weights, copy the <link> tags they give you into your <head> (above your own stylesheet), then use the name in font-family. It's the quickest way to start, though the visitor's browser now contacts another company's server, and the page depends on it being up. The easy way: Google Fonts Find a font at fonts.google.com and choose the weights you need. Copy the <link> tags it gives you into your page's <head>, above your own stylesheet. Use the font in CSS with font-family, followed by fallbacks. Self-hosting with @font-face Put the .woff2 files in a fonts/ folder in your project. Add an @font-face rule naming the font and pointing to the file. Use that name in font-family like any other font. Practice"},{"id":"sec-load-a-web-font-properly","title":"Load a web font properly","kind":"Practice","text":"The font names and fallbacks here are broken. Fix them so text always shows, in the right font when it arrives. Read"},{"id":"sec-font-face-in-more-detail","title":"@font-face in more detail","kind":"Read","text":"font-family: the name you'll refer to the font by. It can be anything, but must match exactly where you use it. src: the path to the file, with format(\"woff2\") so the browser can skip formats it doesn't support. List several files, comma-separated, and it uses the first it can. font-weight and font-style: which weight and style this file contains. To use regular and bold, write two @font-face rules with the same font-family and different font-weight. If you ask for a bold you didn't load, the browser fakes one by smearing the regular letters. font-display: what to show while the file downloads. swap shows the fallback straight away and swaps when the font arrives; it's the usual choice. Play"},{"id":"sec-what-visitors-see-while-it-loads","title":"What visitors see while it loads","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a font-display setting and simulate a slow connection where the font takes 2.5 seconds. font-display swap block fallback optional Simulate slow load Royal Arch Trail 0sfont arrives at 2.5s3s Press \"Simulate slow load.\" Play"},{"id":"sec-only-load-what-you-use","title":"Only load what you use","kind":"Play","text":"Every weight and style is a separate file to download. These sizes are rough estimates for a typical font. Extra download Variable fonts and other descriptors A variable font packs a whole range of weights (and sometimes other qualities) into a single file. Its @font-face gives a range, like font-weight: 300 1000, and then any weight in between works. Extra design axes are set with font-variation-settings. Two more descriptors you may meet: font-stretch (condensed to expanded) and unicode-range (download this file only if the page uses these characters). Play"},{"id":"sec-a-variable-font","title":"A variable font","kind":"Play","text":"Recursive, the font this book uses for code, is one variable file. Drag the weight, and try its extra axes. font-weight \"CASL\" (casual) \"MONO\" (monospace) Royal Arch, 3.4 miles"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"@font-face names a font and points to its file; then use the name in font-family. Use WOFF2, and check the licence. Always keep fallbacks in the stack, similar in style to the web font. font-display: swap shows text straight away while the font loads. Every weight and style is another download, unless it's a variable font."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"A font-family name that doesn't exactly match the @font-face name: it fails silently. A src path relative to the HTML page instead of the CSS file. Using font-weight: 700 after loading only 400, so the browser fakes a bold. Loading every weight a family offers \"just in case\". Uploading a font you don't have a web licence for. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-web-fonts/quiz"],"exercises":["core-web-fonts/ex-selfhost","core-web-fonts/ex-fonts"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-pseudo-classes","num":"4.1","title":"Pseudo-classes and Link States","desc":"Position and user-action pseudo-classes, the five link states in order, and turning links into a menu.","props":[":hover",":focus-visible",":nth-child()","a:visited"],"intro":"Some styles should apply only some of the time: while a link is hovered, when a field is focused, to the first item in a list. Pseudo-classes select elements by their state or position, without adding a single class to your HTML.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-is-a-pseudo-class","title":"What is a pseudo-class?","kind":"Read","text":"A pseudo-class is a keyword after a selector, starting with one colon, that selects elements only when they're in a certain state. It acts as if you'd added a class to the element at exactly the right moment, without touching the HTML. Basic pseudo-class example To make the first paragraph of each article larger, you could add class=\"first\" to it. But then if someone adds a new paragraph at the top, the class is on the wrong one. A pseudo-class always finds the right element: article p:first-child { font-size: 120%; font-weight: bold; } :first-child and :last-child match an element that's the first or last child of its parent. :nth-child() takes a pattern: odd, even, a number, or a formula like 3n+1. Watch out p:first-child means \"a p that is the first child of its parent\", not \"the first p\". If an h2 comes first, no paragraph matches. Play"},{"id":"sec-position-nth-child","title":"Position: :nth-child()","kind":"Play","text":"The formula an+b is calculated for n = 0, 1, 2, 3… and every result picks an item. li:nth-child( ) Read"},{"id":"sec-user-action-pseudo-classes","title":"User-action pseudo-classes","kind":"Read","text":"These match only while the visitor is doing something: :hover: while the pointer is over the element. Touch screens don't have it, so never hide anything important behind a hover. :focus: when the element is selected for keyboard input, by clicking a field or pressing Tab. :focus-visible: focus, but only when the browser thinks a visible ring helps, mainly keyboard use. :active: while it's being pressed. Many people use a keyboard instead of a mouse, so give every :hover style a matching :focus-visible one, in the same rule: .btn:hover, .btn:focus-visible { background: #1a2fa8; } Forms add more: :disabled, :checked, :required, :invalid. Watch out Never remove the focus outline with outline: none unless you add a clear :focus-visible style in its place. Without it, keyboard users can't see where they are. Play"},{"id":"sec-interactive-states","title":"Interactive states","kind":"Play","text":"Point at, click and tab to each control. button Save trail button disabled Sold out aView the trail map input type=\"email\" required input type=\"checkbox\" Dogs allowed Point at or tab to a control Try clicking the button with the mouse, then reaching it with the Tab key. Only the keyboard shows the :focus-visible ring. Practice"},{"id":"sec-style-the-states","title":"Style the states","kind":"Practice","text":"Stripes, hover feedback and a first-item highlight, all without adding a single class. Read"},{"id":"sec-link-states","title":"Link states","kind":"Read","text":"Links are the most-styled element with states. They have five: :link: a link with an href that hasn't been visited. :visited: one that's in the browser's history. :hover, :focus and :active, as above. Default styles Browsers underline links, colour them blue (purple once visited, red while active), show a pointer cursor, and draw a focus outline. All of it can be changed. Keep links recognisable, though: if a link looks like normal text, nobody will click it. Styling links Write the state rules in this order, because they have the same specificity and later rules win. If :hover came before :visited, visited links would never show the hover style. Remember it as LoVe Fears HAte: a { } /* every link */ a:link { } a:visited { } a:focus { } a:hover { } a:active { } For privacy, :visited can only change colours, not size or layout. Play"},{"id":"sec-style-your-own-links","title":"Style your own links","kind":"Play","text":"Edit the CSS, then hover over, tab to and click the links in the result. CSS Result Practice"},{"id":"sec-link-states-in-order","title":"Link states in order","kind":"Practice","text":"Style the trail links, with the states in an order that works. Read"},{"id":"sec-including-icons-on-links","title":"Including icons on links","kind":"Read","text":"A small icon can tell people where a link goes, like an arrow for links to other websites. Combine an attribute selector with the ::after pseudo-element (next lesson) to add one without touching the HTML: a[href^=\"http\"]::after { content: \" ↗\"; } Read"},{"id":"sec-styling-links-as-buttons","title":"Styling links as buttons","kind":"Read","text":"A navigation menu is just a list of links. Remove the bullets, put the items in a row, give each link padding so the whole box is clickable, and add hover and focus styles. Mark the current page with aria-current=\"page\" in the HTML, and style that attribute. Play"},{"id":"sec-building-a-menu","title":"Building a menu","kind":"Play","text":"Turn on each step. Remove bullets and indent Put the links in a row (flex) Make each link a padded block :hover and :focus-visible styles Mark the current page with aria-current Turn everything on Home Trails Maps Gear"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Pseudo-classes start with one colon and select by state or position. :first-child means \"first child of its parent\", whatever its type. Pair every :hover style with :focus-visible. Link states in order: :link, :visited, :focus, :hover, :active. A nav menu is a styled list of links; mark the current page with aria-current."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Removing the focus outline without a replacement. Putting :hover before :visited, so visited links never react. Expecting p:first-child to find the first paragraph after a heading. Hiding information or menus behind :hover only. Styling links so they look like plain text. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-pseudo-classes/quiz"],"exercises":["core-pseudo-classes/ex-states","core-pseudo-classes/ex-links"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-pseudo-elements","num":"4.2","title":"Pseudo-elements","desc":"Style parts of elements with ::first-line, ::first-letter and ::marker, and add decoration with ::before and ::after.","props":["::before","::after","content","::first-letter"],"intro":"Pseudo-elements select a part of an element, like its first line or first letter, or add something that isn't in the HTML at all, like a decorative icon. They're written with two colons.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-is-a-pseudo-element","title":"What is a pseudo-element?","kind":"Read","text":"A pseudo-element acts as if you'd wrapped part of the content in a new element and styled that. It's written with two colons, which is how you tell it apart from a pseudo-class. For example, to style the first line of a paragraph, you can't wrap it in a <span>: where the line ends depends on the screen width. ::first-line always finds it: article p::first-line { font-size: 120%; font-weight: bold; } Others select different parts: ::first-letter: the first letter, for a drop cap. ::marker: a list item's bullet or number. ::selection: text the visitor highlights. ::placeholder: the hint text in an input. Play"},{"id":"sec-switch-each-one-on","title":"Switch each one on","kind":"Play","text":"Turn each pseudo-element on and off to see exactly what it adds. Read"},{"id":"sec-combining-pseudo-classes-and-pseudo-elements","title":"Combining pseudo-classes and pseudo-elements","kind":"Read","text":"A selector can have both. To make only the first line of the first paragraph bold, put the pseudo-class first and the pseudo-element last: article p:first-child::first-line { font-weight: bold; } Play"},{"id":"sec-every-paragraph-or-only-the-first","title":"Every paragraph, or only the first?","kind":"Play","text":"Narrow the window to watch ::first-line follow the line break. (off) p::first-line p:first-child::first-line Royal Arch is a steep 3.4-mile round trip from Chautauqua Park to a sandstone arch with views over the plains. Start early in summer: afternoon storms build quickly over the Flatirons, and there's little shade on the upper switchbacks. Read"},{"id":"sec-generating-content-with-before-and-after","title":"Generating content with ::before and ::after","kind":"Read","text":"::before and ::after insert a new box at the start or end of an element's content, filled with whatever the content property says: .box::before { content: \"This should show before the other content. \"; } Every ::before and ::after needs content, even an empty one, content: \"\", or it doesn't appear at all. They go inside the element, as its first and last child, so they don't work on empty elements like <img> or <input>. Use them for decoration only. Text in content can't be selected or copied, and screen readers treat it inconsistently, so anything visitors need to read belongs in the HTML. Generated content icons A common use is a small decorative icon: .box::after { content: \" ➥\"; }. Generated shapes With empty content and display: block, a pseudo-element becomes a box you can size and colour: an underline bar, a coloured dot, a corner ribbon. .box::before { content: \"\"; display: block; width: 100px; height: 10px; background-color: rebeccapurple; } Play"},{"id":"sec-playing-with-generated-content","title":"Playing with generated content","kind":"Play","text":"Edit the content, move it before or after, or make it a shape. ::before ::after content: make it a shape (empty content, block, 100 × 10px) Trail conditions: dry Practice"},{"id":"sec-decorate-with-before-and-after","title":"Decorate with ::before and ::after","kind":"Practice","text":"Add three decorations with CSS alone. None of them belongs in the HTML. Practice"},{"id":"sec-parts-of-the-text","title":"Parts of the text","kind":"Practice","text":"Style parts that you couldn't select with any element."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Pseudo-elements use two colons; pseudo-classes use one. ::first-line, ::first-letter, ::marker and ::selection style parts of existing content. Pseudo-class first, pseudo-element last: p:first-child::first-line. ::before and ::after need a content property, even an empty one. Generated content is for decoration; real information goes in the HTML."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Forgetting content, so the pseudo-element never appears. Adding ::before to an <img> or <input>. Putting important text in content, where it can't be copied and may not be read aloud. Writing the pseudo-element before the pseudo-class: p::first-line:first-child. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-pseudo-elements/quiz"],"exercises":["core-pseudo-elements/ex-pseudo","core-pseudo-elements/ex-parts"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-custom-properties","num":"4.3","title":"CSS Custom Properties","desc":"Store values once with --name and var(), override them per section, add fallbacks, and register typed properties.","props":["--brand","var()",":root","@property"],"intro":"Type a colour forty times and changing it means forty edits. A custom property (often called a CSS variable) stores a value once, under a name, so every rule that uses it updates together.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-declaring-custom-properties","title":"Declaring custom properties","kind":"Read","text":"Using a prefix of two dashes (--) A CSS variable is a property whose name starts with two dashes. You declare it in a rule, like any other property: section { --main-bg-color: brown; } Names are case-sensitive: --Brand and --brand are different. Using the @property at-rule For more control, @property registers a custom property with a type, whether it inherits, and a starting value. More on that below. Referencing custom properties with var() To use the value, put its name in var(): background-color: var(--main-bg-color);. Writing color: --brand; does nothing: you always need var(). Read"},{"id":"sec-first-steps-and-the-root-pseudo-class","title":"First steps, and the :root pseudo-class","kind":"Read","text":"The usual pattern is to declare site-wide values once, on :root (the <html> element), so every element can use them: :root { --brand: #0b6b5e; --space: 16px; } .btn { background: var(--brand); padding: var(--space); } Change --brand in one place and every button, link and heading that uses it follows. The same trick gives you a dark theme: redefine just the colour variables inside @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark). Play"},{"id":"sec-change-a-variable","title":"Change a variable","kind":"Play","text":"Change one variable and watch every part of the site that uses it update. Then try the dark theme and the override. Variables on #site --brand --accent --radius --space Try these too Dark theme (redefines --bg, --text, --card) Featured card overrides --brand Define --sale Pick a variable above to outline everything that uses it. Trailhead3 new trails Royal Arch 3.4 mi · Hard Guided hike: 20% off Save Mesa Trail 6.7 mi · Featured Save Chautauqua 1.2 mi · Easy Save Practice"},{"id":"sec-use-a-variable","title":"Use a variable","kind":"Practice","text":"One brand colour is typed three times. Store it once, then make a sale card that changes it. Read"},{"id":"sec-inheritance-of-custom-properties","title":"Inheritance of custom properties","kind":"Read","text":"Custom properties inherit, like color. An element sees the value set on itself or on its nearest ancestor. That's what makes overrides work: set --brand on one .card, and that card and everything inside it use the new value, while the rest of the page doesn't change. Using @property to control inheritance A registered property can switch inheritance off. Then only the element it's set on gets the value; its children get the initial-value: @property --box-color { syntax: \"<color>\"; inherits: false; initial-value: #9aa2bf; } Play"},{"id":"sec-who-sees-the-value","title":"Who sees the value?","kind":"Play","text":"Four nested boxes, each with background: var(--box-color). Set the variable on one of them. box 1 box 2 box 3 registered with @property, inherits: false box 1 box 2 box 3 box 4 Read"},{"id":"sec-custom-property-fallback-values","title":"Custom property fallback values","kind":"Read","text":"Defining fallbacks in the var() function Add a second value to var(), after a comma, to use when the variable isn't defined: var(--sale, crimson). Fallbacks can nest: var(--my-var, var(--my-background, pink)). Fallbacks using the @property initial value A registered property also falls back to its initial-value when it's given a value of the wrong type, like peenk for a <color>. Read"},{"id":"sec-invalid-custom-properties","title":"Invalid custom properties","kind":"Read","text":"Normally an invalid declaration is simply skipped. Variables are different: the browser can't know whether var(--text-color) makes sense until it substitutes the value. So with :root { --text-color: 16px; }, the rule p { color: var(--text-color); } isn't skipped. It becomes invalid when used, and color falls back to its inherited value, not to your previous rule. (Registering the property with a <color> syntax prevents that.) Play"},{"id":"sec-what-does-var-resolve-to","title":"What does var() resolve to?","kind":"Play","text":"Choose what --my-var is set to. not defined #2340d9 16px The parent's text colour is #b4410c. Practice"},{"id":"sec-register-a-property","title":"Register a property","kind":"Practice","text":"Use @property to make a colour variable safe. Read"},{"id":"sec-values-in-javascript","title":"Values in JavaScript","kind":"Read","text":"JavaScript (Week 7) can read and change custom properties, which is how interactive themes work. Changing one variable restyles everything that uses it: // read getComputedStyle(element).getPropertyValue(\"--brand\"); // set element.style.setProperty(\"--brand\", \"#b4410c\"); Play"},{"id":"sec-set-a-variable-from-javascript","title":"Set a variable from JavaScript","kind":"Play","text":"Each button runs document.documentElement.style.setProperty(\"--demo-hue\", …). Everything below uses hsl(var(--demo-hue) 70% 40%). 20 160 225 300 Trail ClubJoin a hikeNext: Saturday at 8am"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Custom properties start with -- and are read with var(). Declare global values on :root; override them on any element for that part of the page. They inherit, unless registered with @property and inherits: false. var(--x, fallback) covers an undefined variable. A wrong-type value isn't skipped: the property falls back to inherited or initial."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"color: --brand; without var(). Mismatched case: --Brand versus --brand. Declaring a variable on one element and expecting a sibling to see it. A space after var( in the name, or a missing --. Expecting a bad value in a variable to be ignored like a normal invalid declaration. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-custom-properties/quiz"],"exercises":["core-custom-properties/ex-vars","core-custom-properties/ex-prop"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-backgrounds-borders","num":"4.4","title":"Backgrounds and Borders","desc":"Background colours, images, gradients and layers; repeat, size, position and attachment; borders and rounded corners.","props":["background","linear-gradient()","border","border-radius"],"intro":"Backgrounds fill a box with colour, images or gradients, stacked in as many layers as you like. Borders frame it, and rounded corners soften it. Together they're most of what makes a plain box look designed.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-background-colors","title":"Background colors","kind":"Read","text":"background-color fills the content and padding of a box (the area inside the border). It works on any element, including inline ones like a <span>. A semi-transparent colour, like rgb(255 255 255 / 50%), lets whatever's behind show through. Read"},{"id":"sec-background-images","title":"Background images","kind":"Read","text":"background-image: url(\"star.png\") puts an image behind the content. By default, it's shown at its natural size and tiled to fill the box. A background colour shows through wherever the image is transparent or doesn't reach. Controlling background-repeat no-repeat, repeat-x (tile across), repeat-y (tile down), repeat (the default), plus space and round, which fit a whole number of tiles. Sizing the background image background-size takes lengths or percentages (80px 10em), or a keyword: cover fills the whole box, cropping the image if needed; contain fits the whole image inside, leaving gaps. Positioning the background image background-position takes keywords (top center), lengths and percentages (20px 10%), or edge offsets: top 20px right 10px is 20px from the top and 10px from the right. Watch out An empty element has no height, so its background is invisible. Backgrounds fill the box; give the box some content, padding or a height. Play"},{"id":"sec-build-a-trail-card","title":"Build a trail card","kind":"Play","text":"Combine a background and a border into a card, then see how size, position and corner radius change it. Background type solid color linear-gradient radial-gradient image image + gradient overlay repeating image color 1 color 2 gradient angle background-size cover contain auto 50% background-position center top bottom left right background-repeat no-repeat repeat repeat-x Border border-width border-style solid dashed dotted double groove border-color border-radius Only round the top-left and bottom-right corners Mesa Trail 6.7 miles · Moderate Read"},{"id":"sec-gradient-backgrounds","title":"Gradient backgrounds","kind":"Read","text":"A gradient is an image that CSS draws, so it goes in background-image, not background-color: .a { background-image: linear-gradient(105deg, rgb(0 249 255) 39%, rgb(51 56 57) 96%); } .b { background-image: radial-gradient(circle, rgb(0 249 255) 39%, rgb(51 56 57) 96%); } A linear gradient runs along an angle; a radial one spreads out from a point. Each colour can have a stop position, a percentage along the way. Read"},{"id":"sec-multiple-background-images","title":"Multiple background images","kind":"Read","text":"background-image takes a comma-separated list. The first layer is drawn on top. The other background properties take lists too, matched up in the same order: .hero { background-image: url(\"star.svg\"), linear-gradient(135deg, #0b6b5e, #2340d9); background-repeat: no-repeat, no-repeat; background-position: right 12px top 12px, center; } A classic use: a dark gradient over a photo, so white text on top stays readable. Play"},{"id":"sec-playing-with-multiple-background-images","title":"Playing with multiple background images","kind":"Play","text":"Reorder the layers. The one at the top of the list is drawn on top. Read"},{"id":"sec-background-attachment","title":"Background attachment","kind":"Read","text":"background-attachment decides what the background scrolls with: scroll (default): fixed to the element; when the page scrolls, it moves with the element, but if the element itself scrolls, the background stays put. fixed: fixed to the window, so content slides over it. Used for \"parallax\" effects. local: fixed to the element's content, so it scrolls along with the text inside. Play"},{"id":"sec-scroll-the-box","title":"Scroll the box","kind":"Play","text":"Choose a value, then scroll inside the box. scroll local fixed Mesa Trail runs along the base of the Flatirons for 6.7 miles. It rolls up and down through meadows and pine forest, crossing several small creeks. Spring brings wildflowers; autumn brings golden grass and clear views east across the plains. Several side trails climb to the Flatirons themselves. Parking at both ends fills early on weekends. Dogs are allowed on leash. Read"},{"id":"sec-using-the-background-shorthand-property","title":"Using the background shorthand property","kind":"Read","text":"background sets everything at once, with layers separated by commas. Two rules: the colour can only go in the last layer, and a size must come straight after the position, with a slash: center / 80%. .box { background: url(\"star.svg\") no-repeat right 10px top 10px / 40px, linear-gradient(105deg, rgb(255 255 255 / 20%) 39%, rgb(51 56 57 / 100%) 96%) center / 400px 200px no-repeat, rebeccapurple; } Read"},{"id":"sec-accessibility-considerations-with-backgrounds","title":"Accessibility considerations with backgrounds","kind":"Read","text":"Text on top of a background image must still have enough contrast, everywhere the text might land, at every screen size. Set a background colour too, so text stays readable if the image doesn't load. And background images are for decoration: screen readers ignore them, so anything that carries meaning belongs in an <img> with alt text. Practice"},{"id":"sec-layers-and-the-shorthand","title":"Layers and the shorthand","kind":"Practice","text":"One background declaration, two layers and a colour. Read"},{"id":"sec-borders","title":"Borders","kind":"Read","text":"A border needs a width, a style and a colour: border: 1px solid black. Leave out the style and nothing appears, because the default is none. Every part has longhands: one side (border-top), one quality (border-width), or both (border-bottom-style): .box { border: 1px solid black; border-bottom: 1em double rgb(24 163 78); } Styles include solid, dashed, dotted, double, groove and ridge. Read"},{"id":"sec-rounded-corners","title":"Rounded corners","kind":"Read","text":"border-radius: 10px rounds all four corners, even without a border. Each corner has a longhand, like border-top-right-radius, and two values make an elliptical corner: border-top-right-radius: 1em 10%. border-radius: 50% turns a square into a circle. Practice"},{"id":"sec-backgrounds-and-borders","title":"Backgrounds and borders","kind":"Practice","text":"A gradient banner, a round avatar and a rounded card."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"background-color fills inside the border; images and gradients go in background-image. background-size: cover fills and crops; contain fits the whole image. Multiple backgrounds are comma-separated; the first is on top. In the background shorthand, the colour goes last and size follows position after a slash. Borders need a style; border-radius rounds corners, 50% makes a circle."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"A gradient in background-color, where it's ignored. A background on an empty element with no height. Text over a photo with no overlay or fallback colour, so it's unreadable. border: 2px navy with no style. Putting meaningful images in CSS backgrounds, where screen readers can't see them. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-backgrounds-borders/quiz"],"exercises":["core-backgrounds-borders/ex-layers","core-backgrounds-borders/ex-bg"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-shadows-effects","num":"4.5","title":"Shadows and Effects","desc":"Box and text shadows, filters, blend modes, CSS shapes and gradient text.","props":["box-shadow","filter","mix-blend-mode","shape-outside"],"intro":"Shadows add depth, filters change how an element is drawn, and blend modes mix layers like a photo editor. Used lightly, they give a page polish; overdone, they make it hard to read.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-box-shadows","title":"Box shadows","kind":"Read","text":"A simple box shadow box-shadow takes up to five values: horizontal offset, vertical offset, blur, spread, and a colour. .simple { box-shadow: 5px 5px 5px rgb(0 0 0 / 70%); } The best shadows are barely noticeable. Use a semi-transparent colour rather than solid black, and let it fall slightly downward, as if lit from above. A shadow is painted, not laid out: it takes no space and never pushes other boxes away. Multiple box shadows Separate several shadows with commas. Stacking a tight, darker one with a wide, faint one looks much more natural than either alone: .card { box-shadow: 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 12%), 0 8px 24px rgb(0 0 0 / 10%); } Other box shadow features The inset keyword draws the shadow inside the box, which makes it look pressed in. Swapping an outer shadow for an inset one on :active is a classic \"pressed button\" effect. Play"},{"id":"sec-box-shadow","title":"box-shadow","kind":"Play","text":"A shadow has an x offset, a y offset, a blur, a spread and a colour. Try the presets, then adjust them yourself. x offset y offset blur spread color opacity inset (shadow inside the box) Add a second, tighter shadow layer Mesa Trail 6.7 miles · Moderate · Dogs on leash Text shadows text-shadow works the same way, without spread or inset. Its best use is keeping big headings readable on a busy photo; on body text it just looks blurry. Play"},{"id":"sec-text-shadow-for-readable-text-on-images","title":"text-shadow for readable text on images","kind":"Play","text":"blur shadow darkness Busy background Above Treeline Practice"},{"id":"sec-add-depth-with-shadows","title":"Add depth with shadows","kind":"Practice","text":"Make the card float with a soft, natural shadow, and lift the heading off the photo. Read"},{"id":"sec-filters","title":"Filters","kind":"Read","text":"The filter property changes how an element is drawn, like a photo editor: blur(10px), grayscale(60%), brightness(), contrast(), invert(), hue-rotate(), sepia(). It applies to the whole element, text and children included. filter: drop-shadow() is a shadow that follows the element's actual shape, including transparent areas of an image, where box-shadow always follows the rectangle. Look"},{"id":"sec-box-shadow-vs-filter-drop-shadow","title":"box-shadow vs filter: drop-shadow()","kind":"Look","text":"box-shadow follows the element's rectangle. drop-shadow follows the shape you can see. box-shadow The shadow outlines the image's square box. filter: drop-shadow() The shadow traces the mountain's shape. Play"},{"id":"sec-other-filters","title":"Other filters","kind":"Play","text":"filter none grayscale(1) sepia(1) blur(4px) brightness(0.6) contrast(1.6) saturate(2) hue-rotate(120deg) A common use: show photos in grayscale and bring the color back on hover. Read"},{"id":"sec-blend-modes","title":"Blend modes","kind":"Read","text":"Blend modes decide how overlapping colours mix, as in Photoshop: multiply darkens, screen lightens, overlay boosts contrast, difference inverts. background-blend-mode Blends an element's own background layers together, like a background image with its background colour: background-blend-mode: multiply tints the image with the colour. mix-blend-mode Blends a whole element with whatever is behind it on the page. Play"},{"id":"sec-try-each-blend-mode","title":"Try each blend mode","kind":"Play","text":"The striped box has a green background colour under a stripe image; the purple circle overlaps it. background-blend-mode (striped box) normal multiply screen overlay difference luminosity mix-blend-mode (circle) normal multiply screen overlay difference exclusion Mix Read"},{"id":"sec-css-shapes","title":"CSS shapes","kind":"Read","text":"Text normally wraps around a floated element's rectangle. shape-outside makes it wrap around a shape instead, such as a circle: img { float: left; shape-outside: circle(50%); } Play"},{"id":"sec-wrap-text-around-a-circle","title":"Wrap text around a circle","kind":"Play","text":"Switch shape-outside on and off. shape-outside: circle(50%) The sun sets behind the Flatirons around eight in midsummer, painting the sandstone orange. Hikers on the Mesa Trail stop to watch the light change, then hurry down before dark. Notice how these lines hug the curve of the circle when the shape is on, and stay square when it's off. Read"},{"id":"sec-webkit-background-clip-text","title":"-webkit-background-clip: text","kind":"Read","text":"background-clip decides how far a background reaches. Its text value clips the background to the letters, so a gradient or image fills the text. Make the text itself transparent to see it: h2 { background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, #0b6b5e, #2340d9, #b4410c); -webkit-background-clip: text; background-clip: text; -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent; } The -webkit- prefix is a vendor prefix: a browser-specific version from before the feature was standardised. Some browsers still need it here, so write both. Check that the text stays readable, and that a colour shows if the effect isn't supported. Play"},{"id":"sec-gradient-text","title":"Gradient text","kind":"Play","text":"Switch clipping on. background-clip: text and transparent text Above Treeline Practice"},{"id":"sec-effects-on-a-trail-card","title":"Effects on a trail card","kind":"Practice","text":"A pressed button, a grayscale photo and gradient text."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"box-shadow: x, y, blur, spread, colour; stack several with commas. Shadows take no space; inset draws them inside. filter edits how a whole element is drawn; drop-shadow() follows its shape. background-blend-mode mixes an element's own layers; mix-blend-mode mixes it with the page. background-clip: text fills letters with a background; keep a readable fallback."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Solid black shadows, or shadows so big they look like smudges. text-shadow on body text. Relying on a shadow alone to separate a card from its background, especially in dark mode. Gradient text with no fallback colour, which can vanish entirely. Blend modes that destroy text contrast. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-shadows-effects/quiz"],"exercises":["core-shadows-effects/ex-shadow","core-shadows-effects/ex-effects"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-transforms","num":"4.6","title":"Transforms","desc":"Translate, rotate, scale and skew; transform-origin and order; individual transform properties; and 3D perspective.","props":["transform","rotate","transform-origin","perspective"],"intro":"Transforms move, turn, resize and slant an element after the page is laid out, without nudging anything around it. That makes them ideal for hover effects, icons and small bits of motion.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-css-transforms-properties","title":"CSS transforms properties","kind":"Read","text":"The transform property takes one or more functions, separated by spaces: .badge { transform: translateY(-4px) rotate(-3deg) scale(1.1); } translate() moves it (translateX(), translateY()). rotate() turns it, in deg or turn. A number with no unit does nothing. scale() resizes it: 1 is normal size, 2 double, 0.5 half. skew() slants it (skewX(), skewY()). Transforms are drawn after layout, so the browser keeps the element's original space and nothing around it moves. A card can grow on hover without shoving its neighbours. transform-origin sets the point the element turns and scales around. The default is the centre; transform-origin: top left makes it pivot from the corner, like a door on a hinge. Play"},{"id":"sec-transform-a-card","title":"Transform a card","kind":"Play","text":"Drag the sliders and watch the dashed outline: that's the space the card still takes up in the layout. #box { transform } translateX translateY rotate scale skewX transform-origin center top left top right bottom left bottom center Reset sibling #box sibling Copy Individual transform properties There are also separate translate, rotate and scale properties. They're easier to read, and they don't replace each other. With the transform property, a hover rule that sets transform: scale(1.1) wipes out an earlier rotate(-3deg). With separate properties, scale: 1.1 just adds to rotate: -3deg. .stamp { rotate: -3deg; } .stamp:hover { scale: 1.1; } /* still rotated */ Play"},{"id":"sec-transform-vs-individual-properties","title":"transform vs individual properties","kind":"Play","text":"Both cards start tilted. Turn on the hover state. apply the :hover rule transform rotate + scale Read"},{"id":"sec-examples","title":"Examples","kind":"Read","text":"Rotating rotate: 90deg; transform-origin: bottom left; swings an element a quarter-turn around its bottom-left corner instead of its centre. Skewing and translating When you combine functions, order matters. Each function works in the space left by the ones before it: rotate first, and \"right\" now points diagonally, so a following translateX() moves the element diagonally too. Play"},{"id":"sec-same-functions-different-order","title":"Same functions, different order","kind":"Play","text":"Transform functions apply from right to left, and each one changes the direction the next one uses. transform: translateX(140px) rotate(45deg); A Turn in place, then slide 140px right along the page. transform: rotate(45deg) translateX(140px); B Turn first, so \"right\" is now tilted 45° downward, and it slides along that tilted line. Play both Look"},{"id":"sec-four-everyday-uses","title":"Four everyday uses","kind":"Look","text":"Center anything centered .badge { position: absolute; top: 50%; left: 50%; transform: translate(-50%, -50%); } top/left: 50% puts the corner in the middle. The translate pulls it back by half its own size. Lift on hover Hover or Tab to me .card { transition: transform 0.2s; } .card:hover { transform: translateY(-6px); } Turn an arrow Trail conditions Icy above 7,000 feet. details[open] summary::before { transform: rotate(90deg); } Mirror an icon ➜➜ .icon-back { transform: scaleX(-1); } One arrow icon serves as both \"next\" and \"back.\" Practice"},{"id":"sec-transform-without-moving-the-layout","title":"Transform without moving the layout","kind":"Practice","text":"Tilt a stamp, lift a card on hover, and swing a door from its edge. Read"},{"id":"sec-3d-specific-css-properties","title":"3D specific CSS properties","kind":"Read","text":"Transforms also work in three dimensions: rotateX() and rotateY() tip an element towards or away from you, and translateZ() moves it nearer. Without perspective, though, 3D looks flat. Perspective Set perspective on the parent: the distance between the viewer and the page. A small value (250px) is like holding your face close, with dramatic distortion; a large one (1000px) is subtle. perspective-origin moves the viewer's eye: left, top right and so on. .scene { perspective: 600px; } .card { transform: rotateY(40deg); } Play"},{"id":"sec-perspective","title":"Perspective","kind":"Play","text":"Turn the card in 3D, then change how close the viewer is. rotateY perspective (on the parent) perspective-origin center left right top bottom right remove perspective Trail pass Practice"},{"id":"sec-individual-properties-and-3d","title":"Individual properties and 3D","kind":"Practice","text":"A tilted badge that grows, and a card turned in 3D."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Transforms change how an element is drawn, not the space it takes. Functions in one transform apply right to left, and order changes the result. A second transform replaces the first; the individual rotate, scale and translate properties combine. transform-origin moves the pivot point. 3D needs perspective on the parent."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"rotate(45) with no unit. Overriding a rotation by setting transform: scale() on hover. Using transforms to build layout: the space doesn't move with the element. Putting perspective on the element being rotated instead of its parent. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-transforms/quiz"],"exercises":["core-transforms/ex-transform","core-transforms/ex-3d"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-transitions","num":"4.7","title":"Transitions","desc":"Smooth changes between states: transition properties, what can animate, display transitions, reduced motion and transition events.","props":["transition","ease-out","@starting-style","transitionend"],"intro":"By default a style change is instant: hover a button and its colour snaps. A transition tells the browser to blend from the old value to the new one over time, so the change feels smooth instead of abrupt.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-which-css-properties-can-be-transitioned","title":"Which CSS properties can be transitioned?","kind":"Read","text":"A transition works by calculating the in-between values, so the property needs values that have an in-between: colours, lengths, numbers, transforms, opacity. Properties with on/off values, like display or font-family, just switch. And auto can't be transitioned: height: 0 to height: auto jumps. Play"},{"id":"sec-does-it-animate","title":"Does it animate?","kind":"Play","text":"Each box has transition: all 0.8s. Toggle each property. Read"},{"id":"sec-defining-transitions","title":"Defining transitions","kind":"Read","text":"The transition shorthand takes the property, the duration, a timing function and a delay: .btn { background: #0b6b5e; transition: background-color 200ms ease, transform 200ms ease; } .btn:hover { background: #084c43; transform: translateY(-2px); } transition-property: which properties to animate. Name them, rather than all, so nothing unexpected moves. transition-duration: how long, in s or ms. About 150 to 400ms feels right for interface changes. transition-timing-function: the speed curve, like ease, linear, ease-out. transition-delay: how long to wait before starting. Put the transition on the element's normal state, not on :hover, so it animates on the way in and the way out. Your device is set to reduce motion. This page plays its demos anyway so you can learn from them, but the rest of this site turns motion off for you. Your own sites should do the same with a prefers-reduced-motion media query. Play"},{"id":"sec-transitions","title":"Transitions","kind":"Play","text":"Change the duration, curve and delay, then change state. what changes position (left) color and shape size rotation (transform) transition-duration transition-timing-function ease linear ease-in ease-out ease-in-out back (overshoot) steps(5) transition-delay Change state Speed curve Same timing on hover Hover or Tab to me Read"},{"id":"sec-examples","title":"Examples","kind":"Read","text":"Multiple animated properties List several transitions with commas: transition: width 2s, height 2s, background-color 2s, rotate 2s;. When property value lists are of different lengths With the longhands, the lists are matched up by position. If transition-duration has fewer values than transition-property, its values repeat to fill the gap; extra values are ignored. transition-property: opacity, left, top, height; transition-duration: 3s, 5s; /* = 3s, 5s, 3s, 5s */ Using transitions when highlighting menus A short transition on menu links, transition: background-color 0.3s ease-out, color 0.3s ease-out;, makes hover and focus states feel smooth. Transitioning display Normally an element switching from display: none to display: block can't fade in: it doesn't exist until the switch. Two newer features fix that. allow-discrete lets display take part in the transition, and @starting-style says what the element looks like the moment it appears, so it has something to transition from: .panel { opacity: 0; display: none; transition: opacity 0.6s, display 0.6s allow-discrete; } .panel.showing { opacity: 1; display: block; } @starting-style { .panel.showing { opacity: 0; } } Play"},{"id":"sec-fade-in-from-display-none","title":"Fade in from display: none","kind":"Play","text":"This uses @starting-style and allow-discrete, in browsers that support them. Show trail alert Royal Arch is closed above the switchbacks after today's rockfall. Practice"},{"id":"sec-smooth-menu-links","title":"Smooth menu links","kind":"Practice","text":"Add transitions to a navigation menu. Read"},{"id":"sec-keyframe-animations-and-reduced-motion","title":"Keyframe animations and reduced motion","kind":"Read","text":"A transition needs a trigger, like a hover or a class change. An animation runs by itself, through steps you describe with @keyframes: @keyframes pulse { from { transform: scale(1); } to { transform: scale(1.08); } } .new-badge { animation: pulse 800ms ease-in-out infinite alternate; } Some people get dizzy or sick from motion on screen, and set their device to reduce it. Wrap big or looping motion in @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) { … }, so it only runs for people who haven't asked for less. Play"},{"id":"sec-keyframe-animations","title":"Keyframe animations","kind":"Play","text":"animation-name pulse spin slide-in shake bounce animation-duration animation-iteration-count 1 3 infinite animation-direction normal alternate reverse Play animation box Practice"},{"id":"sec-add-motion-carefully","title":"Add motion carefully","kind":"Practice","text":"Smooth a hover, add a pulse, and respect visitors who've asked for less motion. Read"},{"id":"sec-javascript-examples","title":"JavaScript examples","kind":"Read","text":"Using transitions to make JavaScript functionality smooth When JavaScript changes a style, a transition in the CSS animates it, with no animation code in your script. Set transition: transform 1s on a ball, and every time JavaScript sets a new transform, the ball glides there. Detecting the start and completion of a transition Transitions fire events you can listen for: transitionrun (it's set up), transitionstart (after any delay), and transitionend (finished). Each tells you the propertyName and the elapsedTime. Play"},{"id":"sec-click-to-move-the-ball","title":"Click to move the ball","kind":"Play","text":"JavaScript sets the transform; the CSS transition animates it. The log shows the events. Click the track."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Transitions blend between two states; animations run through keyframes on their own. Only properties with in-between values animate; auto and display normally snap. Put transition on the normal state, name the properties, and keep it to 150–400ms. transform and opacity animate most smoothly. Respect prefers-reduced-motion."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"transition only on :hover, so it snaps back. transition: all, which animates things you didn't mean to. Trying to transition height to auto. Slow, bouncy motion on every interaction. Looping animations with no reduced-motion fallback. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-transitions/quiz"],"exercises":["core-transitions/ex-menu","core-transitions/ex-motion"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-styling-lists-tables-forms","num":"4.8","title":"Styling Lists, Tables and Forms","desc":"List markers and spacing, readable data tables, images that fit their boxes, and form controls that match the page.","props":["list-style","border-collapse","object-fit","font: inherit"],"intro":"Lists, tables, images and form controls each come with their own default styling quirks. This lesson tames them: custom bullets, readable tables, images that fit their boxes, and forms that match the rest of the page.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-styling-lists","title":"Styling lists","kind":"Read","text":"Handling list spacing Browsers give lists a top and bottom margin and about 40px of left padding, which is where the bullets sit. Set the spacing yourself so lists line up with your paragraphs: matching line-height and margins on p and li keeps the rhythm even. List-specific styles list-style-type: the bullet or numbering style: disc, circle, square, decimal, lower-alpha, upper-roman, none, or any string like \"→ \". list-style-position: outside (the default; the marker hangs in the padding) or inside (the marker sits in the text, which wraps under it). list-style-image: an image as the bullet. You can't size it, so many people use ::marker or a background image instead. list-style: the shorthand, as in list-style: square inside;. Controlling list counting In the HTML, <ol start=\"4\"> starts counting at 4, reversed counts down, and <li value=\"2\"> sets one item's number. Play"},{"id":"sec-list-styles","title":"List styles","kind":"Play","text":"Change the marker type and position. Watch where the marker sits relative to the dashed edge of the list. ul list-style-type disc circle square \"→ \" none ol list-style-type decimal lower-alpha upper-roman decimal-leading-zero list-style-position outside inside padding-left Water, at least two litres, plus a filter if you're heading somewhere remote Layers Park at Chautauqua Follow the Bluebell Road Turn left at the sign Practice"},{"id":"sec-styling-a-nested-list","title":"Styling a nested list","kind":"Practice","text":"Different markers for each kind of list. Read"},{"id":"sec-styling-tables","title":"Styling tables","kind":"Read","text":"A well-structured table (Week 2) is the starting point. Then a handful of rules makes it readable: Font: a clean sans-serif is easiest to scan. Spacing: border-collapse: collapse merges double borders; padding on cells gives the data room; table-layout: fixed with a width makes column widths predictable. Alignment: left-align text, right-align numbers so their digits line up, and vertical-align: top for cells of different heights. :nth-child() picks the column. Borders: a few lines, like one under the header and one above the totals, beat a grid of boxes. Zebra striping: tbody tr:nth-child(odd) shades alternate rows, so eyes don't slip to the wrong row. Caption: caption-side: bottom moves it under the table. Play"},{"id":"sec-style-the-table-one-step-at-a-time","title":"Style the table one step at a time","kind":"Play","text":"Turn on each step. Font Spacing: fixed layout, collapse, padding Alignment: numbers right Borders: header and footer only Zebra stripes Caption at the bottom Turn everything on Boulder trails, by visits Trail Visits (2025) Notes Mesa Trail 48,200 Busy on weekends Royal Arch 30,950 Steep; closed after storms Bear Peak 12,400 Long day Sanitas 27,800 Dog friendly Total 119,350 Practice"},{"id":"sec-a-readable-data-table","title":"A readable data table","kind":"Practice","text":"Apply the table-styling basics. Read"},{"id":"sec-images-and-media","title":"Images and media","kind":"Read","text":"Replaced elements Images and videos are replaced elements: their content comes from an outside file, and CSS can't change what's inside them, only the box they sit in. Sizing images An image is shown at its natural size, so a large photo overflows its container. One rule stops that everywhere: img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; } Handling image display issues with object-fit When you force an image into a box of a different shape, it stretches. object-fit controls how it fills the box instead: cover fills it and crops; contain fits the whole image and leaves gaps; fill (the default) stretches. Replaced elements in layout In layouts like Grid, items normally stretch to fill their area, but images keep their own proportions. Give the image width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; if you want it to fill its cell. Look"},{"id":"sec-object-fit","title":"object-fit","kind":"Look","text":"The same wide image in three square boxes. Practice"},{"id":"sec-thumbnails-that-fit","title":"Thumbnails that fit","kind":"Practice","text":"Make three images of different shapes into tidy square thumbnails. Read"},{"id":"sec-styling-form-elements","title":"Styling form elements","kind":"Read","text":"Challenges in styling form widgets Form controls started out drawn by the operating system, not the browser, and some still are. They fall into three groups: Easy to style: text inputs, <textarea>, <button>, <fieldset> and <legend> behave like ordinary boxes. Harder: checkboxes and radio buttons. accent-color recolours them in one line. Mostly unstylable inside: colour and date pickers, range sliders, file inputs, <select> dropdowns, <progress> and <meter>. You can size the box, but not much of what's inside. Normalising form behaviour: a reset Form controls don't inherit the page's font, and their sizing differs between browsers. A short reset fixes both: button, input, select, textarea { font-family: inherit; font-size: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; } textarea { overflow: auto; } Styling text inputs Then style them like any box: border, padding, border-radius. Put each label above its input, make the input full width, and give :focus-visible a clear outline. Keep controls recognisable: an input that doesn't look like an input won't get filled in. Watch out Don't show errors with colour alone. Some visitors can't tell red from green. Add a text message next to the field explaining what's wrong. Play"},{"id":"sec-improve-the-form-one-layer-at-a-time","title":"Improve the form one layer at a time","kind":"Play","text":"Step through the layers and watch the same form get easier to use. Try the fields at each step. Name Please enter your name. Email That doesn't look like an email address. Favorite trail Royal Arch Mesa Trail Chautauqua Loop Message Optional. Tell us about your last hike. Email me when new trails are added Join the club Practice"},{"id":"sec-style-the-form","title":"Style the form","kind":"Practice","text":"Fix the fonts, the overflow and the missing focus style."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"list-style-type, -position and -image (or ::marker) style list markers. Tables: collapse borders, pad cells, right-align numbers, stripe rows with :nth-child(odd). img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; } stops images overflowing. object-fit: cover fills a box without stretching the image. Form controls need font: inherit and box-sizing: border-box to match the page."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Removing list padding with markers outside, so the bullets disappear off the edge. Centring numbers in a table, so digits don't line up. Setting a width and height on an image without object-fit, so it stretches. Restyling form controls until they no longer look like form controls. Removing the focus outline from inputs. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-styling-lists-tables-forms/quiz"],"exercises":["core-styling-lists-tables-forms/ex-list","core-styling-lists-tables-forms/ex-table","core-styling-lists-tables-forms/ex-thumbs","core-styling-lists-tables-forms/ex-formstyle"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-sizing","num":"5.1","title":"Sizing","desc":"Intrinsic and extrinsic sizes, percentages (including margins and padding), min- and max- sizes, images that fit, and viewport units.","props":["max-width","min-height","padding: 10%","vh"],"intro":"Every box has a size, whether it comes from its content or from you. Knowing which, and choosing values that adapt instead of fixed numbers, is the difference between a layout that works on every screen and one that only works on yours.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-the-natural-or-intrinsic-size-of-things","title":"The natural or intrinsic size of things","kind":"Read","text":"HTML elements have a natural size before any CSS touches them. An image is as big as the file's pixel dimensions. A paragraph is as tall as its lines of text. This is its intrinsic size: it comes from the content. An empty <div> has no content, so it has no height at all. Give it a border and you just see a flat line, the top and bottom borders touching. Put some text inside and it grows to fit. Play"},{"id":"sec-where-size-comes-from","title":"Where size comes from","kind":"Play","text":"Put different content in the same box, then give it a fixed height. Empty A little text Lots of text An image add height: 120px The next element Read"},{"id":"sec-setting-a-specific-size","title":"Setting a specific size","kind":"Read","text":"You can override the intrinsic size with width and height. That's an extrinsic size: it comes from outside the content. A box with height: 120px stays 120px tall whatever is inside, and if the text needs more room it overflows. (The next lesson, Overflow, covers what to do about that.) Using percentages A percentage width is a share of the containing block, usually the parent. width: 50% is half of whatever contains it, so the box grows and shrinks with its parent. That's much safer than a fixed width like width: 1000px, which sticks out on a 390px phone and makes the whole page scroll sideways. The pattern you'll use constantly combines a percentage with a limit: .content { width: 100%; max-width: 600px; } On a phone the box fills the screen. On a big screen it stops at 600px, which keeps lines of text short enough to read. Percentage heights are trickier: they only work if the parent has a set height, which it usually doesn't. Play"},{"id":"sec-width-fixed-flexible-and-limited","title":"Width: fixed, flexible and limited","kind":"Play","text":"Drag the parent's width and compare how each box responds. The numbers are measured live. parent width Watch the 400px box when the parent gets narrower than 400px. no width (auto) width: 50% width: 400px width: 100%; max-width: 400px width: 30%; min-width: 180px fit-content Percentage margins and padding Here's a surprise: percentage margin and padding are calculated from the containing block's width, even on the top and bottom. So padding: 10% gives the same amount of space on all four sides, and that space changes when the parent gets wider, not taller. .box { margin: 10%; /* 10% of the parent's width, on every side */ padding: 10%; } Play"},{"id":"sec-what-10-means","title":"What 10% means","kind":"Play","text":"Change the parent's width. The top and bottom spacing changes too, even though the parent's height hasn't. parent width margin and padding 5% 10% 15% Dashed: the parent. Solid: the child's border box. The shaded band is its padding. content Read"},{"id":"sec-min-and-max-sizes","title":"min- and max- sizes","kind":"Read","text":"Instead of a fixed size, you can set a floor or a ceiling and let the content decide the rest. Text wraps onto more lines as the screen narrows, so a box with a fixed height eventually can't hold it. min-height says \"at least this tall\", and the box still grows when the content needs it. max-width works the other way: the box can be narrower but never wider. min-width sets a floor for width. Play"},{"id":"sec-height-vs-min-height","title":"height vs min-height","kind":"Play","text":"Add more text and see which box copes. paragraphs of text height: 120px Content below the box min-height: 120px Content below the box max-width on images An image is drawn at its intrinsic size, so a 1200px photo in a 400px column sticks out. You could set width: 100%, but that also stretches small images past their natural size, and they go blurry. The standard fix is: img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; /* keep the shape when it shrinks */ } Now big images shrink to fit their container, and small ones stay at their natural size. Play"},{"id":"sec-width-100-vs-max-width-100","title":"width: 100% vs max-width: 100%","kind":"Play","text":"This image is 120px wide. Make the container bigger and smaller than that. container width rule on the image (nothing) width: 100% max-width: 100% Practice"},{"id":"sec-size-boxes-that-adapt","title":"Size boxes that adapt","kind":"Practice","text":"Fixed sizes break on other screens. Make each of these flexible. Read"},{"id":"sec-viewport-units","title":"Viewport units","kind":"Read","text":"The viewport is the visible part of the page in the browser window. Viewport units size things relative to it, not to the parent: 1vw is 1% of the viewport's width. 1vh is 1% of the viewport's height. So min-height: 100vh makes a banner that fills the screen, and width: 50vw is half the window wide whatever its parent is. On phones, where browser toolbars appear and disappear, 100dvh adjusts for them. A related tool is aspect-ratio. Set a width and aspect-ratio: 16 / 9, and the browser works out the height, which is perfect for video and image placeholders. Play"},{"id":"sec-aspect-ratio-and-viewport-units","title":"aspect-ratio and viewport units","kind":"Play","text":"The second box is sized in vh. Resize your browser window to see it change. aspect-ratio 16 / 9 4 / 3 1 / 1 3 / 4 width height in vh Practice"},{"id":"sec-percentages-viewport-units-and-images","title":"Percentages, viewport units and images","kind":"Practice","text":"A banner sized by the window, padding that scales with the page, and an image that fits."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Without a set size, a box takes its intrinsic size from its content. An empty block has no height. Percentage widths are a share of the containing block. width: 100%; max-width: 600px fills phones and caps big screens. Percentage margins and padding use the containing block's width, even on the top and bottom. Prefer min-height to height for boxes with text, and max-width: 100% for images. vw and vh are 1% of the viewport's width and height."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Big fixed widths like width: 1000px that overflow on phones. A percentage height that does nothing because the parent has no set height. Expecting padding-top: 10% to be 10% of the parent's height. Using width: 100% on images, which stretches small ones until they blur. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-sizing/quiz"],"exercises":["core-sizing/ex-sizing","core-sizing/ex-vp"],"questions":7},{"slug":"core-overflow","num":"5.2","title":"Overflow","desc":"Why CSS shows overflow by default, the overflow values, each axis on its own, hidden vs clip, and finding the element that makes a page scroll sideways.","props":["overflow: auto","overflow: clip","overflow-x","text-overflow"],"intro":"Overflow happens when content is bigger than its box. CSS doesn't hide it by default: it lets it spill out so you notice. Here's how to control it on each axis, and how to track down the element that makes a whole page scroll sideways.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-is-overflow","title":"What is overflow?","kind":"Read","text":"Overflow is what happens when there's too much content to fit in a box: a long paragraph in a card with a fixed height, a giant image in a narrow column, or a long URL with no spaces to break at. It only happens when a box's size is constrained. A box with no set height just grows to fit its content, as you saw in Sizing. Set height: 100px, though, and anything beyond 100px has to go somewhere. Read"},{"id":"sec-css-tries-to-avoid-data-loss","title":"CSS tries to avoid \"data loss\"","kind":"Read","text":"By default, CSS lets overflowing content spill out of its box, visibly, even when it overlaps whatever comes next. That looks messy, but it's deliberate: if CSS silently hid the extra content, you might never notice that part of your page was missing. A mess is a problem you'll see and fix. The lesson for your own CSS: be careful about fixing the height of anything that holds text. Text gets longer when it's translated, when someone sets a bigger default font, or when the screen gets narrower. Let the content decide the height wherever you can. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-overflow-property","title":"The overflow property","kind":"Read","text":"When you do need to control overflow, the overflow property decides what happens: visible, the default, lets it spill out. clip cuts off anything outside the box. hidden also cuts it off, but the box can still be scrolled by code. scroll always shows scrollbars. auto shows scrollbars only when they're needed. It's usually the right choice. Play"},{"id":"sec-make-the-content-fit","title":"Make the content fit","kind":"Play","text":"Change overflow and the box's height, then fix the long title and the long link. overflow visible hidden scroll auto clip height Use min-height instead of height Title: one line with an ellipsis (…) Long link: overflow-wrap: anywhere Chautauqua to Royal Arch via the Bluebell Mesa Trail Climb through ponderosa pines to a natural sandstone arch with views across Boulder. The last half mile is steep, rocky and has over 200 stone steps. https://example.org/trails/boulder/royal-arch-via-bluebell-mesa-trail-map Bring water: there's none on the trail. Read"},{"id":"sec-hiding-overflowing-content","title":"Hiding overflowing content","kind":"Read","text":"overflow: clip cuts content off at the edge of the box (its padding edge, just inside the border). The cut-off part can't be reached at all, so only use it for things nobody needs to read, like a decorative shape that pokes out of a banner. If clipping right at the edge looks too tight, overflow-clip-margin lets the content spill a little way past the edge before it's cut: .banner { overflow: clip; overflow-clip-margin: 10px; } Read"},{"id":"sec-scrolling-overflowing-content","title":"Scrolling overflowing content","kind":"Read","text":"overflow: auto keeps everything reachable: if the content doesn't fit, the box gets a scrollbar; if it does, there's no scrollbar at all. It's the safe choice for a box that has to stay a fixed size, like a chat window or a long list in a sidebar. .trail-log { height: 200px; overflow: auto; } Read"},{"id":"sec-controlling-overflow-on-each-axis","title":"Controlling overflow on each axis","kind":"Read","text":"overflow is a shorthand for two properties: overflow-x (side to side) and overflow-y (up and down). You can set them separately, or give overflow two values, x first: .code-sample { overflow-x: auto; } /* scroll sideways only */ .strip { overflow: clip auto; } /* clip x, scroll y */ One combination isn't allowed: a box can't scroll in one direction and spill visibly in the other. If one axis is scroll, auto or hidden, a visible on the other axis is quietly turned into auto. Play"},{"id":"sec-two-axes","title":"Two axes","kind":"Play","text":"This box has a long line (too wide) and several paragraphs (too tall). Set each axis and watch what the browser actually uses. overflow-x visible clip hidden scroll auto overflow-y visible clip hidden scroll auto https://example.org/trails/boulder/mesa-trail-full-map.pdf The Mesa Trail runs 6.7 miles along the foot of the Flatirons. It links a dozen side trails, so you can make a loop of almost any length. Long words and one-line titles Two text problems come up constantly. A long URL or word has no spaces to break at, so it pushes past its box. overflow-wrap: anywhere lets it break where it needs to. And a title that must stay on one line can end in an ellipsis (…) instead of spilling: .title { white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; } Read"},{"id":"sec-always-displaying-scrollbars","title":"Always displaying scrollbars","kind":"Read","text":"overflow: scroll shows scrollbars even when there's nothing to scroll, on systems that show scrollbars all the time. That's rarely what you want, but it does stop the layout jumping sideways when content grows and a scrollbar suddenly appears. A better way to get that stability is overflow: auto plus scrollbar-gutter: stable, which reserves room for the scrollbar without drawing an empty one. .panel { overflow: auto; scrollbar-gutter: stable; } Read"},{"id":"sec-the-hidden-value","title":"The hidden value","kind":"Read","text":"overflow: hidden looks just like clip: extra content is cut off and there are no scrollbars. The difference is that a hidden box is still a scroll container. JavaScript can scroll it, and so can keyboard focus moving to a link inside the hidden part. That can leave the box scrolled to an odd position with no way for the user to scroll it back. Use clip when you just want to cut content off, and hidden only when you're going to scroll the box with code (a carousel, for example). Play"},{"id":"sec-hidden-vs-clip","title":"hidden vs clip","kind":"Play","text":"Both boxes cut off their content. Now try scrolling each one with code. Scroll both down 40px with JavaScript Reset overflow-clip-margin (clip box) overflow: hidden 1. Chautauqua trailhead 2. Bluebell shelter 3. Royal Arch junction 4. The stone steps 5. Royal Arch overflow: clip 1. Chautauqua trailhead 2. Bluebell shelter 3. Royal Arch junction 4. The stone steps 5. Royal Arch Read"},{"id":"sec-unwanted-overflow-in-web-design","title":"Unwanted overflow in web design","kind":"Read","text":"Most overflow you meet won't be a box you chose to constrain. It'll be a bug: text spilling out of a card that looked fine with the sample text, or a whole page that scrolls sideways on a phone because one element is wider than the screen (a fixed width: 400px, a big image, a long link). To catch these early, test your layouts with much more and much less content than you expect, and with a larger browser font size. When you find overflow, fix the cause instead of hiding it. Watch out overflow: hidden on the body hides the sideways scroll, but the content is still too wide and now some of it is unreachable. Find the element that's too wide and make it flexible. Practice"},{"id":"sec-find-what-s-making-the-page-scroll-sideways","title":"Find what's making the page scroll sideways","kind":"Practice","text":"This phone-sized page scrolls left and right. Click each part of the page to inspect its width, find the one wider than the screen, then fix it. Trailhead Fall hours: trailheads open at 6am. Parking fills by 8am on weekends. Trail · Miles · Gain · Difficulty · Dogs · Shade · Water MapSave Inspector Click a part of the page on the left. Practice"},{"id":"sec-tame-the-overflow","title":"Tame the overflow","kind":"Practice","text":"Three boxes, three kinds of overflow. Pick the right fix for each. Practice"},{"id":"sec-clip-scroll-one-way-and-shorten","title":"Clip, scroll one way, and shorten","kind":"Practice","text":"A decorative shape, a long line of code and a title that must stay on one line."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Overflow only happens when a box's size is constrained. Let content decide the height where you can. By default overflow is visible, so you notice the problem instead of losing content. auto adds scrollbars only when needed; clip cuts content off; hidden cuts it off but can still be scrolled by code. overflow-x and overflow-y control each axis. A scrolling axis turns visible on the other into auto. overflow-wrap: anywhere breaks long words; text-overflow: ellipsis shortens one-line text."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Fixed pixel widths, like width: 380px, wider than a phone screen. Putting overflow: hidden on body to hide the sideways scroll instead of fixing the cause. Fixed height on a text box that later gets more text, translated text, or larger font settings. Using overflow: scroll, which shows scrollbars even when nothing overflows. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-overflow/quiz"],"exercises":["core-overflow/ex-overflow","core-overflow/ex-axes"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-flexbox","num":"5.3","title":"Flexbox","desc":"The flex model, direction and wrapping, sharing space with the flex shorthand, alignment, ordering, and nested flex containers.","props":["display: flex","flex: 1","justify-content","align-self"],"intro":"Flexbox lays out items in one direction, a row or a column. You set the rules on the container and its children follow them, sharing out space, lining up and wrapping as the room changes.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-why-flexbox","title":"Why flexbox?","kind":"Read","text":"For a long time, CSS had no good way to do some very common things: center a box vertically inside its parent, make several columns share the available width equally, or make columns the same height whatever their content. People used floats and workarounds that broke easily. Flexbox was designed for exactly these jobs. It lays items out along one line, a row or a column, and gives you control over how they share space and line up. Read"},{"id":"sec-specifying-what-elements-to-lay-out-as-flexible-boxes","title":"Specifying what elements to lay out as flexible boxes","kind":"Read","text":"Add display: flex to a parent, and its direct children line up in a row: section { display: flex; } The parent becomes the flex container, and its children become flex items. Only direct children: grandchildren are laid out normally unless their own parent is also a flex container. Almost every rule goes on the container, which is also the most common mistake. display: flex goes on the parent, not on the items you want to move. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-flex-model","title":"The flex model","kind":"Read","text":"Every flex container has two directions: The main axis runs the way items flow. With the default flex-direction: row, that's left to right. Its two ends are the main start and main end, and an item's length along it is its main size. The cross axis runs at a right angle to it, so up and down in a row, from cross start to cross end. An item's length along it is its cross size. Most flex properties work along one axis or the other, so keep track of which way the main axis runs. Read"},{"id":"sec-columns-or-rows","title":"Columns or rows?","kind":"Read","text":"flex-direction sets the main axis: row (the default), column, or row-reverse and column-reverse, which run backwards. Switch to column and the axes swap: the main axis now runs top to bottom. Read"},{"id":"sec-wrapping","title":"Wrapping","kind":"Read","text":"Flex items don't wrap by default. When they run out of room they shrink, and if they can't shrink any more, they overflow the container. flex-wrap: wrap lets them move onto a new line instead. Each new line is laid out on its own. Read"},{"id":"sec-flex-flow-shorthand","title":"flex-flow shorthand","kind":"Read","text":"flex-flow sets both at once, direction first: .cards { display: flex; flex-flow: row wrap; } Play"},{"id":"sec-try-every-container-setting","title":"Try every container setting","kind":"Play","text":"Change one property at a time and watch the items move. Then switch to a column and see which controls swap jobs. .container flex-direction row row-reverse column column-reverse justify-content flex-start center flex-end space-between space-around space-evenly align-items stretch center flex-start flex-end baseline flex-wrap nowrap wrap gap 12px Items number of items 4 .item:first-child { flex-grow } 0 (don't grow) 1 (fill extra space) Read"},{"id":"sec-flexible-sizing-of-flex-items","title":"Flexible sizing of flex items","kind":"Read","text":"Now for the items themselves. The flex property on an item says how much of the container's spare space it should take: article { flex: 1; } /* every article gets an equal share */ article:nth-child(3) { flex: 2; } /* this one gets two shares */ The number has no unit: it's a proportion. With flex: 1, flex: 1 and flex: 2, the space is split into four shares, and the third item gets two of them. You can add a starting size too. flex: 1 200px means \"start at 200px, then share out whatever space is left\". That's useful for items that shouldn't get too narrow. Read"},{"id":"sec-flex-shorthand-versus-longhand","title":"flex: shorthand versus longhand","kind":"Read","text":"flex is a shorthand for three properties: flex-grow: how many shares of spare space the item takes (the unitless number). flex-shrink: how much it shrinks, compared with the others, when there isn't enough room. flex-basis: its starting size before space is shared out. Use the shorthand: it sets sensible values for the parts you leave out. flex: 1 is 1 1 0% (grow, shrink, start from nothing), while the initial value is 0 1 auto (don't grow, but do shrink, and start from the content's size). flex: none is 0 0 auto: a fixed size that neither grows nor shrinks. Play"},{"id":"sec-share-out-the-space","title":"Share out the space","kind":"Play","text":"Give each item a flex value, then change the container's width. container width item A item B item C Read"},{"id":"sec-horizontal-and-vertical-alignment","title":"Horizontal and vertical alignment","kind":"Read","text":"You've already met the two container properties in the playground above: align-items lines items up on the cross axis. The default, stretch (its keyword is normal), makes every item as tall as the tallest, which is how flexbox gives you equal-height columns. flex-start, center, flex-end and baseline keep each item's own height. justify-content spaces items along the main axis: flex-start, center, flex-end, space-between (no space at the ends), space-around (half-size space at the ends) and space-evenly. To align just one item differently, put align-self on it. It takes the same values as align-items. (There's no justify-self in flexbox: a single item's main-axis position comes from the free space, so use margin-left: auto to push one item to the end.) .row { display: flex; align-items: center; } .row .tall { align-self: stretch; } Read"},{"id":"sec-ordering-flex-items","title":"Ordering flex items","kind":"Read","text":"The order property changes where an item appears without changing the HTML. Every item starts at order: 0. Items with lower values come first; items with the same value keep their source order. Negative numbers work too, so order: -1 moves an item to the front. Use it sparingly. order only changes what people see: keyboard users still tab through the items in source order, and screen readers still read them in source order. If the order matters, change the HTML. Play"},{"id":"sec-align-self-and-order","title":"align-self and order","kind":"Play","text":"Set an order and alignment for each item, then click the first item and press Tab to see which order the keyboard uses. order align-self 1 2 3 4 Practice"},{"id":"sec-sizing-order-and-alignment","title":"Sizing, order and alignment","kind":"Practice","text":"A sidebar that keeps its width, a main column and an extra panel that share the rest, in a different visual order. Read"},{"id":"sec-nested-flex-boxes","title":"Nested flex boxes","kind":"Read","text":"A flex item can be a flex container too. That's how most real layouts are built: a header is a flex row (logo on one side, nav on the other), and the nav inside it is another flex row of links. .site-nav { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; } .site-nav ul { display: flex; gap: 16px; } Each container only lays out its own direct children, so you can mix rows, columns and wrapping at every level. Note Flexbox handles one direction at a time. For rows and columns that line up together, use Grid. Flexbox or Grid? builds the same layouts both ways. Practice"},{"id":"sec-build-a-nav-bar","title":"Build a nav bar","kind":"Practice","text":"Most site headers are a flex row inside a flex row. Try it on a real nav."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"display: flex goes on the parent; its direct children become flex items. justify-content works along the main axis, set by flex-direction; align-items works along the cross axis. Items don't wrap unless you add flex-wrap: wrap. flex-flow sets direction and wrap together. flex: 1 shares out spare space; flex: 1 200px starts at 200px first. flex is shorthand for grow, shrink and basis. align-self aligns one item; order moves it visually but not for the keyboard."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Putting display: flex on the items instead of their parent. Expecting align-items: center to center vertically when the container has no extra height. Forgetting flex-wrap: wrap, so items squish instead of moving to a new line. Using order to fix content that's in the wrong order in the HTML. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-flexbox/quiz"],"exercises":["core-flexbox/ex-flex","core-flexbox/ex-nav"],"questions":7},{"slug":"core-grid","num":"5.4","title":"Grid","desc":"Tracks, fr and gaps, explicit and implicit grids, minmax(), placing items by line number and by named areas, and subgrid.","props":["grid-template-columns","minmax()","grid-column: 1 / -1","grid-template-areas"],"intro":"Grid controls rows and columns at the same time. Define the tracks on the container, and items fill the cells in order, or go exactly where you place them by line number or by name.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-is-grid-layout","title":"What is grid layout?","kind":"Read","text":"A grid is a set of horizontal and vertical lines that make columns and rows. Designers have used them on paper for centuries, because they make a page look consistent: things line up from section to section. CSS Grid lets you build that structure in the browser. Flexbox lines items up in one direction; Grid handles two at once. The rows and columns are called tracks, and the spaces between them are gutters (in CSS, gap). Read"},{"id":"sec-creating-your-grid-in-css","title":"Creating your grid in CSS","kind":"Read","text":"Defining a grid display: grid on the container makes its direct children grid items, but on its own it just stacks them in one column. Add columns with grid-template-columns: .container { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 200px 200px 200px; } Flexible grids with the fr unit Fixed pixel columns don't adapt to the screen. The fr unit means \"a fraction of the available space\", so 1fr 1fr 1fr makes three equal columns at any width, and 2fr 1fr 1fr makes the first twice as wide as the others. You can mix units: in 200px 1fr, the 200px column is sized first and the 1fr column takes what's left. Gaps between tracks column-gap and row-gap add space between tracks, and the gap shorthand sets both. Gaps can be lengths or percentages, but not fr. Repeating track listings repeat(3, 1fr) is a shortcut for 1fr 1fr 1fr. You can repeat a pattern too: repeat(2, 2fr 1fr) is 2fr 1fr 2fr 1fr. Play"},{"id":"sec-build-a-grid","title":"Build a grid","kind":"Play","text":"Change the columns, gap and alignment, then make the first item span more cells. .container grid-template-columns repeat(3, 1fr) repeat(4, 1fr) 1fr 2fr 1fr 200px 1fr repeat(auto-fit, minmax(120px, 1fr)) gap 12px justify-items stretch start center end align-items stretch start center end Items number of items 7 .item-1 { grid-column } span 1 span 2 span 3 .item-1 { grid-row } span 1 span 2 Implicit and explicit grids The tracks you define with grid-template-columns and grid-template-rows make the explicit grid. When there are more items than cells, the browser adds extra rows to hold them: that's the implicit grid. Implicit rows are sized auto (as tall as their content) unless you set grid-auto-rows: .container { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); grid-auto-rows: 100px; /* every implicit row is 100px */ } The minmax() function A fixed row height has the same problem as any fixed height: content that needs more room overflows. minmax(100px, auto) means \"at least 100px, but taller if the content needs it\". Play"},{"id":"sec-explicit-implicit-and-minmax","title":"Explicit, implicit and minmax()","kind":"Play","text":"Solid items sit in explicit rows; dashed ones are in rows the browser added. Turn on the long text and compare row sizes. grid-template-rows none 60px 60px grid-auto-rows auto 40px minmax(40px, auto) number of items long text in item 8 As many columns as will fit Put repeat() and minmax() together and you get the most useful line of Grid: .cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)); } Read it as: \"fit as many columns as you can, each at least 220px wide, and share any extra space equally.\" On a phone you get one column, on a laptop four, with no media queries. Pick the last option in the Build a grid playground above and resize your window to watch it. Read"},{"id":"sec-line-based-placement","title":"Line-based placement","kind":"Read","text":"So far items have filled the cells in order. You can also place an item exactly, using the numbered lines between tracks. A grid with 4 columns has 5 column lines, numbered from 1 at the left (in English). Lines also count backwards from the end: -1 is always the last line. header { grid-column: 1 / -1; /* from the first line to the last: full width */ grid-row: 1; } article { grid-column: 2 / 4; /* from line 2 to line 4: two columns */ } grid-column is shorthand for grid-column-start and grid-column-end; grid-row works the same way. Instead of an end line you can say how many tracks to cover: grid-column: 2 / span 2. Play"},{"id":"sec-place-an-item-by-line-number","title":"Place an item by line number","kind":"Play","text":"Type a value for grid-column and grid-row, or pick a preset. The numbers show the lines. grid-column grid-row header sidebar span 2 last cell 12345 1234 Practice"},{"id":"sec-build-a-photo-grid","title":"Build a photo grid","kind":"Practice","text":"Three equal columns, with one featured photo that takes up four cells. Read"},{"id":"sec-positioning-with-grid-template-areas","title":"Positioning with grid-template-areas","kind":"Read","text":"Another way to place items is to draw the layout with names. grid-template-areas takes one quoted string per row, with one name per column. Then each item says which area it goes in with grid-area: .page { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 3fr; grid-template-areas: \"header header\" \"sidebar content\" \"footer footer\"; } header { grid-area: header; } aside { grid-area: sidebar; } main { grid-area: content; } footer { grid-area: footer; } The rules are strict: Every row must have the same number of cells, and every cell must be filled. Repeat a name to make an area span cells. Use . for an empty cell. Each area must be a rectangle: no L-shapes, and no area in two separate places. If a rule is broken, the whole declaration is ignored. Play"},{"id":"sec-draw-a-layout-with-names","title":"Draw a layout with names","kind":"Play","text":"Edit the strings, or try a preset. The column widths are 110px 1fr 90px. grid-template-areas classic tall nav no nav broken header nav main aside footer Practice"},{"id":"sec-lay-out-a-page-with-named-areas","title":"Lay out a page with named areas","kind":"Practice","text":"A header, a sidebar beside the main content, a footer, and a notice placed by line numbers. Read"},{"id":"sec-nesting-grids-and-subgrid","title":"Nesting grids and subgrid","kind":"Read","text":"A grid item can be a grid container too, just like nested flex boxes. A card in a page grid might use its own grid to lay out its title, text and button. The catch is that a nested grid's tracks know nothing about its neighbours. If three cards sit side by side and one has a two-line title, its text starts lower than the others. subgrid fixes this: the nested grid uses its parent's tracks instead of making its own. .card { display: grid; grid-row: span 3; /* take three rows of the parent grid */ grid-template-rows: subgrid; /* and use those rows for title, text, button */ } Play"},{"id":"sec-line-up-the-cards-with-subgrid","title":"Line up the cards with subgrid","kind":"Play","text":"Each card has a title, some text and a button. Turn subgrid on and off. use subgrid rows Mesa Trail Rolling and shady. Details Chautauqua to Royal Arch via Bluebell A steep climb to a sandstone arch, with over 200 stone steps at the end. Details Bear Peak Big climb, bigger views. Details Read"},{"id":"sec-grid-frameworks","title":"Grid frameworks","kind":"Read","text":"Before CSS Grid, people used grid frameworks: libraries of classes that faked a grid, often with 12 or 16 columns. Grid makes most of them unnecessary. If you want a 12-column design grid, it's one line, and items are placed with line numbers: .page { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr); gap: 20px; } header { grid-column: 1 / -1; } /* all 12 */ aside { grid-column: 1 / 4; } /* 3 columns */ main { grid-column: 4 / -1; } /* the other 9 */"},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"display: grid and the track lists go on the container. fr shares out the available space; repeat() saves typing. Tracks you define are the explicit grid. Extra rows the browser adds are implicit, sized by grid-auto-rows. minmax(100px, auto) sets a minimum but lets the track grow. repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)) fits as many columns as there's room for. Place items by line number (grid-column: 1 / -1) or by name (grid-template-areas + grid-area). subgrid lets a nested grid share its parent's tracks, so content lines up across cards."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Setting grid-template-columns on the items instead of the container. A grid-template-areas layout with an uneven row or an L-shaped area, so the whole thing is ignored. Mixing up lines and tracks: grid-column: 1 / 3 covers two columns, not three. Fixed grid-auto-rows heights that make text overflow. Use minmax(). Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-grid/quiz"],"exercises":["core-grid/ex-grid","core-grid/ex-areas"],"questions":8},{"slug":"core-flex-or-grid","num":"5.5","title":"Flexbox or Grid?","desc":"One dimension or two, content out or layout in: choosing the right tool for a layout, and nesting flex containers inside a grid.","props":["1D vs 2D","content out","layout in","margin-top: auto"],"intro":"Both can build most layouts, but each has a sweet spot. Flexbox arranges items along one line and lets their content decide their size. Grid lays out rows and columns together. Real pages use both, often one inside the other.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-one-dimension-or-two","title":"One dimension or two?","kind":"Read","text":"You can build most layouts with either Flexbox or Grid, which is why people argue about which is better. They're not competitors. Each has a sweet spot. The key difference is dimensions. Flexbox is one-dimensional: it arranges items along a single row or column. When flex items wrap onto a second line, that line is laid out on its own, so items in different lines don't line up with each other. Grid is two-dimensional: you define columns and rows, and items line up in both directions at once. The classic sign you picked wrong is a card gallery built with Flexbox: the last row has two cards, and they stretch to fill the whole width. With Grid, they keep the same width as the cards above. Read"},{"id":"sec-content-out-or-layout-in","title":"Content out, or layout in?","kind":"Read","text":"A second way to decide is to ask where the sizes come from. Content out: the items' own sizes should decide the layout. A row of buttons, a logo next to a nav, tags that wrap like words. That's Flexbox. Layout in: you draw the rows and columns first and place content into them. A card gallery, a page with a sidebar, a photo layout where one item spans several cells. That's Grid. A few things only one of them can do. Only Grid can make an item span several rows, and only Grid keeps columns lined up across wrapped rows. Flexbox is simpler for a single row whose items are different sizes, and for pushing one item to the end with margin-left: auto. Tip Ask yourself: \"Do I care about columns lining up?\" If yes, use Grid. If you just need things in a row, spaced nicely, use Flexbox. Play"},{"id":"sec-compare-the-same-layout","title":"Compare the same layout","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a layout and see it built both ways. Change the container width to see where each one struggles. container width Flexbox Grid Rule of thumb Content out: if the items' sizes should decide the layout (a row of buttons, tags, a navbar), use Flexbox. Layout in: if you want to draw the rows and columns first and drop items into them (a gallery, a page, a dashboard), use Grid. Real sites use both, often nested. Practice"},{"id":"sec-sort-the-layouts","title":"Sort the layouts","kind":"Practice","text":"For each layout, pick the tool that fits best. Read"},{"id":"sec-using-them-together","title":"Using them together","kind":"Read","text":"Most real pages use both. A common pattern: Grid for the overall structure (the page, a gallery of cards), and Flexbox inside each piece (the card's header, its row of buttons). .cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)); gap: 16px; } .card { display: flex; flex-direction: column; } .card .btn { margin-top: auto; /* push the button to the bottom of the card */ } Grid makes every card in a row the same height. Inside each card, a flex column with margin-top: auto on the button pushes the button to the bottom, so the buttons line up even when the text above them is different lengths. Practice"},{"id":"sec-grid-outside-flex-inside","title":"Grid outside, flex inside","kind":"Practice","text":"The cards are already a grid. Lay out each card with Flexbox. Practice"},{"id":"sec-pick-the-right-tool","title":"Pick the right tool","kind":"Practice","text":"This page uses Flexbox for everything. One part should be Grid."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Flexbox is one-dimensional: a row or a column. Wrapped lines don't line up with each other. Grid is two-dimensional: columns line up from row to row. Content out (sizes from the items) → Flexbox. Layout in (tracks first, then content) → Grid. Only Grid can make one item span several rows and columns. It's normal to put a flex container inside a grid item, or a grid inside a flex item."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Building a card gallery with Flexbox and getting a stretched last row. Adding lots of wrapper divs to fake a two-dimensional layout with Flexbox. Using Grid for a simple row of buttons and fighting fixed column widths. Thinking one is \"better.\" They solve different problems. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-flex-or-grid/quiz"],"exercises":["core-flex-or-grid/ex-nest","core-flex-or-grid/ex-choose"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-positioning","num":"5.6","title":"Positioning","desc":"Static, relative, absolute, fixed and sticky; positioning contexts and z-index; and practical examples: stacked tab panels and a sliding panel.","props":["position: absolute","position: sticky","z-index","top / left"],"intro":"The position property takes an element out of the normal flow and puts it somewhere else: nudged a little, pinned to a corner of its card, stuck to the window, or stuck to the top while you scroll.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-introducing-positioning","title":"Introducing positioning","kind":"Read","text":"Normally every element sits where the normal flow puts it, one after another. The position property lets you move an element from there, for things that normal flow, Flexbox and Grid can't do: a badge over the corner of a photo, a header that stays at the top of the window, a menu that slides in over the page. Once an element is positioned, the offset properties top, right, bottom and left move it. The question each position value answers is: measured from where? Try them all first, then we'll go through each one. Play"},{"id":"sec-try-every-position-value","title":"Try every position value","kind":"Play","text":"Change the position value and offsets, then scroll inside the window to see how each one behaves. #box position static relative absolute fixed sticky top left z-index .parent position: relative browser window .parent sibling 1 #box sibling 2 · z-index 1 Scroll inside the window above. The window has its own scrollbar. Read"},{"id":"sec-static-positioning","title":"Static positioning","kind":"Read","text":"position: static is the default. The element sits in the normal flow, and top, left and z-index have no effect. You'll rarely write it, except to undo another position value. Read"},{"id":"sec-relative-positioning","title":"Relative positioning","kind":"Read","text":"position: relative keeps the element in the normal flow, then lets you nudge it from there. Its original space stays reserved, so nothing around it moves; the element can end up overlapping its neighbours. Introducing top, bottom, left, and right The offsets can feel backwards. top: 30px pushes the element 30px away from its top edge, so it moves down. left: 30px moves it right. To move up or left, use bottom and right, or a negative value. .positioned { position: relative; top: 30px; /* 30px down */ left: 30px; /* 30px right */ } On its own, relative positioning is mostly used for small nudges. Its big job is the next one: being the reference point for absolutely positioned children. Read"},{"id":"sec-absolute-positioning","title":"Absolute positioning","kind":"Read","text":"Setting position: absolute position: absolute takes the element out of the normal flow completely. It no longer takes up space: the elements after it close up as if it weren't there. It still has its own margins, but they don't collapse with anything. Positioning contexts An absolute element's offsets are measured from its containing block: the nearest ancestor that is itself positioned (anything but static). If there isn't one, it's the initial containing block, roughly the size of the window at the top of the page. That's why you'll see this pattern everywhere. To pin a badge to a card's corner, make the card the positioning context: .card { position: relative; } /* the context */ .card .badge { position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; } Without position: relative on the card, the badge looks for the next positioned ancestor up the page and often ends up in the corner of the whole page. Practice"},{"id":"sec-pin-a-badge-to-a-card","title":"Pin a badge to a card","kind":"Practice","text":"Place a badge in each card's corner, so it stays put whatever the card's size. Introducing z-index Positioned elements can overlap. By default, the one that comes later in the HTML is drawn on top. z-index changes that: higher numbers come forward. It takes whole numbers with no unit, including negatives; the default, auto, acts like 0. z-index only works on positioned elements (and on flex and grid items). On a static element it does nothing. Play"},{"id":"sec-which-card-is-on-top","title":"Which card is on top?","kind":"Play","text":"Three overlapping, absolutely positioned cards. Give them z-index values. card A z-index auto 0 1 2 3 -1 card B z-index auto 0 1 2 3 -1 card C z-index auto 0 1 2 3 -1 A B C Read"},{"id":"sec-fixed-positioning","title":"Fixed positioning","kind":"Read","text":"position: fixed works like absolute, but it's positioned against the viewport (the browser window), not an ancestor. It stays in the same place on screen while the page scrolls under it. That's how headers, chat buttons and cookie banners stay visible. .site-header { position: fixed; top: 0; width: 100%; } main { margin-top: 60px; } /* so the header doesn't cover the start of the page */ Because a fixed element is out of the flow, the content underneath doesn't know it's there. Leave room for it, or it will cover the top of your page. Read"},{"id":"sec-sticky-positioning","title":"Sticky positioning","kind":"Read","text":"Basic example position: sticky is a mix of relative and fixed. The element scrolls with the page normally until it reaches its offset, then it sticks there, until its parent scrolls out of view and takes it away. .positioned { position: sticky; top: 30px; /* stick when 30px from the top */ } Sticky needs an offset like top; without one it never sticks. Scrolling index A classic use is an alphabetical list where each letter heading sticks at the top until the next letter pushes it away. Each heading only sticks inside its own section. Sticky also works inside any scrolling box, not just the page: the element sticks to the nearest ancestor that scrolls. Play"},{"id":"sec-a-sticky-index","title":"A sticky index","kind":"Play","text":"Scroll the list. Then turn sticky off, or change the offset. dt { position: sticky; } top 0px 20px auto (no offset) Practice"},{"id":"sec-a-sticky-bar-that-stays-on-top","title":"A sticky bar that stays on top","kind":"Practice","text":"Make the bar stick, then fix the cards that scroll over it. Read"},{"id":"sec-practical-positioning-examples","title":"Practical positioning examples","kind":"Read","text":"A tabbed info-box A tabbed box shows one panel at a time. A neat way to build it: give the panels' wrapper position: relative, and stack every panel inside it with position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0. They all occupy the same spot, and a small script hides all but the selected one (with a class like .is-hidden { display: none; }) and sets aria-selected on the tabs. Play"},{"id":"sec-stacked-tab-panels","title":"Stacked tab panels","kind":"Play","text":"Click the tabs. Then show the hidden panels to see them stacked in the same place. show the hidden panels (fanned out) Every panel is position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0 inside .panels { position: relative; }. Route Weather Parking Route 1.7 miles from Chautauqua, climbing 1,400 feet. The last section has over 200 stone steps. Weather Clear in the morning. Afternoon storms are common in summer, so start early. Parking Chautauqua's lot fills by 8am on weekends. Take the free Park to Park shuttle. A fixed position tabbed info-box Change the box to position: fixed; top: 0 and it stays on screen while the rest of the page scrolls past. The page content then needs a margin on that side (for example margin-left as wide as the box) so it isn't hidden underneath. A sliding hidden panel A panel can wait just off-screen and slide in when you need it. Give it position: fixed and a negative right equal to its width, so it's hidden past the edge of the window. A class like .open { right: 0; } brings it back, and a transition on right animates the move. The button that opens it is positioned too, with a z-index so it stays above the panel. Play"},{"id":"sec-a-sliding-panel","title":"A sliding panel","kind":"Play","text":"In this demo the panel is absolute inside the frame, standing in for fixed inside a window. .panel { position: fixed; top: 0; right: -220px; width: 200px; height: 100%; transition: right 0.6s ease-out; } .panel.open { right: 0; } .menu-btn { position: absolute; z-index: 1; } Info Royal Arch is one of the most rewarding short hikes near Boulder. The trail climbs steeply through ponderosa pine forest, gaining about 1,400 feet in under two miles. Park at Chautauqua and follow the Bluebell Road to the trailhead. Trail info Open sunrise to sunset. Dogs on leash."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"static is the default: offsets and z-index do nothing. relative nudges the element; its original space stays reserved. top: 30px moves it down. absolute leaves the flow and is placed inside the nearest positioned ancestor, so put position: relative on the parent. fixed is placed against the viewport; sticky scrolls normally, then sticks at its offset inside its parent. z-index brings positioned elements forward; with equal values, later in the source is on top."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Using absolute without a positioned parent, so the element flies to the corner of the page. Building whole layouts with absolute positioning. Use Flexbox or Grid, and save positioning for overlays and badges. Setting z-index on a static element. Sticky that never sticks because there's no top value, or a parent has overflow: hidden. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-positioning/quiz"],"exercises":["core-positioning/ex-badge","core-positioning/ex-sticky"],"questions":7},{"slug":"core-floats","num":"5.7","title":"Floats (optional)","desc":"Wrapping text around an image, why text runs behind a float, clearing floats, and containing them with display: flow-root.","props":["float","clear","shape-outside","display: flow-root"],"intro":"A floated element moves to the left or right, and the text flows around it, like a photo in a magazine. That's still the job floats do best. Before Flexbox and Grid existed they were also used for whole-page layouts, so you'll see them in older code.","more":"Optional lesson You won't need floats for new layouts: Flexbox and Grid do that job better. This lesson is here so you can wrap text around images, and so you can read older CSS. Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-the-background-of-floats","title":"The background of floats","kind":"Read","text":"The float property was added to CSS for one job: letting an image sit to one side of a column of text, with the text wrapping around it, the way magazines and newspapers have always done it. It's also used for drop caps, a large first letter that the paragraph wraps around. Before Flexbox and Grid, floats were the only way to put boxes side by side, so developers built entire multi-column page layouts out of them, along with workarounds like the \"clearfix\" hack. That's a legacy technique now, but there's a lot of it in older code and tutorials. Read"},{"id":"sec-a-float-example","title":"A float example","kind":"Read","text":"Floating the box float: left takes the element out of the normal flow and moves it to the left edge of its container. The content that follows wraps around its right side. float: right does the mirror image. .box { float: left; width: 150px; margin-right: 15px; /* space between the box and the text */ } Give a floated element a width, or it shrinks to fit its content. And put a margin on the side facing the text, so the words don't press right against it. Play"},{"id":"sec-wrap-text-around-an-image","title":"Wrap text around an image","kind":"Play","text":"Float the photo, change its margin, and round it off with shape-outside. float none left right margin (space around it) Add clear: both to the next heading Round photo with shape-outside: circle() photo Royal Arch is one of the most rewarding short hikes near Boulder. The trail climbs steeply through ponderosa pine forest, gaining about 1,400 feet in under two miles. Getting there Park at Chautauqua and follow the Bluebell Road to the trailhead. The final stretch has over 200 stone steps, so take your time and save some energy for the way down. Visualizing the float Here's something surprising. The paragraphs beside a float aren't actually narrower: their boxes still run the full width, right behind the float. Only the lines of text inside them are shortened to make room. That's why a margin on the float pushes the text away, but a margin on the paragraph doesn't seem to do anything: the paragraph's left edge is already behind the float, so a small margin-left just moves its hidden edge further behind it. Play"},{"id":"sec-where-the-paragraph-really-is","title":"Where the paragraph really is","kind":"Play","text":"Outline the paragraphs, then compare a margin on the float with a margin on the text. outline the paragraphs and the float float's margin-right paragraph's margin-left float: left The Mesa Trail runs along the foot of the Flatirons for almost seven miles, linking a dozen side trails. You can walk any part of it as an out-and-back, or make a loop with one of the canyons. In spring the meadows near NCAR are full of wildflowers. Read"},{"id":"sec-clearing-floats","title":"Clearing floats","kind":"Read","text":"Everything after a float wraps around it, until you tell an element not to. The clear property moves an element down until it's below the floats: clear: left clears floats on the left. clear: right clears floats on the right. clear: both clears any floats. Use it on whatever should start fresh, like the next section's heading. You tried clear: both in the first playground: turn it on there again and watch \"Getting there\" drop below the photo. Read"},{"id":"sec-clearing-boxes-wrapped-around-a-float","title":"Clearing boxes wrapped around a float","kind":"Read","text":"The problem A floated element doesn't count toward its parent's height. If a box contains a float that's taller than the rest of its content, the box ends before the float does: its background and border stop short, the float hangs out of the bottom, and the next element tries to wrap around it. display: flow-root The modern fix is one line on the parent: .wrapper { display: flow-root; } flow-root makes the box a new block formatting context: a self-contained area whose floats stay inside it. Older code used the \"clearfix\" hack (an ::after pseudo-element with clear: both) to do the same thing. Play"},{"id":"sec-the-collapsing-parent","title":"The collapsing parent","kind":"Play","text":"This parent only contains a float, so it shrinks to almost nothing and the next element slides up underneath. Fix the parent with display: flow-root parent (dashed border) floated The next section Practice"},{"id":"sec-wrap-text-around-a-photo","title":"Wrap text around a photo","kind":"Practice","text":"Float the photo, start the next section below it, and fix a box that collapsed."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Floats are for wrapping text around images. For layout, use Flexbox or Grid. A floated element needs a width, and a margin on the side facing the text. Text beside a float is shortened, but its box runs behind the float, so margin on the text doesn't push it away. clear: left, right or both makes an element start below the floats. display: flow-root on the parent makes it contain its floats."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Building columns with floats, then fighting collapsed parents and uneven heights. Trying to space text from a float with a margin on the paragraph instead of the float. Forgetting that floats don't work on flex or grid items. The float is ignored. On phones, a floated image squeezes the text into a narrow strip. Remove the float on small screens. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-floats/quiz"],"exercises":["core-floats/ex-float"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-media-queries","num":"6.1","title":"Media Queries","desc":"Media types and features, range syntax, and/or/not logic, choosing breakpoints, mobile-first queries, and when you need no query at all.","props":["@media","(width >= 600px)","(hover: hover)","viewport meta"],"intro":"A media query applies CSS only when a condition is true: the screen is at least this wide, the page is being printed, the device has a mouse. It's how one stylesheet adapts to every device.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-media-query-basics","title":"Media query basics","kind":"Read","text":"A media query wraps some CSS in a condition. The rules inside only apply while the condition is true: @media media-type and (media-feature-rule) { /* CSS rules go here */ } Media types The media type says what kind of output the CSS is for: screen, print or all. Leave it out and the query applies to everything. A print stylesheet is a common use: @media print { body { font-size: 12pt; } nav, .ads { display: none; } } Media feature rules Features test something about the device or the window. The one you'll use most is width, with a min- or max- prefix: @media (min-width: 600px) { … } /* 600px or wider */ @media (max-width: 599px) { … } /* up to 599px */ A few others are worth knowing: (orientation: landscape) or portrait: is the window wider than it is tall? (hover: hover): can the main input hover, like a mouse? Touchscreens can't, so hover-only menus fail there. (pointer: fine) or coarse: is it precise, like a mouse, or a finger? Make targets bigger for coarse. Using ranged syntax Newer browsers let you write width tests as comparisons, which is easier to read than min- and max-: @media (width >= 600px) { … } /* same as min-width: 600px */ @media (30em Read"},{"id":"sec-more-complex-media-queries","title":"More complex media queries","kind":"Read","text":"\"and\" logic in media queries Join conditions with and; all of them must be true: @media screen and (width >= 600px) and (orientation: landscape) { … } \"or\" logic in media queries Separate whole queries with commas; any one being true is enough: @media (width >= 600px), (orientation: landscape) { … } \"not\" logic in media queries not at the start reverses the whole query. not all and (orientation: landscape) matches portrait. Play"},{"id":"sec-ask-your-browser","title":"Ask your browser","kind":"Play","text":"Type a media query (without @media) or pick one. Your browser tests it right now, against this window and this device. = 600px)\" spellcheck=\"false\" aria-label=\"Media query to test\"> (width >= 600px) (max-width: 400px) (600px <= width <= 1000px) print screen and (orientation: landscape) (hover: hover) (pointer: coarse) (max-width: 500px), (orientation: portrait) not all and (hover: hover) This device, right now Resize the window, or open this page on your phone, and watch the answers change. Read"},{"id":"sec-how-to-choose-breakpoints","title":"How to choose breakpoints","kind":"Read","text":"The width where a query kicks in is called a breakpoint. Don't choose breakpoints from a list of phone and tablet sizes: there are thousands of devices, and new ones every year. Instead, start narrow and widen your browser window slowly (or use the responsive design mode in your browser's DevTools). Add a breakpoint wherever your layout starts to look wrong: lines too long, cards too stretched, too much empty space. When two queries both match and set the same property, the later one wins, just like any other rule. So list min-width queries from smallest to largest. Play"},{"id":"sec-change-the-screen-width","title":"Change the screen width","kind":"Play","text":"Drag the slider to change the pretend screen width and watch which rules switch on. Screen width 375px Phone · 375 Tablet · 768 Laptop · 1280 Trailhead TrailsMapsGear Weekend hikes near you Royal Arch3.4 mi · Hard Mesa Trail6.7 mi · Moderate Chautauqua Loop1.2 mi · Easy Trail conditions Muddy below 7,000 ft. Bring layers. Read"},{"id":"sec-mobile-first-responsive-design","title":"Mobile-first responsive design","kind":"Read","text":"There are two ways to write those queries. You can write the wide-screen layout first and use max-width queries to undo it on small screens. Or you can write the simple, single-column layout first, with no query at all, and use min-width queries to add columns as the screen grows. The second approach is called mobile first. It usually means less CSS, because you never write a rule just to cancel another. And because single-column, in source order, is close to what HTML does with no CSS, your base layout is mostly done before you start. The next lesson, Responsive Design and Mobile First, builds a whole page this way. Read"},{"id":"sec-creating-your-own-mobile-first-design","title":"Creating your own mobile-first design","kind":"Read","text":"A mobile-first stylesheet has the same shape every time: base styles, then one query per breakpoint. /* Base: one column for every screen */ body { width: 90%; margin: 0 auto; } /* Medium: two columns */ @media screen and (width >= 40em) { article { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 3fr 1fr; column-gap: 20px; } nav ul { display: flex; } } /* Wide: a third column */ @media screen and (width >= 70em) { main { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 3fr 1fr; } } Notice the breakpoints are in em. If someone sets a larger default text size, an em breakpoint moves with it, so the layout switches to fewer columns when the text needs more room. viewport meta None of this works on phones without one line in your <head>: <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"> Without it, mobile browsers pretend to be about 980px wide and shrink the whole page down, so your phone layout never switches on. Practice"},{"id":"sec-rewrite-the-queries-mobile-first","title":"Rewrite the queries mobile first","kind":"Practice","text":"These queries start from the desktop and undo it. Flip them to start small and add. Read"},{"id":"sec-do-you-really-need-a-media-query","title":"Do you really need a media query?","kind":"Read","text":"Flexbox and Grid are flexible by design, so many layouts adapt with no breakpoint at all. This grid fits as many 200px-plus columns as there's room for, from one on a phone to five on a monitor: .cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr)); gap: 16px; } Reach for a media query when something has to change at a size: the nav moves, a sidebar appears, the order of things shifts. For \"more columns when there's room\", let the layout do it. Practice"},{"id":"sec-print-hover-orientation-and-ranges","title":"Print, hover, orientation and ranges","kind":"Practice","text":"Four queries that aren't about a simple minimum width."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"@media print, screen or all picks the output; features like (min-width: 600px) test the device. Range syntax reads naturally: (width >= 600px), (30em <= width <= 50em). and needs every condition; a comma means or; not reverses a whole query. Mobile first: base styles for phones, then min-width queries, smallest to largest. Pick breakpoints where your layout starts to look wrong, and don't forget the viewport meta tag."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Forgetting the viewport meta tag, so phones ignore your queries. @media min-width: 600px without parentheses. Features always go in brackets. Putting a query above the base rules, so the base rules override it. Hiding important features behind :hover without checking (hover: hover). Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-media-queries/quiz"],"exercises":["core-media-queries/ex-mq","core-media-queries/ex-feat"],"questions":7},{"slug":"core-mobile-first","num":"6.2","title":"Responsive Design and Mobile First","desc":"Fluid layouts, flexible media and media queries; flexbox and grid that adapt; the viewport meta tag; and building a page mobile first.","props":["fluid layout","flex: 1","viewport meta","min-width"],"intro":"Responsive design is the idea that one page should work on every screen, by adapting its layout instead of serving a separate mobile site. Mobile first is the way to build it: start with the phone, and add to it as the screen grows.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-precursor-to-responsive-design-mobile-web-design","title":"Precursor to responsive design: mobile web design","kind":"Read","text":"When phones first got web browsers, many companies built a second, separate mobile site (often at an address like m.example.com) and used code on the server to guess which device was asking. Two sites meant twice the work, and the guessing often went wrong: tablets got the phone site, new phones got the desktop one. Phones are still different from laptops in ways that matter, like slower connections and battery life. But the answer today is one site that adapts. Read"},{"id":"sec-introducing-responsive-web-design","title":"Introducing responsive web design","kind":"Read","text":"The term responsive web design was coined by Ethan Marcotte in 2010. It combines three ideas: Fluid layouts: columns sized in percentages or fr, not fixed pixels, so they stretch and shrink with the window. Flexible media: images and video that shrink to fit, with max-width: 100%. Media queries: rules that change the layout at certain widths. Here's a useful fact: HTML is responsive by default. Text wraps, blocks fill the width they're given. Most responsive problems come from CSS that fixes a size, like a width: 960px wrapper from the days when every screen was a desktop. Read"},{"id":"sec-media-queries","title":"Media Queries","kind":"Read","text":"You met media queries in the last lesson. In responsive design, each one marks a breakpoint where the layout changes, for example from one column to two: @media screen and (width >= 80rem) { .container { margin: 1em 2em; } } Use relative units like em or rem for breakpoints where you can, and remember that a flexible layout may not need many queries at all. Read"},{"id":"sec-responsive-layout-technologies","title":"Responsive layout technologies","kind":"Read","text":"Modern layout tools are flexible by design, which is half the work of responsive design. Flexbox Flex items grow and shrink to share the space: flex: 1 and flex: 2 make the second column twice the width of the first at any window size. @media (width >= 600px) { .wrapper { display: flex; } .col1 { flex: 1; margin-right: 5%; } .col2 { flex: 2; } } CSS grid The fr unit does the same in a grid: @media (width >= 600px) { .wrapper { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr; column-gap: 5%; } } Play"},{"id":"sec-fixed-flex-and-grid","title":"Fixed, flex and grid","kind":"Play","text":"Change the window width. Compare a fixed-width layout with the flexible ones, then let them stack on narrow screens. window width layout fixed: 640px + 320px flex: 1 and flex: 2 grid: 1fr 2fr only use two columns at width >= 600px Trailhead Sidebar: trail list, filters. Main: the trail description, photos and a map of Royal Arch. Read"},{"id":"sec-responsive-images-media","title":"Responsive images/media","kind":"Read","text":"Images, video and other embeds have a fixed size, so they need one rule to make them fluid: img, picture, video { max-width: 100%; height: auto; } That stops them overflowing, but a phone still downloads the big file. HTML has tools for sending a smaller file or a different crop, which are the subject of Responsive Images. And where you can, use CSS gradients and shadows instead of image files: they're tiny and sharp at any size. Read"},{"id":"sec-responsive-typography","title":"Responsive typography","kind":"Read","text":"Text should change size too: a heading that looks right on a laptop may be too big for a phone. You can step the size up with media queries: h1 { font-size: 2rem; } @media (width >= 1200px) { h1 { font-size: 4rem; } } Or make it grow smoothly with the window by mixing in viewport units, as in font-size: calc(1.5rem + 4vw). Never use vw on its own for text: it doesn't grow when people zoom in. Fluid Sizing covers this, and clamp(), in detail. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-viewport-meta-tag","title":"The viewport meta tag","kind":"Read","text":"Every responsive page needs this line in its <head>: <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"> When phones first got browsers, most sites were built for desktop widths. So mobile browsers pretend the screen is about 980px wide, lay the page out at that size, then shrink the whole thing to fit. That kept old sites readable, sort of. But it also means your phone layout never switches on, because the browser says it's 980px wide. The viewport meta tag says \"this page is built for real device widths\", so the browser uses the phone's actual width. Play"},{"id":"sec-with-and-without-the-viewport-meta-tag","title":"With and without the viewport meta tag","kind":"Play","text":"The same page on the same phone. Change the phone's width. phone width no viewport meta with viewport meta Read"},{"id":"sec-building-mobile-first","title":"Building mobile first","kind":"Read","text":"Now put it together. There are two ways to make a site responsive. You can design for a laptop and then squeeze it onto a phone, undoing styles as the screen shrinks. Or you can start with the phone and add to it as the screen grows. The second way is mobile first, and it almost always means less CSS and fewer bugs. The phone layout is usually the simplest: one column, everything stacked, full width. So your base styles, outside any media query, describe the phone version, and each min-width query adds only what changes on wider screens. You never write a rule just to undo another one. Mobile first also applies to content. If something is too much for a phone, ask whether it's needed at all, rather than hiding it on small screens. Tip Narrowing your browser window is a good first check, but test on a real phone too. Touch targets, real font sizes and the on-screen keyboard only show up on the real thing. Play"},{"id":"sec-build-a-page-step-by-step","title":"Build a page step by step","kind":"Play","text":"Step through how one page comes together, and watch it on three screen sizes at once. Phone · 375px Tablet · 768px Laptop · 1280px How it looks now ← Previous step Next step → Practice"},{"id":"sec-flip-it-to-mobile-first","title":"Flip it to mobile first","kind":"Practice","text":"This page is built for the desktop and then undone for phones. Rewrite it to start with the phone. Practice"},{"id":"sec-challenge-a-mobile-first-layout","title":"Challenge: a mobile-first layout","kind":"Practice","text":"Based on MDN's mobile-first challenge. The phone layout is done; add a middle layout, a wide layout and print styles. Before you hand it in"},{"id":"sec-responsive-checklist","title":"Responsive checklist","kind":"Before you hand it in","text":"Tick these off for your own project. Your checks are saved in this browser only."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Responsive design = fluid layouts + flexible media + media queries. HTML is responsive by default; fixed widths are what break it. flex: 1 / flex: 2 and 1fr 2fr give columns that adapt to any width. Without the viewport meta tag, phones lay pages out at about 980px and shrink them. Mobile first: base styles are the phone layout; min-width queries only add."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Designing for a laptop, then cramming it onto a phone with lots of max-width overrides. Fixed widths like width: 960px on a wrapper, which cause sideways scrolling on phones. Forgetting the viewport meta tag. Hiding important content on mobile instead of rearranging it. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-mobile-first/quiz"],"exercises":["core-mobile-first/ex-mf","core-mobile-first/ex-challenge"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-fluid-sizing","num":"6.3","title":"Fluid Sizing","desc":"Responsive typography with media queries and viewport units, why vw alone breaks zoom, and clamp(), min(), max() and calc().","props":["clamp()","calc()","min()","vw + rem"],"intro":"Media queries change sizes in jumps. Viewport units and the math functions calc(), min(), max() and clamp() let text and spacing change smoothly with the screen, inside limits you set, often with no media query at all.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-using-media-queries-for-responsive-typography","title":"Using media queries for responsive typography","kind":"Read","text":"A heading that suits a laptop can be far too big for a phone, where a long word might not even fit across the screen. The simplest fix is a media query that steps the size up on wide screens: h1 { font-size: 2rem; } @media (width >= 1200px) { h1 { font-size: 4rem; } } That works, but it jumps: at 1199px the heading is 32px, at 1200px it doubles. More breakpoints give smaller jumps, at the cost of more CSS. Read"},{"id":"sec-using-viewport-units-for-responsive-typography","title":"Using viewport units for responsive typography","kind":"Read","text":"Viewport units grow smoothly: font-size: 6vw is 6% of the window's width, so the heading scales continuously as the window changes. But never size text with vw alone. A size that depends only on the window ignores the reader's settings. A bigger default text size has no effect on it at all, and zooming in makes the window narrower in CSS pixels, so the vw size shrinks by exactly as much as the zoom enlarges it. People who need larger text can't get it. The fix is to mix in a unit that does respond to zoom, with calc(): h1 { font-size: calc(1.5rem + 4vw); } Now the heading still grows with the window, and the 1.5rem part grows when someone zooms. Play"},{"id":"sec-text-size-when-someone-zooms","title":"Text size when someone zooms","kind":"Play","text":"Pretend the window is 400px wide, then raise the browser's text size, as someone with low vision might. browser text size At 100%, 1rem is 16px. At 200%, it's 32px. The window stays 400px wide. Read"},{"id":"sec-clamp-grow-within-limits","title":"clamp(): grow, within limits","kind":"Read","text":"calc(1.5rem + 4vw) still has no limits: on a huge monitor it gets enormous. clamp() adds them. It takes three values, always in this order: a minimum, a preferred value and a maximum. h1 { font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1rem + 3vw, 3.5rem); } The preferred value, 1rem + 3vw, grows as the window widens. clamp() keeps it between 28px and 56px, so the heading is never tiny on a phone or enormous on a big monitor. One line replaces several media queries, and the rem keeps it zoomable. Play"},{"id":"sec-four-ways-to-size-a-heading","title":"Four ways to size a heading","kind":"Play","text":"Drag the window width and compare a fixed size, a media query, vw alone and clamp(). window width clamp( min, preferred, max ) min preferred: 1rem + ?vw max 1rem is 16px here. Keeping a rem in the preferred value means the heading still grows when someone zooms in. fixed remmedia queryvw onlyclamp() Read"},{"id":"sec-min-max-and-calc","title":"min(), max() and calc()","kind":"Read","text":"min() and max() pick one of their values. width: min(100%, 600px) uses whichever is smaller, so the box fills the screen on phones but never grows past 600px. It works like max-width. max() sets a floor instead: padding: max(10px, 4vw) is never less than 10px. The names feel backwards at first: min() sets an upper limit. calc() mixes units: calc(100% - 2rem) means \"the full width, minus 32px.\" Inside clamp(), min() and max() you can do math without writing calc(). Watch out calc(100%-2rem) is silently ignored. The + and - in calc() need a space on each side: calc(100% - 2rem). Play"},{"id":"sec-columns-that-fit-with-min-and-max","title":"Columns that fit with min() and max()","kind":"Play","text":"min() picks the smallest of its values, max() the largest. They're perfect for \"this wide, but never wider than the screen\" and \"at least this much padding.\" page width .column { … } width: 640px max-width: 640px width: min(640px, 100% - 32px) padding 10px max(10px, 4vw) Look"},{"id":"sec-recipes-worth-keeping","title":"Recipes worth keeping","kind":"Look","text":"Copy these into your own stylesheet. Each one replaces a media query or two. Practice"},{"id":"sec-size-with-clamp-min-and-calc","title":"Size with clamp(), min() and calc()","kind":"Practice","text":"Replace fixed sizes with ones that grow smoothly, within limits. Practice"},{"id":"sec-responsive-typography","title":"Responsive typography","kind":"Practice","text":"From MDN's mobile-first challenge: headings that scale without wrapping, and body text that steps up on wide screens."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Media queries change text size in steps; viewport units change it smoothly. Never size text in vw alone: it ignores zoom. Mix in rem: calc(1.5rem + 4vw). clamp(1.75rem, 1rem + 3vw, 3.5rem): grow with the window, but stay between 28px and 56px. min() sets an upper limit (like max-width), max() a lower limit. These work anywhere a length works: font sizes, widths, padding, gap and grid tracks."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Leaving out the spaces: calc(100%-2rem) is ignored. Sizing text in vw alone, so it doesn't grow when people zoom. Putting clamp()'s values in the wrong order. It's always min, preferred, max. Using 100vw for widths on a page with a scrollbar, which causes sideways scrolling. Use 100%. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-fluid-sizing/quiz"],"exercises":["core-fluid-sizing/ex-fluid","core-fluid-sizing/ex-type"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-responsive-images","num":"6.4","title":"Responsive Images","desc":"Resolution switching with srcset and sizes, x descriptors for fixed-size images, art direction with picture, and lazy loading.","props":["srcset","sizes","<picture>","loading=\"lazy\""],"intro":"CSS can make an image fit any screen, but it can't change which file the browser downloads. HTML can: offer several sizes with srcset and sizes, a different crop with <picture>, and skip images nobody scrolls to with loading=\"lazy\".","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-why-responsive-images","title":"Why responsive images?","kind":"Read","text":"Say your page has a big, wide photo of a trail. On a laptop it looks great. On a phone, two things go wrong: The file is too big. CSS like max-width: 100% shrinks how the image is displayed, but the phone still downloads the full 1600px file, which wastes data and time. Sending a smaller file to smaller screens is called resolution switching. The picture doesn't work small. A wide landscape shrunk to phone size turns the interesting part into a speck. Showing a different crop is called art direction. For simple graphics like logos and icons, an SVG solves both: it's small and sharp at any size. For photos, HTML gives you srcset, sizes and <picture>. Play"},{"id":"sec-css-can-shrink-an-image-but","title":"CSS can shrink an image, but…","kind":"Play","text":"This photo is 800 × 500 pixels. Make the container narrower and see what each setting does. However small it shows, the browser downloaded all 800 pixels. container width max-width: 100% height: auto The img tag has width=\"800\" height=\"500\" attributes, which you should always include. Read"},{"id":"sec-how-do-you-create-responsive-images","title":"How do you create responsive images?","kind":"Read","text":"Resolution switching: Different sizes Make several copies of the image at different widths, and list them in srcset. Each entry is a file name and its real width in pixels, written with a w: <img src=\"arch-800.jpg\" srcset=\"arch-400.jpg 400w, arch-800.jpg 800w, arch-1600.jpg 1600w\" alt=\"The sandstone arch at the top of the trail\"> The browser works out how many real pixels it needs (including the screen's pixel density) and downloads the smallest file that will look sharp. src stays as the fallback for very old browsers. Play"},{"id":"sec-send-the-right-file-with-srcset","title":"Send the right file with srcset","kind":"Play","text":"Change how wide the image shows and the screen's pixel density. The browser picks the smallest file that's big enough. image shows at (CSS px) screen pixel density 1x 2x 3x Most phones are 2x or 3x: each CSS pixel uses 2 or 3 real pixels across. There's a catch. The browser picks the file before your CSS has loaded, so it doesn't know your layout. Unless you say otherwise, it assumes the image fills the whole window. The sizes attribute tells it how wide the image will really be: <img srcset=\"mesa-400.jpg 400w, mesa-800.jpg 800w, mesa-1600.jpg 1600w\" sizes=\"(width <= 600px) 100vw, 33vw\" src=\"mesa-800.jpg\" alt=\"Mesa Trail at sunrise\"> Each part of sizes is a media condition and a width (a length or vw, never a percentage). The first condition that matches wins, and the last value, with no condition, is the default. Here: full width on screens up to 600px, a third of the window otherwise. Play"},{"id":"sec-tell-the-browser-the-size-with-sizes","title":"Tell the browser the size with sizes","kind":"Play","text":"This layout shows 1, 2 or 3 cards per row. Without sizes, the browser assumes each card is the full window wide. window width sizes (no sizes attribute) 100vw (min-width: 700px) 50vw, 100vw (min-width: 1100px) 33vw, (min-width: 700px) 50vw, 100vw screen pixel density 1x 2x Read sizes left to right: the first media condition that's true wins, and the last value has no condition, so it's the default. Resolution switching: Same size, different resolutions Some images always show at the same size, like a 160px-wide logo. They don't need sizes; they just need sharper copies for high-density screens, where each CSS pixel is made of 2 or 3 real pixels. Use x descriptors: <img srcset=\"logo-160.png, logo-320.png 2x, logo-480.png 3x\" src=\"logo-160.png\" alt=\"Trailhead\"> The first entry has no descriptor, so it counts as 1x. Keep the display size fixed in CSS (width: 160px). Play"},{"id":"sec-which-copy-gets-picked","title":"Which copy gets picked?","kind":"Play","text":"This image has 1x, 2x and 3x copies. Start with your own screen, then try other densities. (Zooming the page changes your pixel density, too.) Pixel density your screen 1x 2x 3x <img srcset=\"logo-1x.svg, logo-2x.svg 2x, logo-3x.svg 3x\" src=\"logo-1x.svg\" alt=\"…\"> Art direction When a smaller copy isn't enough and you need a different crop, wrap the image in picture. Each <source> has a media condition and a srcset; the browser uses the first one that matches, and falls back to the <img>: <picture> <source media=\"(width < 800px)\" srcset=\"arch-square.jpg\"> <source media=\"(width >= 800px)\" srcset=\"arch-wide.jpg\"> <img src=\"arch-wide.jpg\" alt=\"Royal Arch above the trees\"> </picture> The <img> is required: it's what actually shows the picture, it holds the alt text, and your CSS targets it. Use media on sources only for art direction; for plain size switching, srcset and sizes let the browser make smarter choices. Play"},{"id":"sec-change-the-crop-with-picture","title":"Change the crop with <picture>","kind":"Play","text":"Change the window width and watch which source the browser uses. window width The browser checks each <source> in order and uses the first one whose media matches. If none match, it uses the <img>. The <img> is required. It holds the alt text, and any CSS you write targets the img, not the picture. Why can't we just do this using CSS or JavaScript? Because they're too late. To show pages quickly, browsers start downloading images as soon as they see an <img> tag, using a preload scanner, before the CSS and JavaScript have even arrived. By the time a script could choose a smaller file, the big one is already downloading. Putting the choices in the HTML lets the browser choose right the first time. Read"},{"id":"sec-lazy-loading","title":"Lazy loading","kind":"Read","text":"One more HTML attribute saves data on long pages. loading=\"lazy\" waits to download an image until the visitor scrolls near it. On a long gallery, people who never scroll to the bottom never download the images there. This is called lazy loading. Watch out Don't lazy-load the big image at the top of the page. It's the first thing visitors see, so it should start downloading as early as possible. And keep width and height on lazy images so the page doesn't jump when they arrive. Play"},{"id":"sec-skip-what-nobody-sees-with-loading-lazy","title":"Skip what nobody sees with loading=\"lazy\"","kind":"Play","text":"Scroll the page on the left and watch the network log. loading eager (default) lazy The first image is above the fold, so it is always loaded right away. Lazy-loading it would only make the page feel slower. Reload the page trailclub.github.io/hikes/ Downloaded so far Read"},{"id":"sec-implementing-your-own-responsive-images","title":"Implementing your own responsive images","kind":"Read","text":"For your own projects: export each photo at two or three widths (for example 400, 800 and 1600 pixels), list them in srcset, and add sizes if the image isn't full width. If a photo needs a different crop on phones, make a tighter crop and offer it with <picture>. In CSS, keep img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }. Practice"},{"id":"sec-make-the-images-fit","title":"Make the images fit","kind":"Practice","text":"A big photo breaks the layout and an avatar gets squashed. Fix both, and offer smaller files. Practice"},{"id":"sec-send-the-right-image","title":"Send the right image","kind":"Practice","text":"Tell the browser how big one image is, swap the crop of another, and lazy-load the third."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"CSS shrinks how an image is displayed; only HTML can change which file is downloaded. srcset with w descriptors (arch-800.jpg 800w) plus sizes lets the browser pick the smallest sharp file. x descriptors (logo-320.png 2x) suit images that always show at one size. <picture> with <source media> changes the crop (art direction); the <img> inside is required. loading=\"lazy\" on images further down the page, never the first one."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Leaving out sizes with w descriptors, so a small card image downloads at full-window size. Using a percentage in sizes. It takes lengths or vw. Forgetting the <img> inside <picture>. Without it, nothing shows. Uploading a 4000px photo straight from a phone for something shown 400px wide. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-responsive-images/quiz"],"exercises":["core-responsive-images/ex-img","core-responsive-images/ex-picture"],"questions":10},{"slug":"core-accessibility","num":"6.5","title":"Accessibility Essentials","desc":"Who accessibility is for, WCAG and the accessibility tree, semantic HTML, contrast, focus, hiding content properly, and keyboard-friendly JavaScript.","props":["semantic HTML","alt","4.5 : 1",":focus-visible"],"intro":"An accessible site works for people using a keyboard, a screen reader, zoom, captions, or a phone in bright sun. Most of the work is choosing the right HTML and not breaking it with CSS and JavaScript.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-so-what-is-accessibility","title":"So what is accessibility?","kind":"Read","text":"Accessibility is the practice of making your sites usable by as many people as possible: people with disabilities, but also people on slow connections, on small screens, in bright sunlight, or holding a baby in one arm. It isn't an extra feature for a few people. An accessible site has clear structure (which also helps search engines), works on any device, and is easier for everyone to use. In many countries it's also a legal requirement for public and commercial sites. Read"},{"id":"sec-what-kinds-of-disability-are-we-looking-at","title":"What kinds of disability are we looking at?","kind":"Read","text":"Around 1 in 6 people worldwide has a significant disability. Many use assistive technology (AT) to browse the web. People with visual impairments This includes blindness, low vision and color blindness. People may use a screen reader that reads the page aloud (JAWS and NVDA on Windows, VoiceOver on Mac and iPhone, TalkBack on Android), a screen magnifier, or simply the browser's zoom. People with hearing impairments Deaf and hard-of-hearing people need text alternatives for audio: captions on video and transcripts for podcasts. People with mobility impairments Some people can't use a mouse. They use the keyboard (especially Tab, Enter and Space), switches, voice control or a head pointer. Everything that works with a mouse must work with a keyboard. People with cognitive impairments This is the widest group, including learning disabilities, memory problems, ADHD and dyslexia. It's helped by plain language, consistent layouts, clear headings, links that look like links, and helpful error messages. Play"},{"id":"sec-see-the-same-card-differently","title":"See the same card differently","kind":"Play","text":"Turn on each condition and notice what stops working. blurred vision no color vision zoomed to 200% images don't load Royal Arch Trail status MapSaveShare Closed after dark. Dogs on leash. Read"},{"id":"sec-implementing-accessibility-into-your-project","title":"Implementing accessibility into your project","kind":"Read","text":"Accessibility is much cheaper to build in from the start than to bolt on at the end. Use the right HTML as you write it, check contrast when you pick colors, and test as you go: with the keyboard, with zoom, with an automated checker, and, if you can, with disabled users. Many organizations publish an accessibility statement saying what they support and how to report problems. Read"},{"id":"sec-accessibility-guidelines-and-the-law","title":"Accessibility guidelines and the law","kind":"Read","text":"The main standard is the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG). It's organized under four principles, often remembered as POUR: Perceivable: people can see or hear the content (alt text, captions, contrast). Operable: people can use the controls (keyboard access, enough time, no flashing). Understandable: content and controls are clear (plain language, helpful errors). Robust: it works with many browsers and assistive technologies (valid, semantic HTML). Laws in many places, such as Section 508 in the US and EN 301 549 in the EU, point to WCAG. Read"},{"id":"sec-accessibility-apis","title":"Accessibility APIs","kind":"Read","text":"How does a screen reader know what's on a page? The browser builds an accessibility tree from your HTML: every element's role (button, link, heading, text field), its name (the button's text, the image's alt, the input's label) and its state (checked, expanded). The operating system passes that tree to assistive technology. That's why HTML matters so much. A <button> arrives in the tree as \"button, Sign up\". A <div> styled to look the same arrives as plain text. WAI-ARIA attributes like role and aria-label can patch the tree when HTML has no suitable element, but the first rule of ARIA is: if a native element does the job, use it. Read"},{"id":"sec-html-a-good-basis-for-accessibility","title":"HTML: A good basis for accessibility","kind":"Read","text":"Most accessibility comes from choosing the right element, so semantic HTML brings it with it for free. Good semantics Well-structured text. Real headings in order (<h1>, <h2>…), paragraphs and lists, not bold <div>s and <br>s. Screen reader users skim by jumping from heading to heading. Clear language. Write \"5 to 7\" rather than \"5–7\", and expand abbreviations, or mark them with <abbr>. Logical page sections. <header>, <nav>, <main>, <aside> and <footer> become landmarks people can jump to. Semantic UI controls. A real <button> can be reached with Tab, pressed with Enter or Space, and is announced as a button. A clickable <div> does none of that. Every form control needs a <label>. Accessible data tables Use <th> for headers with a scope, and a <caption>, so a screen reader can say which row and column each cell belongs to. Text alternatives Every meaningful image needs alt text that says what it shows. Decorative images get an empty alt=\"\", so they're skipped. A <figure> with <figcaption> adds a visible caption. More on links Links should look like links (underlined, or clearly different) and have visible hover and focus styles. Link text should make sense on its own: \"Royal Arch trail guide\", not \"click here\". Screen reader users often list every link on a page. If a link opens a new tab or a file like a PDF, say so in the link text. A skip link at the top (\"Skip to main content\") lets keyboard users jump past the navigation. Leave space between links and buttons that sit close together, so they're easy to hit. Play"},{"id":"sec-find-the-seven-problems","title":"Find the seven problems","kind":"Play","text":"Click into the sample page and press Tab to move through it. Then switch to the fixed version and compare. Broken version Fixed version Keyboard log: what Tab reaches Clear Click the sample page, then press Tab. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-barriers","title":"Fix the barriers","kind":"Practice","text":"This sign-up box has four problems that lock people out. Fix each one. Read"},{"id":"sec-css-and-javascript-accessibility-best-practices","title":"CSS and JavaScript accessibility best practices","kind":"Read","text":"Correct semantics and user expectation CSS can make anything look like anything, which makes it easy to break what people expect. Keep the conventions: links underlined or otherwise obvious, with distinct :hover, :focus and :visited styles; abbreviations with their dotted underline; buttons that look like buttons; comfortable body text with line-height around 1.5. And never remove the focus outline without putting a clear replacement in its place. Color and color contrast Text needs enough contrast with its background: at least 4.5 to 1 for normal text and 3 to 1 for large text (WCAG AA). And never use color alone to carry meaning, like a red dot for \"closed\": add a word or an icon. Play"},{"id":"sec-check-the-contrast","title":"Check the contrast","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a text color and a background. The ratio runs from 1 (no contrast) to 21 (black on white). text color background Large heading text Normal body text, like the description of a trail or a form's help text. Small print: closed after dark, dogs on leash. Hiding things There are several ways to hide something, and they don't all hide it from screen readers: display: none and visibility: hidden hide it from everyone, screen readers included. Use them for things nobody should get, like a closed menu. A visually hidden class shrinks the element to 1px and clips it. It's invisible on screen but still read aloud. Use it for things only screen reader users need, like a label for an icon-only search button. opacity: 0 makes it invisible but still clickable and read aloud, which is usually confusing. .visually-hidden { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap; } Play"},{"id":"sec-ways-to-hide","title":"Ways to hide","kind":"Play","text":"Hide the highlighted label in different ways, and see who still gets it. hide the label with (not hidden) display: none visibility: hidden opacity: 0 .visually-hidden Search trails 🔍 Accept that users can override styles People may zoom to 200% or more, set a larger default font, force high-contrast colors, or use their own stylesheet. Build layouts that cope: sizes in rem, no fixed heights on text, nothing that relies on exact pixel positions. JavaScript JavaScript can make a page more accessible, or much less. Keep it unobtrusive: build the page with real HTML first (a real form with labels and a submit button), then add scripts to improve it, such as checking the form before it's sent. Don't build whole interfaces out of <div>s that only work with a script and a mouse. Watch out for mouse-specific events. Anything that happens on mouseover should also happen on focus; anything on mouseout should also happen on blur. In CSS, the same goes for :hover: pair it with :focus-visible or :focus-within. Practice"},{"id":"sec-accessible-css","title":"Accessible CSS","kind":"Practice","text":"Hide a label the right way, bring back focus styles, and make a hover-only detail work with the keyboard. Your turn"},{"id":"sec-five-checks-for-your-own-project","title":"Five checks for your own project","kind":"Your turn","text":"Unplug your mouseUse Tab, Shift+Tab, Enter and Space. Can you reach and use everything? Can you always see where you are? Zoom to 200%Press Ctrl or Cmd and + a few times. Nothing should overlap or get cut off. Check contrastBody text needs at least 4.5 : 1 against its background. Use the contrast checker above. Read the headings aloneDo h1, h2 and h3 make a sensible outline of the page, in order? Turn images offDoes the alt text tell you what you'd miss? See Images."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Accessibility helps people with visual, hearing, mobility and cognitive disabilities, and everyone else too. WCAG's four principles: perceivable, operable, understandable, robust. The browser builds an accessibility tree from your HTML: role, name and state. The right element gets them right. Text needs 4.5 : 1 contrast; never use color alone to show meaning. display: none hides from everyone; a visually-hidden class hides from the screen only."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Clickable divs and spans. They can't be reached with Tab. outline: none with no replacement focus style. Placeholder text used as the only label. Things that only appear on :hover or mouseover, so keyboard users never see them. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-accessibility/quiz"],"exercises":["core-accessibility/ex-a11y","core-accessibility/ex-css"],"questions":8},{"slug":"core-organizing-css","num":"6.6","title":"Organizing CSS","desc":"Style guides, formatting and comments, logical sections, low specificity, splitting stylesheets, BEM and other methodologies, and build tools like Sass.","props":["/* || */","BEM","low specificity","Sass"],"intro":"CSS doesn't care how your stylesheet is arranged. You do, the next time you need to change something and can't find it. A few habits, and a naming system or two, keep a stylesheet easy to work with as it grows.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-tips-to-keep-your-css-tidy","title":"Tips to keep your CSS tidy","kind":"Read","text":"Does your project have a coding style guide? If you're working with a team, or on an existing project, check whether there's a style guide first. Following the team's conventions matters more than your own preferences: a stylesheet written five different ways is harder to work with than one written any single way. Keep it consistent Whatever rules you choose, use them everywhere: the same way of naming classes, the same color format (all hex, or all rgb()), the same indentation. Consistency means you can predict where things are and how they're written. Formatting readable CSS Some people write each rule on one line; most put each declaration on its own line, which is easier to scan and gives cleaner diffs in version control. Either is fine as long as you're consistent. Play"},{"id":"sec-the-same-css-three-ways","title":"The same CSS, three ways","kind":"Play","text":"Switch between formats. The browser reads them all the same way. One line per rule One declaration per line With section comments Comment your CSS Comments help the next person, including you in six months. Use them for two things. First, section headings, written in a way that's easy to search for. MDN suggests ||, which rarely appears anywhere else, so searching for || jumps from section to section: /* || General styles */ /* || Utilities */ /* || Site-wide */ Second, explain anything surprising, like why a rule exists: .box { background-color: red; /* fallback for browsers without gradients */ background-image: linear-gradient(to right, #ff0000, #aa0000); } Create logical sections in your stylesheet A good order runs from general to specific: General styles for plain elements: body, p, headings, lists, links, tables. Utility classes: tiny one-job classes like .no-bullets or .text-center. Site-wide components: things on every page, like .main-nav and .logo. Specific components and pages: cards, the trail page, the sign-up form. Because later rules win ties, this order also means the specific stuff naturally overrides the general. Play"},{"id":"sec-clean-up-a-stylesheet","title":"Clean up a stylesheet","kind":"Play","text":"Step through four cleanups. The page looks identical at every step; only the stylesheet changes. Practice"},{"id":"sec-where-does-this-rule-go","title":"Where does this rule go?","kind":"Practice","text":"Base styles for plain elements, then layout, then components, then small utility classes. Read"},{"id":"sec-avoid-overly-specific-selectors","title":"Avoid overly-specific selectors","kind":"Read","text":"A selector like article.main p.box only works in one exact place, and its high specificity makes it hard to override. Then the override needs an even longer selector, and eventually !important. Style with single classes instead: .box works wherever you put it. Read"},{"id":"sec-break-large-stylesheets-into-multiple-smaller-ones","title":"Break large stylesheets into multiple smaller ones","kind":"Read","text":"On a big site, one huge file gets hard to navigate. You can split it, for example into a global stylesheet and one per section, and link them in order. Rules in later files win ties, just as later rules in one file do: <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"global.css\"> <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"trails.css\"> Read"},{"id":"sec-other-tools-that-can-help","title":"Other tools that can help","kind":"Read","text":"CSS methodologies Some teams follow a published set of rules for writing CSS, called a methodology. Two well-known ones: OOCSS (Object-Oriented CSS) separates reusable patterns into classes. A common \"media object\", an image beside some text, gets one .media class reused everywhere, instead of being styled again in each place. BEM (Block, Element, Modifier) is a naming system. A block is a standalone component (.card). An element is a part of it, joined with two underscores (.card__title). A modifier is a variation, joined with two hyphens (.card--featured). The names are long, but you can tell from a class alone what it belongs to. Others include SMACSS, ITCSS and Atomic CSS. You don't need one for a small project, but you'll meet them on bigger ones. Play"},{"id":"sec-name-the-parts-with-bem","title":"Name the parts with BEM","kind":"Play","text":"Click each part of the card to see its BEM class. Then turn on the modifier. .card add a modifier Royal Arch A steep climb to a sandstone arch. Details Practice"},{"id":"sec-pick-the-better-class-name","title":"Pick the better class name","kind":"Practice","text":"Name classes for what something is, not how it looks. Build systems for CSS Larger projects often run CSS through tools before it reaches the browser. A preprocessor like Sass adds features to CSS and compiles your files into plain CSS. Sass variables ($brand: #2340d9;) came before CSS had its own; today native CSS variables do the same job in the browser. Sass is still popular for splitting styles into small partial files and combining them with @use: // main.scss @use \"base\"; @use \"layout\"; @use \"components/card\"; A post-processor works on the finished CSS: for example, cssnano strips comments and spaces to make the file smaller. Your readable, commented source stays readable; the browser gets the small version. Practice"},{"id":"sec-clean-up-the-stylesheet","title":"Clean up the stylesheet","kind":"Practice","text":"Rename a class by its purpose, store the repeated color once, and drop a heavy selector. Practice"},{"id":"sec-sections-and-bem","title":"Sections and BEM","kind":"Practice","text":"Put the rules in labeled sections, then rename the card's parts with BEM."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Follow the team's style guide; whatever you choose, be consistent. Use searchable section comments like /* || Base */, and comment anything surprising. Order sections from general to specific: base, utilities, site-wide, components. Keep specificity low: single classes, not long chains or IDs. BEM: .block, .block__element, .block--modifier."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Adding every new rule to the bottom of the file. Names like .box2, .new or .left that stop making sense later. Overriding a long selector with an even longer one, then !important. Leaving old, unused rules in \"just in case\". That's what version control is for. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-organizing-css/quiz1","core-organizing-css/quiz2","core-organizing-css/quiz"],"exercises":["core-organizing-css/ex-organize","core-organizing-css/ex-bem"],"questions":14},{"slug":"core-what-is-javascript","num":"7.1","title":"What is JavaScript?","desc":"What JavaScript adds to a page, how the browser runs it, adding scripts with defer or modules, and a first look at a small program.","props":["<script defer>","addEventListener","running order","// comments"],"intro":"HTML gives a page its structure, CSS gives it style, and JavaScript gives it behavior. Here's what JavaScript can do, how it runs in the browser, how to add it to a page, and a first look at a small program.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-a-high-level-definition","title":"A high-level definition","kind":"Read","text":"JavaScript is a programming language that lets you make web pages do things: update content as you watch, show a map you can drag around, animate graphics, check a form before it's sent. It's the third layer of the web's layer cake: HTML gives the page structure and meaning: a heading, a paragraph, a button. CSS styles it: colors, fonts, layout. JavaScript adds behavior: what happens when someone clicks the button. const button = document.querySelector(\"button\"); button.addEventListener(\"click\", updateName); function updateName() { const name = prompt(\"Enter a new name\"); button.textContent = `Player 1: ${name}`; } Each layer builds on the one before. JavaScript can't add behavior to a button that doesn't exist. Tip JavaScript has nothing to do with Java. The similar name was a marketing decision in 1995, and it has confused people ever since. Play"},{"id":"sec-peel-back-the-layers","title":"Peel back the layers","kind":"Play","text":"Turn each layer off and on to see what it adds. Layers HTMLStructure: there is a button, and it says something. CSSPresentation: colors, font, border, shape. JavaScriptBehavior: clicking the button lets you change the name. Each layer builds on the one before it. JavaScript can't add behavior to a button that doesn't exist. Read"},{"id":"sec-so-what-can-it-really-do","title":"So what can it really do?","kind":"Read","text":"The core language gives you the basics of programming: store values in variables, do math and work with text, and run code when something happens (an event, like a click). On top of that, browsers provide APIs, ready-made toolkits you can use from JavaScript: The DOM API lets you change the HTML and CSS of the page, which is how pop-ups, menus and live updates work. The Geolocation API finds where the user is (with permission). The Canvas and WebGL APIs draw 2D and 3D graphics. Audio and video APIs play and record media. There are also third-party APIs from companies like map and payment services. This course stays with the core language and the DOM. Read"},{"id":"sec-what-is-javascript-doing-on-your-page","title":"What is JavaScript doing on your page?","kind":"Read","text":"When a page loads, the browser reads the HTML, applies the CSS, and runs the JavaScript. The script can then keep changing the page while it's open. Browser security Each browser tab runs its code in its own separate environment. Code in one tab can't see or change another tab or another site, which stops a malicious site from reaching into your bank's tab. JavaScript running order The browser runs your code from top to bottom, one line at a time. So order matters: you can't use something before the line that creates it. Run this and read the error: Swap the last two lines and run it again. Reading errors like this one is a skill you'll practise in Troubleshooting JavaScript. Interpreted versus compiled code Some languages are compiled into a program before they run. JavaScript is interpreted: the browser takes your code as text and runs it. (Modern browsers compile it on the fly behind the scenes to make it fast, but you never have to.) Server-side versus client-side code JavaScript in a web page is client-side: it runs in the visitor's browser. Server-side code runs on the web server before the page is sent. JavaScript can run on servers too, with Node.js, but this course is all client-side. Dynamic versus static code A static page shows the same content every time. A dynamic page can change what it shows, for example by building a list from data or updating a score. JavaScript is what makes a page dynamic in the browser. Read"},{"id":"sec-how-do-you-add-javascript-to-your-page","title":"How do you add JavaScript to your page?","kind":"Read","text":"JavaScript goes in a <script> element. Internal JavaScript You can write the code inside the element, usually just before </body>, so the elements above it already exist when it runs. External JavaScript Usually, it's better to keep JavaScript in its own file, just as CSS has its own stylesheet, and link it from the <head>: <script defer src=\"script.js\"></script> MDN's examples use type=\"module\" instead of defer. Modules wait in the same way, but they only work when the page is served by a web server (GitHub Pages, or a local one like VS Code's Live Server), not when you double-click the file. Inline JavaScript handlers You'll see old code like <button onclick=\"createParagraph()\">. Don't do this: it mixes behavior into your HTML, and you'd have to add the attribute to every button by hand. Using addEventListener instead Keep the behavior in your script, and attach it from there. This finds every button and gives each one the same behavior: const buttons = document.querySelectorAll(\"button\"); for (const button of buttons) { button.addEventListener(\"click\", createParagraph); } Script loading strategies The browser reads HTML from top to bottom. A plain <script src> in the head makes it stop, download and run the script before the body exists, so the script can't find the elements it needs. defer (or type=\"module\") says: download now, but run after the whole page has been read. (async runs as soon as it's downloaded, in any order, which suits independent scripts like analytics.) Play"},{"id":"sec-where-does-the-script-tag-go","title":"Where does the script tag go?","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a placement and see what happens as the page loads. Placement <script src> in <head> <script defer src> in <head> <script> at the end of <body> type=\"module\", opened from your computer type=\"module\", on a web server Read"},{"id":"sec-comments","title":"Comments","kind":"Read","text":"Comments are notes the browser ignores. Use them to explain why code does something: Practice"},{"id":"sec-your-first-lines-of-javascript","title":"Your first lines of JavaScript","kind":"Practice","text":"Make the console say something. Press Run to see the output, then Check my work. Read"},{"id":"sec-a-first-splash-thinking-like-a-programmer","title":"A first splash: thinking like a programmer","kind":"Read","text":"MDN's first project is a number guessing game. Before writing any code, a programmer breaks the idea into small steps: Pick a random number between 1 and 100. Let the player guess, and record each guess. Say whether the guess was right, too high or too low. After 10 wrong guesses, or a right one, end the game and offer to start again. Each step becomes a few lines of code using things you'll learn in the next few weeks: variables to store the number and the guesses, conditionals to compare them, a function to check each guess, and an event to run it when the button is clicked. let randomNumber = Math.floor(Math.random() * 100) + 1; let guessCount = 1; function checkGuess() { const userGuess = Number(guessField.value); if (userGuess === randomNumber) { lastResult.textContent = \"Congratulations! You got it right!\"; } else if (guessCount === 10) { lastResult.textContent = \"!!!GAME OVER!!!\"; } else { lastResult.textContent = \"Wrong!\"; lowOrHi.textContent = userGuess < randomNumber ? \"Last guess was too low!\" : \"Last guess was too high!\"; } guessCount++; } guessSubmit.addEventListener(\"click\", checkGuess); Play"},{"id":"sec-play-the-game","title":"Play the game","kind":"Play","text":"This is that program, running. You don't need to understand every line yet. Number guessing game I've picked a number between 1 and 100. You have 10 turns. Enter a guess: Submit guess Start new game Practice"},{"id":"sec-check-a-guess","title":"Check a guess","kind":"Practice","text":"Write the heart of the game: the function that compares a guess with the secret number."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"HTML is structure, CSS is presentation, JavaScript is behavior. Browser APIs (like the DOM) let JavaScript change the page, draw graphics and more. Each tab runs its code separately, from top to bottom; you can't use something before it's created. Link scripts with <script defer src=\"script.js\">, or type=\"module\" on a server. Attach behavior with addEventListener, not onclick attributes."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"A plain <script src> in the head with no defer: the elements don't exist yet, so you get null errors. Writing <script src=\"script.js\" />. A script tag always needs a closing </script>. Expecting type=\"module\" to work from a double-clicked file. A wrong path in src: nothing happens, and the console shows a 404 for the file. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-what-is-javascript/quiz"],"exercises":["core-what-is-javascript/ex-first","core-what-is-javascript/ex-guess"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-troubleshooting-js","num":"7.2","title":"Troubleshooting JavaScript","desc":"Syntax, runtime and logic errors; reading the console; linting, the Console API and the debugger; and handling errors with throw and try...catch.","props":["console.log()","TypeError","debugger","try...catch"],"intro":"Everyone's code breaks. The skill is finding out why, quickly: reading the console's error messages, logging values, using the debugger, and writing code that handles bad input without crashing.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-types-of-error","title":"Types of error","kind":"Read","text":"When HTML or CSS has a mistake, you can usually see it. When JavaScript breaks, the page often just stops responding, with no message at all. The explanation is waiting in the browser's console: open DevTools and click the Console tab. Errors come in three kinds: A syntax error is a typo in the grammar of the code, like a missing bracket or quote. The browser can't understand the file, so none of the script runs. A runtime error happens while the code is running, like calling something that isn't a function. The script runs up to that line, then stops. A logic error shows no message at all. The code runs fine but does the wrong thing: the score goes down instead of up. Read"},{"id":"sec-fixing-errors-reported-in-the-console","title":"Fixing errors reported in the console","kind":"Read","text":"MDN's example is the number guessing game from What is JavaScript?, with bugs added. Every error message tells you what went wrong and where. game.js:21:13 means line 21, character 13; click it and the browser jumps straight to that line. Read the message slowly. guessSubmit.addeventListener is not a function means that on that line, the thing you called isn't a function. Here it's a capital-letter typo: JavaScript is case-sensitive, so it's addEventListener. lowOrHi is null means a querySelector() found nothing, usually because the selector is wrong (a class without its dot). Fix the first error first. One error stops the code after it from running, so later problems can't even show up yet. Fix it, reload, and see what's next. A logic error MDN's game also has a bug with no message: the \"random\" number is always 1. To find a logic error, use console.log() to print values as the code runs. Is the number what you expected? Is that line running at all? Each answer narrows down where the bug is hiding. Play"},{"id":"sec-debug-the-guessing-game","title":"Debug the guessing game","kind":"Play","text":"Play the game, then open the console to find the five bugs. Test the game 1. Load the page 2. Guess 50 and submit 3. Play 4 guesses: 50, 25, 12, 6 4. Add console.log(randomNumber), reload 5 times Open the console in your own browser with F12, or ⌥⌘J on a Mac (Chrome). In Safari, turn on Show features for web developers first. Start over with all bugs Run a test, read the console, then click the line of code you think is broken. Read"},{"id":"sec-other-common-errors","title":"Other common errors","kind":"Read","text":"A handful of mistakes cause most errors: = (assign) where you meant === (compare): if (guessCount = 10) sets the count to 10 instead of checking it, so the game ends after one guess. A missing ), } or quote: \"missing ) after argument list\", \"missing } after function body\", \"unterminated string literal\". A misspelled name: \"something is not defined\". Play"},{"id":"sec-error-message-decoder","title":"Error message decoder","kind":"Play","text":"The errors you'll see most often. Browsers word some differently, but the type at the start (SyntaxError, TypeError, ReferenceError) is the same everywhere. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-it-yourself","title":"Fix it yourself","kind":"Practice","text":"This code has three bugs. Run it, read the first error, fix that one, and run it again. When it works, the console prints three lines. Practice"},{"id":"sec-debug-the-script","title":"Debug the script","kind":"Practice","text":"A syntax error, a runtime error and a logic error. Fix them one at a time, starting with the first message. Read"},{"id":"sec-linting-your-code","title":"Linting your code","kind":"Read","text":"A linter reads your code and points out likely mistakes before you even run it: misspelled names, variables you never use, = inside an if. The most popular one for JavaScript is ESLint. Code editor plugins Install the ESLint extension in VS Code and problems get a squiggly underline as you type, like a spell-checker for code. Read"},{"id":"sec-common-javascript-problems","title":"Common JavaScript problems","kind":"Read","text":"Beyond typos, a few problems trip up everyone at some point: using a variable outside the block or function where it was created (scope), confusing let and var, and code that runs before the data it needs has arrived (like using the result of fetch() before it's finished). You'll meet each of these in later lessons. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-console-api","title":"The Console API","kind":"Read","text":"console.log() is one of several console methods. console.error() prints in red, and shows the call stack: the chain of function calls that led to that line, most recent first. That tells you not just where something happened, but how the code got there. The second call logs an error, then crashes. The log right before the crash, trail: undefined, shows why: the lookup found nothing. In the browser's own console, the red error also lists the call stack: getTrail, called from showMiles, called from the top of the script. Read"},{"id":"sec-using-the-javascript-debugger","title":"Using the JavaScript debugger","kind":"Read","text":"For trickier bugs, the debugger lets you pause the code and look around. In the Sources tab (Chrome) or Debugger tab (Firefox), click a line number to set a breakpoint. When the code reaches that line, it pauses. The Scope panel shows every variable's current value, the Call Stack panel shows how you got there, and the step buttons run one line at a time. You can also pause from your code by writing debugger; on a line. It does nothing unless DevTools is open. Read"},{"id":"sec-handling-javascript-errors-in-your-code","title":"Handling JavaScript errors in your code","kind":"Read","text":"Some errors aren't your typos: they come from bad input, like a user typing \"ten\" where you expected a number. Good code expects them. Conditionals Check values before you use them, and return early if they're wrong: Throwing custom errors Sometimes it's better to stop with a clear message than to carry on with a bad value. throw new Error(\"message\") creates your own error, which stops the function just like a built-in one. try...catch Code that might throw can go in a try block. If anything inside throws, the code jumps to catch, where you can handle it, and the rest of the script keeps running: Feature detection Some browser features aren't available everywhere. Check before you use one, and offer something else if it's missing: if (\"geolocation\" in navigator) { navigator.geolocation.getCurrentPosition(showNearbyTrails); } else { showAllTrails(); } Practice"},{"id":"sec-handle-bad-input","title":"Handle bad input","kind":"Practice","text":"Make a conversion function that refuses bad input, and a caller that copes when it does. Read"},{"id":"sec-finding-help","title":"Finding help","kind":"Read","text":"When you're stuck, search for the exact error message. MDN's JavaScript error reference explains every message; Stack Overflow probably has someone who hit the same problem; and caniuse.com tells you which browsers support a feature. When you ask a person, include the code, the full error and what you've already tried."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Syntax errors stop the whole script; runtime errors stop it at that line; logic errors give no message. Every error says what and where: game.js:21:13 is line 21, character 13. Fix the first error first. console.log() checks values; console.error() also shows the call stack; breakpoints pause the code. Check input with typeof and Number.isNaN(); throw new Error() stops with a clear message. try...catch handles a thrown error so the rest of the script keeps running."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Assuming the error is on the line it names. The real cause can be earlier, such as a selector that matched nothing. Wrong capitalization: addeventListener, queryselector. JavaScript is case-sensitive. Using = when you mean === inside an if. Forgetting to reload the page after saving, then debugging the old code. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-troubleshooting-js/quiz"],"exercises":["core-troubleshooting-js/ex-debug","core-troubleshooting-js/ex-handle"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-variables","num":"7.3","title":"Variables","desc":"Declaring, initializing and updating variables, naming rules, why to avoid var, the main types of value, typeof, and choosing const or let.","props":["let","const","typeof","camelCase"],"intro":"A program needs to remember things: a score, a name, whether a menu is open. Variables give values a name so you can use and change them later.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-is-a-variable","title":"What is a variable?","kind":"Read","text":"A variable is a container for a value: a name that holds something so you can use it later. Think of a labelled box. Variable example MDN's example counts button presses. The count lives in a variable, and each press adds one to it: const buttonA = document.querySelector(\"#button_A\"); let count = 0; buttonA.addEventListener(\"click\", () => { count += 1; headingA.textContent = `${count} clicks so far`; }); Without a variable Without somewhere to store the count, the program can't remember anything between presses. Try both: Play"},{"id":"sec-with-and-without-a-variable","title":"With and without a variable","kind":"Play","text":"With a variable Press me 0 clicks so far Without one Press me 0 clicks so far This version can't store anything, so every press starts from nothing again. Read"},{"id":"sec-declaring-a-variable","title":"Declaring a variable","kind":"Read","text":"To make a variable, declare it with let (or const) and a name. A declared variable with no value holds undefined, which is different from a name that doesn't exist at all: Read"},{"id":"sec-initializing-a-variable","title":"Initializing a variable","kind":"Read","text":"Initializing means giving a variable its first value, with =. You can do it on its own line or, usually, at the same time as declaring: let myName; myName = \"Chris\"; // initialize later let myDog = \"Rover\"; // declare and initialize at once Read"},{"id":"sec-a-note-about-var","title":"A note about var","kind":"Read","text":"Older code uses var. It works, but it has quirks that cause bugs: you can use a var before the line that declares it (it's \"hoisted\" and quietly holds undefined), and you can declare the same name twice without any error. let and const, added in 2015, catch both mistakes. Use them, and read var as \"old code\". Read"},{"id":"sec-updating-a-variable","title":"Updating a variable","kind":"Read","text":"Once a let variable exists, give it a new value with =, without let. Writing let again is an error: you only declare once. let miles = 6.7; miles = miles + 1; // now 7.7 Play"},{"id":"sec-watch-the-boxes-change","title":"Watch the boxes change","kind":"Play","text":"Step through the code one line at a time and watch each variable's value. ◂ Back Run next line ▸ Restart Variables in memory An aside on variable naming rules Names can contain letters, numbers, _ and $, but can't start with a number, contain spaces, or be one of JavaScript's reserved words like let or function. Use \"lower camel case\" for multi-word names: trailName, isOpen. Names are case-sensitive, so score and Score are two different variables. Choose names that say what the value is. Play"},{"id":"sec-name-checker","title":"Name checker","kind":"Play","text":"Some names break your code, and others just make it hard to read. Type a variable name, or try one of the examples. Variable name Read"},{"id":"sec-variable-types","title":"Variable types","kind":"Read","text":"Every value has a type. The main ones: Numbers Whole numbers or decimals, with no quotes: let miles = 6.7; Strings Text, in quotes: let trail = \"Mesa\"; Quotes always make a string, even \"42\". Booleans Just true or false, often the result of a comparison: let isLong = miles > 5; Arrays A list of values in square brackets, counted from 0: let trails = [\"Mesa\", \"Royal Arch\"]; then trails[0] is \"Mesa\". Objects A group of named values in curly braces: let hike = { name: \"Mesa\", miles: 6.7 }; then hike.name is \"Mesa\". Read"},{"id":"sec-dynamic-typing","title":"Dynamic typing","kind":"Read","text":"JavaScript is dynamically typed: you don't say what type a variable holds, and the same let variable can hold a string now and a number later. typeof tells you what type a value is right now. Try it"},{"id":"sec-typeof","title":"typeof","kind":"Try it","text":"Quotes always make a string, even when the value looks like a number. Change the values and run it again. Read"},{"id":"sec-constants-in-javascript","title":"Constants in JavaScript","kind":"Read","text":"const creates a variable that can't be given a new value. It must be initialized when it's declared, and = on it later is an error. But a const holding an object or array can still have its contents changed: the box can't be swapped, but what's inside it can. Read"},{"id":"sec-when-to-use-const-and-when-to-use-let","title":"When to use const and when to use let","kind":"Read","text":"Use const by default, and let only when you know the value will be reassigned, like a counter or a score. That way, anyone reading your code knows which values move, and JavaScript stops you if you change one by accident. Practice"},{"id":"sec-pick-const-or-let","title":"Pick const or let","kind":"Practice","text":"Each of these variables is declared the wrong way. Fix them so the code runs and says what it means. Practice"},{"id":"sec-one-of-each-type","title":"One of each type","kind":"Practice","text":"Store a trail's details using five different types of value."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"A variable is a named container for a value. Declare it once, with let or const. A declared but empty variable is undefined; an undeclared name is a ReferenceError. Avoid var: it's hoisted and can be redeclared without warning. Main types: numbers, strings, booleans, arrays and objects. typeof tells you which. const by default; let when the value will be reassigned. A const object's contents can still change."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Writing let again when updating a variable. Putting quotes around a number: \"42\" is a string. Names with spaces or starting with a number, like 2ndTrail. Using const for a counter, then trying to add to it. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-variables/quiz"],"exercises":["core-variables/ex-vars","core-variables/ex-types"],"questions":8},{"slug":"core-numbers-strings","num":"7.4","title":"Numbers and Strings","desc":"Arithmetic, precedence, increment, assignment and comparison operators; template literals and conversion; and the most useful string methods.","props":["% and **","===","`${}`","slice()"],"intro":"Most of what a program does is work with numbers and text: adding up distances, building messages, checking what someone typed. Here's the math, the operators, and the most useful things you can do with strings.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-types-of-numbers","title":"Types of numbers","kind":"Read","text":"In everyday code you'll use two kinds of number, whole numbers (integers, like 42) and decimals (floats, like 6.7), but JavaScript has just one type for both: Number. (There's also BigInt for enormous whole numbers, which you'll rarely need.) Useful Number methods toFixed() rounds a number to a set number of decimal places, for display. It returns a string. Converting to number data types Values that look like numbers can be strings, especially anything a visitor types into a form. Number() converts a string to a number; if it can't, you get NaN, \"not a number\". Read"},{"id":"sec-arithmetic-operators","title":"Arithmetic operators","kind":"Read","text":"JavaScript has the usual operators, + - * /, plus two more: % (remainder) gives what's left over after dividing, so 7 % 2 is 1, and ** (exponent) raises to a power, so 5 ** 2 is 25. Operator precedence As in math class, * and / happen before + and -. Use parentheses to change the order, or just to make it clear. Predict each line before you run it: Read"},{"id":"sec-increment-and-decrement-operators","title":"Increment and decrement operators","kind":"Read","text":"++ adds one to a variable and -- takes one away. They're common in counters and loops. You can only use them on a variable, not a plain number. Read"},{"id":"sec-assignment-operators","title":"Assignment operators","kind":"Read","text":"= assigns a value. Shortcut versions do the math and assign in one step: x += 4 means x = x + 4, and there's -=, *= and /= too. Read"},{"id":"sec-comparison-operators","title":"Comparison operators","kind":"Read","text":"Comparisons give true or false: === equal, and !== not equal. They check the type too, so 5 === \"5\" is false. <, >, <=, >= for less and greater. You'll see == and != in older code. They convert types before comparing, which gives surprises, so use the triple versions. Read"},{"id":"sec-declaring-strings","title":"Declaring strings","kind":"Read","text":"A string is text in quotes. Without the quotes, JavaScript thinks the word is a variable name and gives an error. Single quotes, double quotes, and backticks You can use 'single', \"double\" or `backtick` quotes, as long as the start and end match. Pick one style for normal strings and stick with it. Backticks are special: they make a template literal. Read"},{"id":"sec-embedding-javascript","title":"Embedding JavaScript","kind":"Read","text":"Inside a template literal, ${ } inserts the value of any variable or expression: const name = \"Chris\"; const greeting = `Hello, ${name}`; Concatenation using \"+\" The older way is to join strings with +, called concatenation: \"Hello, \" + name. It works, but long ones get hard to read, with spaces easy to forget. Including expressions in strings ${ } can hold any expression, not just a variable: `You scored ${(score / highestScore) * 100}%.` Try it"},{"id":"sec-template-literals-and-math","title":"Template literals and Math","kind":"Try it","text":"Backticks let you put values right inside a string with ${ }. Read"},{"id":"sec-multiline-strings","title":"Multiline strings","kind":"Read","text":"Template literals keep line breaks just as you type them. In a normal string, write \\n for a new line. Including quotes in strings To put a quote inside a string, use the other kind around it (\"It's steep\"), or escape it with a backslash: 'It\\'s steep'. Read"},{"id":"sec-numbers-vs-strings","title":"Numbers vs. strings","kind":"Read","text":"+ does two jobs. With two numbers it adds; if either side is a string, it joins them. Other operators like * and - always try to make numbers. Convert on purpose: Number(\"74\") makes a number, and String(74) (or (74).toString()) makes a string. Play"},{"id":"sec-add-or-join","title":"Add or join?","kind":"Play","text":"Change a and b, and switch between numbers and text, to see what each operator does. a number \"string\" b number \"string\" Operator Values typed into a form field are always strings. Click \"string\" on one side to see what happens if you forget to convert them. Practice"},{"id":"sec-add-don-t-join","title":"Add, don't join","kind":"Practice","text":"A value from a form arrives as text, so + joins instead of adding. Fix the total and tidy the output. Read"},{"id":"sec-useful-string-methods","title":"Useful string methods","kind":"Read","text":"Strings as objects Strings come with built-in properties and methods, which you use with a dot: text.length, text.toUpperCase(). Methods never change the original string: they return a new one. (Strings can't be changed at all; they're immutable.) Finding the length of a string text.length is the number of characters. It's a property, so no parentheses. Retrieving a specific string character Each character has an index, counted from 0: text[0] is the first, and text[text.length - 1] is the last. Testing if a string contains a substring includes(\"Arch\"), startsWith(\"Royal\") and endsWith(\"Trail\") return true or false. They're case-sensitive. Finding the position of a substring in a string indexOf(\"Arch\") returns where it starts, or -1 if it isn't there. Extracting a substring from a string slice(start, end) returns the characters from start up to, but not including, end. Leave out end to go to the end of the string. Changing case toLowerCase() and toUpperCase(). Handy for comparing what someone typed, whatever capitals they used. Updating parts of a string replace(\"old\", \"new\") changes the first match; replaceAll() changes every match. Remember to store the result: text = text.replace(…). Play"},{"id":"sec-try-the-string-methods","title":"Try the string methods","kind":"Play","text":"Every character has an index, starting at 0. Pick a method to see what it returns. const text = Practice"},{"id":"sec-learning-challenges","title":"Learning challenges","kind":"Practice","text":"MDN's three string challenges: filter messages, fix capitals, and build a new string from parts."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"One Number type for whole numbers and decimals. toFixed(2) rounds for display. * and / before + and -; % is the remainder, ** a power. ++, += and friends update a variable; === and !== compare value and type. Template literals: `${value}`. + joins if either side is a string, so convert with Number(). String methods return new strings: includes, indexOf, slice, toLowerCase, replace."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Adding a form value without Number(): \"10\" + 5 is \"105\". Using == instead of ===. Forgetting that slice's end index isn't included. Calling a method and not storing the result, expecting the original string to change. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-numbers-strings/quiz"],"exercises":["core-numbers-strings/ex-strings","core-numbers-strings/ex-methods"],"questions":9},{"slug":"core-arrays","num":"7.5","title":"Arrays","desc":"Creating arrays, indexes and length, adding and removing items, for...of, map() and filter(), and converting with split() and join().","props":["[0]","push()","map()","split()"],"intro":"When you have a list of related values, like every trail in a guide, an array keeps them in order under one name. You can read any item, add and remove items, loop through them all, and convert between arrays and strings.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-is-an-array","title":"What is an array?","kind":"Read","text":"An array is a single variable that stores a list of values, in order. Instead of trail1, trail2, trail3, you have one trails list that can hold three items or three thousand, and code that works the same either way. Read"},{"id":"sec-creating-arrays","title":"Creating arrays","kind":"Read","text":"Put the items in square brackets, separated by commas. An array can hold any type, a mix of types, or even other arrays: Read"},{"id":"sec-finding-the-length-of-an-array","title":"Finding the length of an array","kind":"Read","text":"trails.length is the number of items. It's a property, so no parentheses. Read"},{"id":"sec-accessing-and-modifying-array-items","title":"Accessing and modifying array items","kind":"Read","text":"Each item has an index, its position, counted from 0. So the first item is trails[0] and the last is trails[trails.length - 1]. Asking for an index that doesn't exist gives undefined, not an error. To change an item, assign to its index: trails[1] = \"Flagstaff\". For an array inside an array, use two indexes: nested[1][0]. Read"},{"id":"sec-finding-the-index-of-items-in-an-array","title":"Finding the index of items in an array","kind":"Read","text":"indexOf(\"Royal Arch\") returns an item's position, or -1 if it isn't in the array. includes(\"Mesa\") just answers true or false. Read"},{"id":"sec-adding-items","title":"Adding items","kind":"Read","text":"push() adds one or more items to the end; unshift() adds them to the start. Both change the array, and return its new length. Read"},{"id":"sec-removing-items","title":"Removing items","kind":"Read","text":"pop() removes the last item and shift() removes the first; both return the item they removed. To remove from the middle, find the index and use splice(index, howMany): const index = trails.indexOf(\"Royal Arch\"); if (index !== -1) { trails.splice(index, 1); // remove 1 item at that index } Some methods change the array itself (push, pop, splice); others leave it alone and hand you an answer (includes, indexOf, slice, join). Knowing which kind you're using saves a lot of confusion. Play"},{"id":"sec-change-the-array","title":"Change the array","kind":"Play","text":"Try each method and watch which ones change the array and which just return a value. New item Add and remove Read and search Convert Reset array Read"},{"id":"sec-accessing-every-item","title":"Accessing every item","kind":"Read","text":"Often you want to do something with every item. A for...of loop runs its code once per item: Two methods build a new array from an old one. map() runs a function on every item and collects the results. filter() keeps only the items for which a function returns true. You'll learn more about the functions inside them in Loops and Functions. Read"},{"id":"sec-converting-between-strings-and-arrays","title":"Converting between strings and arrays","kind":"Read","text":"split() breaks a string into an array at a separator. join() does the opposite, joining items with a separator you choose. (toString() also joins, but always with commas.) Try it"},{"id":"sec-pick-something-at-random","title":"Pick something at random","kind":"Try it","text":"Math.random() gives a number from 0 up to 1. Multiply it by the length and round down to get a valid index. Practice"},{"id":"sec-change-the-list","title":"Change the list","kind":"Practice","text":"Add and remove trails with array methods, then read from the end of the list. Practice"},{"id":"sec-printing-those-products","title":"Printing those products","kind":"Practice","text":"From MDN's arrays lesson: turn \"name:price\" strings into receipt lines and a total. Practice"},{"id":"sec-storing-the-previous-5-searches","title":"Storing the previous 5 searches","kind":"Practice","text":"Keep a search history with the newest first, and never more than five items."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"An array is an ordered list under one name: [\"Mesa\", \"Royal Arch\"]. length is how many items. Indexes start at 0; the last item is at length - 1. indexOf() returns -1 when not found. push/pop work at the end, unshift/shift at the start, splice in the middle. for...of visits every item; map() transforms them; filter() keeps some. split() turns a string into an array; join() turns an array into a string."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Reading trails[trails.length] for the last item. It's length - 1. Expecting slice() or map() to change the original array. Calling splice(index, 1) when indexOf returned -1, which removes the last item. Forgetting that a const array can still be changed with push and pop. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-arrays/quiz"],"exercises":["core-arrays/ex-arrays","core-arrays/ex-products","core-arrays/ex-history"],"questions":7},{"slug":"core-conditionals","num":"7.6","title":"Conditionals","desc":"if, else if and else; truthy and falsy; nesting; &&, || and !; switch statements; and the ternary operator.","props":["if / else","&& ||","switch","? :"],"intro":"Programs make choices: show the error or don't, say good morning or good evening, pick a color theme. Conditionals run code only when something is true.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-if-else-statements","title":"if...else statements","kind":"Read","text":"Basic if...else syntax A conditional runs a block of code only if a condition is true, and (optionally) a different block if it isn't: if (condition) { // runs if the condition is true } else { // runs otherwise } A real example MDN's example: a child is asked to help their parent. If they did the shopping, they get pocket money; if not, they don't. else if For more than two choices, chain else ifs. JavaScript checks each condition from top to bottom, runs the first block whose condition is true, then skips everything else, even if later conditions would also be true. So order matters: put the most specific conditions first. if (temp >= 85) { msg = \"Hike early. It'll be hot.\"; } else if (temp >= 50) { msg = \"Perfect hiking weather.\"; } else { msg = \"Bring layers.\"; } Play"},{"id":"sec-change-the-temperature","title":"Change the temperature","kind":"Play","text":"Change the value and watch which branch runs. Then flip the order of the conditions. const temp = ; Put the conditions in the wrong order A note on comparison operators Compare with <, >, <=, >=, === and !==. Always use three equals signs. A condition doesn't have to be a comparison: any value works. Six values count as false: false, 0, \"\", null, undefined and NaN. These are called falsy, and everything else is truthy. So if (name) checks whether someone typed anything. Watch out if (x = 5) doesn't compare. It sets x to 5, and since 5 is truthy, the block always runs. Play"},{"id":"sec-truthy-and-falsy","title":"Truthy and falsy","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a value and see whether the if block runs. const value = Nesting if...else You can put an if inside another one, to make a second decision only after the first: Logical operators: AND, OR and NOT Often you can avoid nesting by combining conditions: && (AND): true only if both sides are true. if (choice === \"sunny\" && temperature < 86) || (OR): true if at least one side is true. ! (NOT): flips true and false. if (!isRaining) A classic mistake: if (x === 5 || 7) doesn't mean \"x is 5 or 7\". It means \"x is 5, or 7 is truthy\", and 7 is always truthy, so it always runs. Write both comparisons in full: if (x === 5 || x === 7). Play"},{"id":"sec-and-or-not","title":"AND, OR, NOT","kind":"Play","text":"Flip the two values and switch the operator. const isWeekend = ; const isRaining = ; Operator && || Read"},{"id":"sec-switch-statements","title":"switch statements","kind":"Read","text":"When you're checking one value against lots of exact matches, a long else if chain gets repetitive. A switch statement is tidier: A switch example Each case is one possible value. When one matches, its code runs until break. If you leave out break, it carries on into the next case, which is handy for grouping (Saturday and Sunday below) but a bug if you forget. default runs if nothing matches. Read"},{"id":"sec-ternary-operator","title":"Ternary operator","kind":"Read","text":"The ternary operator is a one-line if...else that produces a value: condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse. It's ideal for picking between two things, like two greetings or two themes: Ternary operator example const greeting = isBirthday ? \"Happy birthday Mrs. Smith — we hope you have a great day!\" : \"Good morning Mrs. Smith.\"; Try it"},{"id":"sec-the-ternary-and-switch","title":"===, the ternary and switch","kind":"Try it","text":"Change the values and run it again. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-decision","title":"Fix the decision","kind":"Practice","text":"This function gives the wrong advice on hot days and ignores the rain. Reorder and add to it. Practice"},{"id":"sec-implementing-a-basic-calendar","title":"Implementing a basic calendar","kind":"Practice","text":"From MDN: how many days does a month have? Practice"},{"id":"sec-adding-more-color-choices","title":"Adding more color choices","kind":"Practice","text":"From MDN: turn a two-way choice into a switch with five themes."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"if / else if / else runs the first block whose condition is true, then stops checking. Compare with === and !==. Falsy values: false, 0, \"\", null, undefined, NaN. && needs both, || needs either, ! flips. switch matches one value against exact cases; don't forget break. condition ? a : b picks a value in one line."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"if (x = 5): one equals sign assigns instead of comparing. x === 5 || 7, which is always true. Write x === 5 || x === 7. Putting a general condition (>= 50) before a specific one (>= 85), so the specific one never runs. Forgetting break in a switch, so the next case runs too. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-conditionals/quiz"],"exercises":["core-conditionals/ex-cond","core-conditionals/ex-cal","core-conditionals/ex-theme"],"questions":7},{"slug":"core-loops","num":"8.1","title":"Loops","desc":"for...of, map() and filter(), the standard for loop, break and continue, while and do...while, and choosing the right loop.","props":["for...of","for (let i…)","break","while"],"intro":"Computers are good at doing the same thing over and over. A loop runs a block of code again and again: once for every item in a list, a set number of times, or until something changes.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-why-are-loops-useful","title":"Why are loops useful?","kind":"Read","text":"Say you have a list of 50 trails and want to show each name. You could write console.log() 50 times, or write it once and let a loop repeat it. Loops are how programs work through lists: every product in a shop, every photo in a gallery, every guess in a game. Looping code example MDN's example draws 100 random circles on a canvas. The drawing code is written once, inside a loop that runs it 100 times: for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) { ctx.beginPath(); ctx.fillStyle = \"rgb(255 0 0 / 50%)\"; ctx.arc(random(WIDTH), random(HEIGHT), random(50), 0, 2 * Math.PI); ctx.fill(); } With and without a loop Without the loop you'd need the four drawing lines 100 times over: 400 lines instead of 6. And changing \"100\" to \"1000\" would mean 3,600 more lines instead of editing one number. Play"},{"id":"sec-draw-with-a-loop","title":"Draw with a loop","kind":"Play","text":"Change how many times the loop runs, and redraw. loop runs Redraw Each pass through the loop draws one circle at a random position and size. Read"},{"id":"sec-looping-through-a-collection","title":"Looping through a collection","kind":"Read","text":"Most of the time you'll loop over a collection, usually an array. The for...of loop for...of runs its block once per item. Each time round, the variable (here trail) holds the current item. Each pass is one iteration. const trails = [\"Mesa\", \"Royal Arch\", \"Flagstaff\"]; for (const trail of trails) { console.log(trail); } map() and filter() Arrays also have methods that do the looping for you. map() calls a function on every item and collects the results in a new array. filter() calls a function on every item and keeps only the items where it returns true. Play"},{"id":"sec-map-and-filter","title":"map() and filter()","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a method and watch each item go through the function. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-standard-for-loop","title":"The standard for loop","kind":"Read","text":"A standard for loop counts. It has three parts, separated by semicolons: for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) { // runs with i = 0, 1, 2 … 9 } An initializer, let i = 0: a counter, run once at the start. A condition, i < 10: checked before each pass; the loop stops when it's false. A final expression, i++: run after each pass. Calculating squares Looping through collections with a for loop A for loop can walk an array too, using the counter as the index. Use it when you need the position, for example to treat the last item differently: Play"},{"id":"sec-step-through-a-loop","title":"Step through a loop","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a loop and press Step to run it one piece at a time. The two \"bug\" versions show the most common loop mistakes. Loop for (classic) for...of while for with <= (bug) while without i++ (bug) Step ▸ Auto-play Restart Read"},{"id":"sec-exiting-loops-with-break","title":"Exiting loops with break","kind":"Read","text":"break stops the loop immediately, even if there are items left. Use it when you've found what you were looking for. Read"},{"id":"sec-skipping-iterations-with-continue","title":"Skipping iterations with continue","kind":"Read","text":"continue skips the rest of the current pass and moves on to the next one. Try it"},{"id":"sec-break-and-continue","title":"break and continue","kind":"Try it","text":"Change the names and run it again. Read"},{"id":"sec-while-and-do-while","title":"while and do...while","kind":"Read","text":"A while loop repeats as long as its condition is true. It's like a for loop with the initializer before it and the final expression inside it, and it's handy when you don't know in advance how many times to go round. do...while checks the condition at the end, so its block always runs at least once. Watch out Every loop needs a way to stop. Forget the i++ in a while loop and its condition never becomes false: it runs forever and freezes the page. If that happens, close the tab, then check what changes the condition. Practice"},{"id":"sec-add-up-the-miles","title":"Add up the miles","kind":"Practice","text":"Start a total at 0, then add each item to it. Practice"},{"id":"sec-implementing-a-launch-countdown","title":"Implementing a launch countdown","kind":"Practice","text":"From MDN: count down from 10 with a while loop. Practice"},{"id":"sec-filling-in-a-guest-list","title":"Filling in a guest list","kind":"Practice","text":"From MDN: sort guests into admitted and refused. Read"},{"id":"sec-which-loop-type-should-you-use","title":"Which loop type should you use?","kind":"Read","text":"For going through every item in an array, use for...of: it's the simplest and hardest to get wrong. If you need the index, or to count a set number of times, use a standard for loop. To build a new array from an old one, reach for map() or filter(). Use while when you don't know how many passes you'll need, and keep do...while for the rare \"at least once\" case."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"A loop runs a block repeatedly; each pass is an iteration. for...of visits every item; map() transforms them; filter() keeps some. for (let i = 0; i < n; i++): initializer, condition, final expression. break stops the loop; continue skips to the next pass. while repeats while a condition is true; do...while always runs once."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"i <= arr.length, which runs one pass too many and reads undefined. A while loop with nothing that makes its condition false: an infinite loop. Declaring the total inside the loop, so it resets every pass. Expecting map() to change the original array. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-loops/quiz"],"exercises":["core-loops/ex-total","core-loops/ex-count","core-loops/ex-guests"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-functions","num":"8.2","title":"Functions","desc":"Built-in functions and methods, parameters and defaults, anonymous and arrow functions, scope, building your own function and return values.","props":["function","parameters","=>","return"],"intro":"A function is a named block of code you can run whenever you need it. You'll use the browser's built-in functions, write your own, pass values in and get values back.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-functions-reusable-blocks-of-code","title":"Functions: reusable blocks of code","kind":"Read","text":"A function is a named block of code that you can run whenever you need it, as many times as you like. You pass values in, it does some work, and it can hand a value back. function hikeTime(miles, pace = 2) { return miles / pace; } const hours = hikeTime(6, 3); // 2 The names in the definition, miles and pace, are parameters: placeholders waiting for values. The values you pass when you call it, 6 and 3, are arguments. pace = 2 is a default, used when no second argument is given. To run a function, you call it with parentheses: hikeTime(6, 3). Without the parentheses, hikeTime just refers to the function itself without running it, which is exactly what you want when you pass it to an event listener, but a bug everywhere else. return sends a value back to wherever the function was called, and ends the function right there. Forget the return and the function still runs, but its return value is undefined. You'll see functions written several ways. The arrow function is the short version: const hikeTime = (miles, pace = 2) => miles / pace;. It does the same thing. Variables declared inside a function only exist inside it. That's called scope, and it's a good thing: two functions can each have their own total without interfering with each other. Tip A good function does one job and is named with a verb that says what: hikeTime(), showError(), addTrail(). Read"},{"id":"sec-built-in-browser-functions","title":"Built-in browser functions","kind":"Read","text":"You've been using functions since your first line of JavaScript. Every time you write something with parentheses after it, you're calling one: const myText = \"I am a string\"; const newString = myText.replace(\"string\", \"sausage\"); // \"I am a sausage\" const myArray = [\"I\", \"love\", \"chocolate\", \"frogs\"]; const madeAString = myArray.join(\" \"); // \"I love chocolate frogs\" const myNumber = Math.random(); // between 0 and 1 These are built into the language or the browser. Someone wrote them once so you don't have to. Functions versus methods A function that belongs to an object is called a method. replace() belongs to strings, join() to arrays, random() to the Math object. You'll learn more about objects in the next lesson. For now, a method is just a function you call with a dot in front of it. Custom functions The functions you write yourself, with the function keyword and a name of your choosing, are custom functions. hikeTime() above is one. Invoking functions Defining a function doesn't run it. You run it (call or invoke it) by writing its name followed by parentheses. You can call it as many times as you like. Read"},{"id":"sec-function-parameters","title":"Function parameters","kind":"Read","text":"Some functions need information to do their job. Math.random() needs nothing; myText.replace(\"string\", \"sausage\") needs two things: what to find and what to put in its place. Those values are arguments; the names that receive them inside the definition are parameters. Optional parameters Some built-in parameters are optional. join() without an argument uses a comma: [\"a\", \"b\"].join() gives \"a,b\". Default parameters In your own functions, you make a parameter optional by giving it a default with =: Play"},{"id":"sec-follow-the-values","title":"Follow the values","kind":"Play","text":"Change the arguments and follow each value through the function. Then try the two common mistakes. Arguments miles pace (leave empty to use the default) Common mistakes Forget the return Call it without () Read"},{"id":"sec-anonymous-functions-and-arrow-functions","title":"Anonymous functions and arrow functions","kind":"Read","text":"A function doesn't need a name. An anonymous function is usually passed straight into another function that will call it later. Here, addEventListener() calls it whenever a key is pressed: textBox.addEventListener(\"keydown\", function (event) { console.log(`You pressed \"${event.key}\".`); }); An arrow function is a shorter way to write the same thing: drop function and put => after the parameters: textBox.addEventListener(\"keydown\", (event) => { console.log(`You pressed \"${event.key}\".`); }); With exactly one parameter, the parentheses are optional. If the body is a single return line, the braces and the word return can go too: Look"},{"id":"sec-three-ways-to-write-a-function","title":"Three ways to write a function","kind":"Look","text":"All three do the same thing. You'll see all of them in code online. MDN recommends arrow functions for short functions, especially ones you pass to another function. Read"},{"id":"sec-function-scope-and-conflicts","title":"Function scope and conflicts","kind":"Read","text":"Variables created inside a function only exist inside it: that's the function's scope. Code outside can't see them. Variables declared outside every function are in the global scope and can be seen everywhere. That's useful. If two scripts on the same page both had a global name, one would overwrite the other. Inside functions, each can have its own total or name without clashing. Play"},{"id":"sec-scope-where-can-a-variable-be-seen","title":"Scope: where can a variable be seen?","kind":"Play","text":"Click a cell to try using that variable from that place. Click a cell. Read"},{"id":"sec-build-your-own-function","title":"Build your own function","kind":"Read","text":"MDN's walkthrough builds a displayMessage() function that pops up a message box. It starts with no parameters, always showing the same text, then gains a msgText parameter for the message and a msgType parameter for the style. That's the general pattern: get it working for one case, then turn whatever changes into a parameter. function displayMessage(msgText, msgType) { const panel = document.createElement(\"div\"); panel.setAttribute(\"class\", \"msg-box\"); html.appendChild(panel); const msg = document.createElement(\"p\"); msg.textContent = msgText; panel.appendChild(msg); // … a close button, and a class for msgType } btn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => displayMessage(\"Brr, it's cold!\", \"warning\")); Notice the last line: addEventListener needs a function to call later, so you wrap the call in an arrow function. Writing displayMessage(…) directly would run it immediately. Play"},{"id":"sec-displaymessage","title":"displayMessage()","kind":"Play","text":"Fill in the two arguments and call the function. msgText msgType (none) warning chat Call displayMessage() Nothing shown yet. Try it"},{"id":"sec-write-your-own","title":"Write your own","kind":"Try it","text":"Change the arguments, add a default, or write a new function that calls these. Read"},{"id":"sec-function-return-values","title":"Function return values","kind":"Read","text":"What are return values? Some functions hand a value back when they finish: myText.replace(\"string\", \"sausage\") returns the new string, which you can store in a variable. Others, like displayMessage(), just do something and return nothing (strictly, they return undefined). Using return values in your own functions Use the return keyword. It sends a value back to the place the function was called, and ends the function there. That value can be stored, printed or passed straight into another function: isNaN() (\"is Not a Number\") is another built-in: it returns true if its argument can't be turned into a number. Try changing input to \"hello\". Practice"},{"id":"sec-write-functions-that-return","title":"Write functions that return","kind":"Practice","text":"Fix a function that forgets to return, give it a default, and write a second one as an arrow function. Practice"},{"id":"sec-functions-that-use-functions","title":"Functions that use functions","kind":"Practice","text":"Write small functions that each do one job, then combine them."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"A function is a named, reusable block of code. A method is a function that belongs to an object. Parameters are the names in the definition; arguments are the values you pass in. Default parameters fill in missing arguments: function greet(name = \"friend\"). Anonymous and arrow functions are often passed to other functions, like map() or addEventListener(). return sends a value back and ends the function. Variables declared inside a function stay inside it."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Using console.log() where you meant return. The value is printed, but the caller gets undefined. Passing a call instead of a function: btn.addEventListener(\"click\", go()) runs go right away. Using a variable declared inside a function from outside it: ReferenceError. Putting code after return and expecting it to run. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-functions/quiz"],"exercises":["core-functions/ex-func","core-functions/ex-math"],"questions":7},{"slug":"core-objects-json","num":"8.3","title":"Objects and JSON","desc":"Object literals, dot and bracket notation, setting members, this, constructors, JSON structure and rules, and JSON.parse() and JSON.stringify().","props":["{ key: value }","this","new","JSON.parse"],"intro":"An array is a list in order. An object groups related values under names, like everything you know about one trail. JSON is the same idea written as text, and it's how most data travels around the web.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-object-basics","title":"Object basics","kind":"Read","text":"An object is a collection of related data and functionality, written as key: value pairs inside curly braces. Each pair is a member. A member whose value is data is a property; a member whose value is a function is a method. const person = { name: [\"Bob\", \"Smith\"], age: 32, bio: function () { console.log(`${this.name[0]} ${this.name[1]} is ${this.age} years old.`); }, introduceSelf() { console.log(`Hi! I'm ${this.name[0]}.`); }, }; introduceSelf() { … } is a shorter way to write introduceSelf: function () { … }. An object written out like this, with braces, is an object literal. Separate members with commas. Read"},{"id":"sec-dot-notation","title":"Dot notation","kind":"Read","text":"To read a member, write the object's name, a dot, and the member's name. Call methods with parentheses: person.age // 32 person.name[0] // \"Bob\" person.bio(); // logs \"Bob Smith is 32 years old.\" Objects as object properties A value can itself be an object. Then you chain dots, one step at a time: const person = { name: { first: \"Bob\", last: \"Smith\" }, }; person.name.first // \"Bob\" Play"},{"id":"sec-read-the-object","title":"Read the object","kind":"Play","text":"Click any key to see the code that reads it. Click a key to read it Read"},{"id":"sec-bracket-notation","title":"Bracket notation","kind":"Read","text":"Square brackets with the key as a string do the same thing as a dot: person[\"age\"] is person.age. You need brackets when the key is in a variable, because a dot always looks for a key with that exact name. function logProperty(propertyName) { console.log(person[propertyName]); } logProperty(\"age\"); // 32 Play"},{"id":"sec-dot-or-brackets","title":"Dot or brackets?","kind":"Play","text":"When the key is chosen from a dropdown, it's in a variable, so you have to use brackets. const key = \"name\" \"miles\" \"open\" \"rating\" Read"},{"id":"sec-setting-object-members","title":"Setting object members","kind":"Read","text":"Change a member by assigning to it, with either notation. If the member doesn't exist yet, assigning creates it: Read"},{"id":"sec-what-is-this","title":"What is \"this\"?","kind":"Read","text":"Inside a method, this means the object the method was called on. That's why bio() above uses this.age and not person.age: the same method will then work on any object that has it. Read"},{"id":"sec-introducing-constructors","title":"Introducing constructors","kind":"Read","text":"Writing out every object by hand gets repetitive. A function can build them for you: function createPerson(name) { const obj = {}; obj.name = name; obj.introduceSelf = function () { console.log(`Hi! I'm ${this.name}.`); }; return obj; } A constructor is a neater version. Its name starts with a capital letter by convention, it uses this instead of creating and returning an object, and you call it with the new keyword: Read"},{"id":"sec-you-ve-been-using-objects-all-along","title":"You've been using objects all along","kind":"Read","text":"Strings, arrays, Math, console, document: they're all objects. myString.split(\",\") calls the split() method of a string object; document.querySelector() calls a method of the document object. That's what all those dots have been. Read"},{"id":"sec-working-with-json","title":"Working with JSON","kind":"Read","text":"What is JSON? JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a text format for structured data, based on JavaScript's object literal syntax. Servers send it, APIs return it, and config files are written in it. Because it's just text, any language can read it, but in JavaScript you first convert it into an object. JSON structure JSON looks like an object literal, and can contain strings, numbers, booleans, null, arrays and other objects: { \"squadName\": \"Super hero squad\", \"formed\": 2016, \"active\": true, \"members\": [ { \"name\": \"Molecule Man\", \"age\": 29, \"powers\": [\"Radiation resistance\", \"Turning tiny\"] } ] } Once parsed (say into superHeroes), you read it like any object: superHeroes.members[0].powers[1] is \"Turning tiny\". Arrays as JSON A JSON file doesn't have to start with {. An array, [ {…}, {…} ], is valid JSON too, and a common way to send a list of things. Other notes JSON is purely data. It can't contain functions (methods) or comments. Property names and strings must use double quotes. Single quotes are invalid. No trailing comma after the last item. One misplaced comma or colon breaks the whole file. A validator helps find it. Play"},{"id":"sec-json-checker","title":"JSON checker","kind":"Play","text":"Edit the text, or click a button to make a common mistake. The status uses the browser's own JSON.parse(). Break it Reset to valid JSON Keys and strings in \"double quotes\" No trailing commas No comments No functions or undefined Allowed: strings, numbers, true, false, null, arrays, objects Read"},{"id":"sec-converting-between-objects-and-text","title":"Converting between objects and text","kind":"Read","text":"JSON arrives as a string. Two built-in methods convert it: JSON.parse(text) turns JSON text into an object you can read with dots. JSON.stringify(obj) turns an object into JSON text, ready to save or send. When you load JSON from a server with fetch(), the response's json() method does the parsing for you. You'll do that in Fetching Data. Try it"},{"id":"sec-parse-and-stringify","title":"parse and stringify","kind":"Try it","text":"This is MDN's superheroes example. Practice"},{"id":"sec-read-and-write-json","title":"Read and write JSON","kind":"Practice","text":"Turn JSON text into an object, pull values out of it, and turn it back into text. Practice"},{"id":"sec-make-trails-with-a-constructor","title":"Make trails with a constructor","kind":"Practice","text":"Write a constructor with a method that uses this."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"An object is a set of key: value members in braces. Data members are properties; function members are methods. Read with a dot (hike.miles) or brackets (hike[\"miles\"]). Use brackets when the key is in a variable. Assign to change or add a member: hike.rating = 5; Inside a method, this is the object it was called on. Constructors use this and are called with new. JSON is text. JSON.parse(text) makes an object; JSON.stringify(obj) makes text."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"hike.key when key is a variable. It looks for a key literally named \"key\". Use hike[key]. Single quotes, trailing commas or comments in JSON. Any one of them makes it fail to parse. Using JSON text as if it were an object: text.members is undefined until you parse it. Forgetting new when calling a constructor. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-objects-json/quiz"],"exercises":["core-objects-json/ex-objects","core-objects-json/ex-ctor"],"questions":7},{"slug":"core-dom-scripting","num":"8.4","title":"DOM Scripting","desc":"window, navigator and document, the DOM tree, querySelector() and textContent, creating, moving and removing nodes, and changing styles with classList and style.","props":["querySelector()","textContent","appendChild()","classList"],"intro":"When the browser reads your HTML, it builds a tree of objects in memory. JavaScript can find any element in that tree and change its text, its attributes, its classes and its styles, and the page updates immediately.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-the-important-parts-of-a-web-browser","title":"The important parts of a web browser","kind":"Read","text":"JavaScript in a web page can reach several objects the browser provides: window is the browser tab the page is loaded into. It knows things like the window's size (window.innerWidth) and can run code when it's resized. navigator represents the browser itself: its preferred language, the user's location (with permission), and more. document is the page loaded into the window. It's your way into the DOM, the Document Object Model, and the object you'll use most. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-document-object-model","title":"The document object model","kind":"Read","text":"The DOM is a tree of objects, one for each element (and each piece of text) in the page. JavaScript can't change your HTML file, but it can change the DOM, and the page updates immediately. The DOM uses the same family words as CSS. Each object in the tree is a node. The top one, <html>, is the root. A parent node directly contains its children; children of the same parent are siblings; and anything inside a node, at any depth, is a descendant. Read"},{"id":"sec-basic-dom-manipulation","title":"Basic DOM manipulation","kind":"Read","text":"To change an element, first find it and store a reference to it in a variable. querySelector() takes any CSS selector and returns the first match: const link = document.querySelector(\"a\"); link.textContent = \"Mozilla Developer Network\"; link.href = \"https://developer.mozilla.org\"; textContent reads or replaces an element's text, and attributes like href and src are properties you can set directly. querySelectorAll() returns every match as a list you can loop over. Older code uses getElementById() and getElementsByTagName(); they still work, but querySelector() handles any selector. Watch out If nothing matches, querySelector() returns null, and using it gives the error \"Cannot read properties of null.\" That almost always means a typo in your selector, or a script that ran before the element existed. Play"},{"id":"sec-select-elements","title":"Select elements","kind":"Play","text":"Pick a method, type a selector and see what it matches. Method querySelector() querySelectorAll() getElementById() Selector Play"},{"id":"sec-the-dom-tree","title":"The DOM tree","kind":"Play","text":"This is the mini page above as the browser sees it. Matches from your selector are highlighted. Click any node to see how it relates to the others. Read"},{"id":"sec-creating-and-placing-new-nodes","title":"Creating and placing new nodes","kind":"Read","text":"document.createElement() makes a new element in memory. It doesn't appear anywhere until you add it to the page, usually with appendChild(), which places it as the last child of a parent: You'll do much more of this, including a shopping list, in Creating Elements. Read"},{"id":"sec-moving-and-removing-elements","title":"Moving and removing elements","kind":"Read","text":"Appending an element that's already in the page moves it rather than copying it. To make a copy, use cloneNode(). To take an element out, call its remove() method (older code calls parent.removeChild(child)). Read"},{"id":"sec-manipulating-styles","title":"Manipulating styles","kind":"Read","text":"There are two ways to change how an element looks. The first sets inline styles through the style property. CSS properties with hyphens become camelCase, because hyphens aren't allowed in JavaScript names: para.style.color = \"white\"; para.style.backgroundColor = \"black\"; para.style.padding = \"10px\"; The second, usually better, adds a class and keeps the styles in your CSS file, where they belong. classList has add(), remove() and toggle(): para.classList.add(\"highlight\"); Tip Write the selector in your CSS file first and make sure it styles the right element. Then use the same selector in JavaScript. Play"},{"id":"sec-change-what-you-selected","title":"Change what you selected","kind":"Play","text":"These act on whatever your selector matched above. Try them on .featured, then on h2 with querySelectorAll. Reset the mini page Pick a change. Practice"},{"id":"sec-change-a-card-with-the-dom","title":"Change a card with the DOM","kind":"Practice","text":"Write a function that finds parts of a trail card and updates them. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fix-the-link","title":"Fix the link","kind":"Practice","text":"From MDN's basic DOM manipulation: change a link, then add a paragraph after it."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"window is the tab, navigator the browser, and document the page and its DOM. querySelector() returns the first match, or null; querySelectorAll() returns every match. Change text with textContent and attributes like href directly. createElement() makes an element; appendChild() places it (or moves it); remove() takes it out. Prefer classList over style, so styles stay in CSS."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"A script that runs before the elements exist, so querySelector() returns null. Use defer. Treating the list from querySelectorAll() as one element: cards.classList is undefined. Loop over it. Writing el.style.background-color. Use camelCase: backgroundColor. Using innerHTML with text people typed. Use textContent. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-dom-scripting/quiz"],"exercises":["core-dom-scripting/ex-dom","core-dom-scripting/ex-link"],"questions":7},{"slug":"core-events","num":"9.1","title":"Events","desc":"addEventListener() and removing listeners, other events, event objects, preventDefault(), bubbling and capture, and event delegation.","props":["addEventListener()","e.target","preventDefault()","bubbling"],"intro":"Most JavaScript on a page waits for something to happen: a click, a key press, a form being submitted. Events are how your code finds out, so it can respond.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-is-an-event","title":"What is an event?","kind":"Read","text":"An event is a signal the browser sends when something happens: the user clicks, presses a key, submits a form or resizes the window; a video finishes; a page finishes loading. Your code can respond by attaching an event listener (a function that runs when the event fires, also called an event handler). Read"},{"id":"sec-using-addeventlistener","title":"Using addEventListener()","kind":"Read","text":"Give addEventListener() the event's name as a string, then the function to run. MDN's example changes the page's background color every time a button is clicked: const btn = document.querySelector(\"button\"); function random(number) { return Math.floor(Math.random() * (number + 1)); } btn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => { const rndCol = `rgb(${random(255)} ${random(255)} ${random(255)})`; document.body.style.backgroundColor = rndCol; }); Play"},{"id":"sec-handling-a-click-event","title":"Handling a click event","kind":"Play","text":"The same code, aimed at a box instead of the whole page. Change color Each click runs the listener once. click the button Listening for other events There are many events. Some useful ones: click and dblclick; focus and blur when a field gains or loses focus; input on every change to a field; keydown; mouseover and mouseout; and submit on a form. Not every element fires every event: submit only comes from forms. Play"},{"id":"sec-listen-for-events","title":"Listen for events","kind":"Play","text":"Pick an event type and interact with the elements to see when it fires. Listen for e.preventDefault() on submit Clear log Click or double-click me Move the pointer in and out of this box Trail name (type, tab in and out, press Enter) Submit Removing listeners removeEventListener() takes the same event name and the same function you added. That's one reason to use named functions for listeners you'll remove later: an anonymous function can't be named again. Adding multiple listeners You can add several listeners for the same event on the same element. They all run, in the order you added them. Read"},{"id":"sec-other-event-listener-mechanisms","title":"Other event listener mechanisms","kind":"Read","text":"Event handler properties Elements also have properties like onclick you can set to a function: btn.onclick = bgChange;. It works, but each property holds only one function, so a second assignment replaces the first. Inline event handlers: don't use these Old code sometimes puts JavaScript straight into HTML: <button onclick=\"bgChange()\">. It mixes JavaScript into your markup, is hard to maintain, and is blocked by many sites' security settings. Keep your JavaScript in your script with addEventListener(). Read"},{"id":"sec-event-objects","title":"Event objects","kind":"Read","text":"The browser passes your listener an event object, usually named e, evt or event. event.target is the element the event happened on, so one function can handle events from many elements. Extra properties of event objects Different events carry different information. A keydown event's key property tells you which key was pressed: textBox.addEventListener(\"keydown\", (event) => { output.textContent = `You pressed \"${event.key}\".`; }); Read"},{"id":"sec-preventing-default-behavior","title":"Preventing default behavior","kind":"Read","text":"Some events come with a default action. Submitting a form sends it and reloads the page; clicking a link navigates away. preventDefault() stops that, so your code can handle it instead, for example to check the fields first: form.addEventListener(\"submit\", (e) => { if (fname.value === \"\" || lname.value === \"\") { e.preventDefault(); para.textContent = \"You need to fill in both names!\"; } }); Watch out Listen for submit on the form, not click on its button. Pressing Enter in a field submits the form without clicking anything. Read"},{"id":"sec-event-bubbling","title":"Event bubbling","kind":"Read","text":"Events travel. A click on a button inside a <div> fires on the button first, then on the div, then on <body>, all the way up to the document. This is event bubbling: the event bubbles up from the innermost element. Stopping propagation Calling event.stopPropagation() in a listener stops the event going any further up. MDN's example is a video inside a box: clicking the video plays it, but without stopPropagation() the click also reaches the box, whose listener hides it. Event capture There's also an earlier capture phase, which goes the other way: from the outside in. Listeners only run during it if you ask, with addEventListener(\"click\", handler, { capture: true }). You'll rarely need it. Play"},{"id":"sec-bubbling","title":"Bubbling","kind":"Play","text":"Click the button and watch the click bubble up through every ancestor. This is the setup from MDN's bubbling example. e.stopPropagation() in the button's listener { capture: true } on body and div body div#container Click me Click the button, then click in the div outside the button. Read"},{"id":"sec-event-delegation","title":"Event delegation","kind":"Read","text":"Bubbling makes a powerful trick possible. Instead of adding a listener to each of 50 items, add one to their container and check event.target to see which item was clicked. That's event delegation, and it even works for items added later. target and currentTarget event.target is the element the event started on (the innermost one). event.currentTarget is the element whose listener is running now: in delegation, that's the container. Play"},{"id":"sec-event-delegation-2","title":"Event delegation","kind":"Play","text":"One listener on the container handles clicks on all 16 tiles. Ignore clicks that aren't on a tile Click some tiles, then click in a gap between them. Reset tiles Practice"},{"id":"sec-respond-to-events","title":"Respond to events","kind":"Practice","text":"Wire up a click counter and a form that doesn't reload the page. Practice"},{"id":"sec-keys-and-delegation","title":"Keys and delegation","kind":"Practice","text":"Read a property of the event object, then handle a whole list with one listener."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"el.addEventListener(\"click\", fn) runs fn every time the event fires. removeEventListener() needs the same function. Pass the function itself: fn, not fn(). The event object tells you what happened: e.target, e.key and more. e.preventDefault() stops a default action like a form submit. Events bubble up through ancestors; stopPropagation() stops them. Delegation puts one listener on a container."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"addEventListener(\"click\", go()): runs go once, immediately. Listening for click on a submit button instead of submit on the form. Inline onclick=\"…\" attributes in HTML. Using e.target in delegation without checking it's the kind of element you expect. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-events/quiz"],"exercises":["core-events/ex-events","core-events/ex-keys"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-creating-elements","num":"9.2","title":"Creating Elements","desc":"createElement(), appendChild() and remove(), textContent versus innerHTML, MDN's dynamic shopping list, and an image gallery built from data.","props":["createElement()","appendChild()","remove()","tabIndex"],"intro":"So far you've changed elements that were already in the HTML. JavaScript can also build new ones: that's how a to-do list grows when you add an item, how search results appear, and how a gallery fills itself from a list of images.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-creating-and-placing-new-nodes","title":"Creating and placing new nodes","kind":"Read","text":"So far you've changed elements that were already in the HTML. JavaScript can also build new ones, which is how a to-do list grows when you add an item, or how search results appear. It takes three steps: const li = document.createElement(\"li\"); // 1. make it li.textContent = \"Trail mix\"; // 2. fill it li.classList.add(\"item\"); list.append(li); // 3. attach it createElement() makes an element in memory. It isn't on the page yet, and it won't show up anywhere until you attach it to something that is. Forgetting that last step is the most common reason a new element never appears. append() adds it as the last child; prepend() adds it as the first. You'll also see the older appendChild(), which works the same way. To take an element away, call remove() on it: li.remove(). An element can only be in one place at a time. Appending an element that's already on the page moves it rather than copying it. To make a copy, use cloneNode(true). There's a shortcut you'll see a lot: setting innerHTML to a string of HTML. It's handy for building markup you wrote yourself. But never use it for text a visitor typed. If someone types <img src=x onerror=…>, innerHTML turns it into a real element, and the code inside runs. textContent shows exactly what was typed and never runs it. Tip After adding an item to a list, clear the input and put focus back in it with input.focus(), so people can type the next one straight away. Play"},{"id":"sec-created-isn-t-the-same-as-on-the-page","title":"Created isn't the same as on the page","kind":"Play","text":"createElement() makes an element in memory. It doesn't show up anywhere until you attach it to something that's already on the page. Next line ▸ Restart In memory (not on the page) On the page Trail mix Water Read"},{"id":"sec-a-dynamic-shopping-list","title":"A dynamic shopping list","kind":"Read","text":"MDN's shopping list puts it all together. The HTML is a label, an input, a button and an empty list. The JavaScript, step by step: Store references to the list, the input and the button. Add a listener to the button (or better, the form's submit). In it, store the input's current value, then empty the input. Create an <li>, a <span> and a <button>. Append the span and button to the list item, set the span's text to the value and the button's text to \"Delete\", then append the list item to the list. Add a listener to the delete button that removes its list item. Finally, call input.focus() so people can type the next item straight away. form.addEventListener(\"submit\", (event) => { event.preventDefault(); const myItem = input.value; input.value = \"\"; const listItem = document.createElement(\"li\"); const listText = document.createElement(\"span\"); const listBtn = document.createElement(\"button\"); listItem.appendChild(listText); listText.textContent = myItem; listItem.appendChild(listBtn); listBtn.textContent = \"Delete\"; list.appendChild(listItem); listBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => { list.removeChild(listItem); }); input.focus(); }); Play"},{"id":"sec-build-a-shopping-list","title":"Build a shopping list","kind":"Play","text":"Add and delete items and watch which lines of code run. Then try each mistake. Try a mistake Leave out event.preventDefault() Forget list.appendChild(listItem) Use innerHTML instead of textContent Add a few items, then turn on a mistake and add another. For the innerHTML one, try typing <b>Milk</b>. My shopping list Enter a new item Add item Add an item. Practice"},{"id":"sec-build-and-remove-list-items","title":"Build and remove list items","kind":"Practice","text":"Write two functions: one adds an item to a list, the other clears out finished ones. Read"},{"id":"sec-an-image-gallery","title":"An image gallery","kind":"Read","text":"MDN's image gallery challenge builds a set of thumbnails from data. Clicking a thumbnail shows it large; a button darkens and lightens the big image. Start from data Instead of writing five <img> elements in the HTML, keep the images in an array of objects. Each object has the file name and the alt text: const images = [ { filename: \"pic1.jpg\", alt: \"Closeup of a human eye\" }, { filename: \"pic2.jpg\", alt: \"Rock that looks like a wave\" }, // … ]; Looping through the images Loop through the array. For each item, create an <img>, set its src and alt, and append it to the thumbnail bar: for (const image of images) { const newImage = document.createElement(\"img\"); newImage.src = `images/${image.filename}`; newImage.alt = image.alt; newImage.tabIndex = 0; thumbBar.appendChild(newImage); newImage.addEventListener(\"click\", updateDisplayedImage); newImage.addEventListener(\"keydown\", (e) => { if (e.code === \"Enter\") updateDisplayedImage(e); }); } function updateDisplayedImage(e) { displayedImage.src = e.target.src; displayedImage.alt = e.target.alt; } Images can't normally be focused, so tabIndex = 0 puts each one in the tab order, and the keydown listener lets keyboard users press Enter to choose it. (In your own work a <button> around each image gets you both for free.) The darken/lighten button The button's listener checks its own class. If it's dark, it switches to light, changes its text to \"Lighten\" and shows a translucent black overlay; otherwise it does the reverse. classList.toggle() is a compact way to flip a class on and off. Play"},{"id":"sec-image-gallery","title":"Image gallery","kind":"Play","text":"Built entirely by the loop above. Click a thumbnail, or tab to one and press Enter. Darken Practice"},{"id":"sec-build-the-thumbnails","title":"Build the thumbnails","kind":"Practice","text":"Turn an array of image data into thumbnails that update the big image."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"Three steps: createElement() to make it, fill it in, then appendChild() or append() to attach it. A created element isn't on the page until it's attached to something that is. remove() takes an element out; appending one that's already on the page moves it. Use textContent for anything a visitor typed, never innerHTML. Keep repeated content in an array of objects and build the elements in a loop."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Creating an element and never attaching it. Using innerHTML with user input, which can run code. Adding listeners to elements before they've been created. Clickable images or divs with no keyboard support. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-creating-elements/quiz"],"exercises":["core-creating-elements/ex-create","core-creating-elements/ex-gallery"],"questions":6},{"slug":"core-fetching-data","num":"9.3","title":"Fetching Data","desc":"The Fetch API, promises and .then(), fetching text and JSON, response.ok and error handling, async and await, and XMLHttpRequest.","props":["fetch()",".then()","response.json()","await"],"intro":"Many pages update part of themselves without reloading: new search results, the latest weather, more posts as you scroll. They ask a server for data with fetch(), then use the DOM to show it.","more":"Read","sections":[{"id":"sec-what-is-the-problem-here","title":"What is the problem here?","kind":"Read","text":"Traditionally, every click on a link loaded a whole new page from the server, even if only one small part changed. That's slow and wasteful. Instead, JavaScript can request just the data it needs and update part of the page with the DOM. This is sometimes called Ajax, and today it's done with the Fetch API. Fetching also saves bandwidth: the browser can cache files, and only the new data travels over the network. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-fetch-api","title":"The Fetch API","kind":"Read","text":"The answer to a request takes time to arrive, from a few milliseconds to several seconds. JavaScript doesn't stop and wait; the code after fetch() keeps running right away. Instead, fetch() returns a promise: a placeholder for a value that will arrive later. Fetching text content MDN's first example loads a text file and shows it. You pass a function to the promise's .then() method, and it runs when the response arrives: fetch(url) .then((response) => { if (!response.ok) { throw new Error(`HTTP error: ${response.status}`); } return response.text(); }) .then((text) => { poemDisplay.textContent = text; }) .catch((error) => { poemDisplay.textContent = `Could not fetch verse: ${error}`; }); The first .then() gets a Response object. Reading its body with response.text() takes time too, so it returns another promise, which the second .then() waits for. .catch() runs if anything along the chain fails. Play"},{"id":"sec-trail-notes","title":"Trail notes","kind":"Play","text":"These are real requests for small text files in this site's data/notes folder. Try the missing one too. Choose a trail Mesa Trail Royal Arch Bear Peak Flagstaff (no file) Open DevTools' Network tab to see each request. Watch out A 404 \"not found\" is a completed request as far as fetch() is concerned, so it won't land in .catch() by itself. Check response.ok and throw an error when it's false. And pages opened by double-clicking the file (file://) can't fetch: use a local server such as VS Code's Live Server, or test on GitHub Pages. Play"},{"id":"sec-watch-a-request-happen","title":"Watch a request happen","kind":"Play","text":"Send a request and follow it to the server and back. Try a slow network and a missing file. What happens on the server Code style .then() async / await Send request ▸ Boulder trails Press Send request. Network panel Read"},{"id":"sec-fetching-json","title":"Fetching JSON","kind":"Read","text":"MDN's second example, the \"can store\", fetches a JSON list of products and builds the page from it. The only difference is the method that reads the body: response.json() reads it and parses it, so you get an array of objects instead of text. Look"},{"id":"sec-the-file-being-fetched","title":"The file being fetched","kind":"Look","text":"The server sends back plain text in JSON format. response.json() parses it into an array of objects, which the code loops over to build the list. trails.json showTrails() Read"},{"id":"sec-async-and-await","title":"async and await","kind":"Read","text":"Chains of .then() work, but there's a tidier way to write the same thing. Inside a function marked async, await pauses that function until a promise settles, and gives you its value. Errors are caught with an ordinary try...catch: Both styles do the same thing. You'll see both in code online, and MDN's Asynchronous JavaScript module goes further. Fetching other kinds of data response.blob() reads the body as raw data, like an image file. URL.createObjectURL(blob) then gives you a URL you can put in an <img>'s src. Read"},{"id":"sec-the-xmlhttprequest-api","title":"The XMLHttpRequest API","kind":"Read","text":"Before fetch(), requests were made with XMLHttpRequest (often \"XHR\"). You'll still find it in older code and tutorials. It does the same job with more ceremony: create the object, attach listeners, then open() and send(). Use fetch() for new code. Practice"},{"id":"sec-handle-the-response","title":"Handle the response","kind":"Practice","text":"Write the pieces a fetch needs: a check for failed responses, and a function that pulls out the data you want. Practice"},{"id":"sec-fetch-a-trail-note","title":"Fetch a trail note","kind":"Practice","text":"A real request: fetch a text file, and fall back to a message if it's missing."},{"id":"sec-key-ideas","title":"Key ideas","kind":"notes","text":"fetch(url) returns a promise for a Response; the code after it keeps running. Read the body with response.text(), response.json() or response.blob(), each a promise too. A 404 doesn't reject: check response.ok and throw. Handle failures with .catch(), or try...catch inside an async function with await. XMLHttpRequest is the older way; prefer fetch()."},{"id":"sec-common-mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","kind":"notes","text":"Using the data on the line after fetch(), before it has arrived. Forgetting that response.json() needs its own .then() or await. Not checking response.ok, so a 404 page gets parsed as data. Opening the page as file://, where fetch is blocked. Check yourself"},{"id":"sec-quick-check","title":"Quick check","kind":"Check yourself","text":""}],"quizzes":["core-fetching-data/quiz"],"exercises":["core-fetching-data/ex-fetch","core-fetching-data/ex-note"],"questions":6}],"terms":[{"term":"!important","id":"term-important","cat":"css","def":"A flag that makes a declaration beat every normal rule, regardless of specificity. Avoid it: it makes styles hard to override later.","ex":"color: red !important;","page":"cascade.html"},{"term":"Absolute path","id":"term-absolute-path","cat":"html","def":"A full web address including the domain. Use it to link to other websites.","ex":"https://developer.mozilla.org","page":"links-paths.html"},{"term":"Accessibility (a11y)","id":"term-accessibility-a11y","cat":"workflow","def":"Making sites usable by everyone, including people using screen readers, keyboards, zoom, or captions. \"a11y\" is short for the 11 letters between a and y.","ex":"","page":"accessibility.html"},{"term":"Alt text","id":"term-alt-text","cat":"html","def":"The alt attribute on an image: a text description read by screen readers and shown if the image fails to load. Use alt=\"\" for decorative images.","ex":"<img src=\"arch.jpg\" alt=\"Royal Arch above the trees\">","page":"images.html"},{"term":"Animation","id":"term-animation","cat":"css","def":"Motion defined with @keyframes that runs by itself, as opposed to a transition, which runs when a style changes.","ex":"animation: pulse 1s infinite;","page":"transitions.html"},{"term":"aspect-ratio","id":"term-aspect-ratio","cat":"css","def":"Keeps an element's width-to-height shape as its size changes.","ex":"aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;","page":"sizing.html"},{"term":"Attribute","id":"term-attribute","cat":"html","def":"Extra information in an element's opening tag, written as name=\"value\".","ex":"<a href=\"about.html\">","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Attribute selector","id":"term-attribute-selector","cat":"css","def":"Selects elements by their attributes, using square brackets.","ex":"a[href$=\".pdf\"]","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"BEM","id":"term-bem","cat":"workflow","def":"A class-naming pattern: Block, Element, Modifier. It makes it clear which classes belong together.","ex":".card__title, .btn--large","page":"organizing-css.html"},{"term":"Block element","id":"term-block-element","cat":"css","def":"An element that starts on a new line and fills the available width, like a div, p or h1.","ex":"display: block;","page":"display.html"},{"term":"Border","id":"term-border","cat":"css","def":"The line around an element's padding. Part of the box model, between padding and margin.","ex":"border: 2px solid gray;","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"border-radius","id":"term-border-radius","cat":"css","def":"Rounds an element's corners. 50% on a square makes a circle.","ex":"border-radius: 8px;","page":"backgrounds-borders.html"},{"term":"box-shadow","id":"term-box-shadow","cat":"css","def":"Draws a shadow around an element's box, set by x and y offset, blur, spread and color.","ex":"box-shadow: 0 4px 12px rgb(0 0 0 / 15%);","page":"shadows.html"},{"term":"box-sizing","id":"term-box-sizing","cat":"css","def":"Decides whether width includes padding and border. border-box is easier to work with, and most stylesheets set it on everything.","ex":"* { box-sizing: border-box; }","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"Box model","id":"term-box-model","cat":"css","def":"Every element is a rectangle made of four layers: content, padding, border and margin.","ex":"","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"Breakpoint","id":"term-breakpoint","cat":"layout","def":"A screen width where a media query changes the layout.","ex":"@media (min-width: 600px)","page":"media-queries.html"},{"term":"Browser cache","id":"term-browser-cache","cat":"workflow","def":"Saved copies of files your browser keeps to load pages faster. A hard refresh (Ctrl+Shift+R, or Cmd+Shift+R on a Mac) skips it to show your latest changes.","ex":"","page":"publishing.html"},{"term":"Cascade","id":"term-cascade","cat":"css","def":"The rules the browser uses to choose between conflicting declarations: importance, then specificity, then source order.","ex":"","page":"cascade.html"},{"term":"Character entity","id":"term-character-entity","cat":"html","def":"A code for a character that would otherwise be read as HTML, or is hard to type.","ex":"&lt;  &amp;  &copy;","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Child","id":"term-child","cat":"html","def":"An element directly inside another element. The outer one is its parent.","ex":"<ul> <li>child</li> </ul>","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Class","id":"term-class","cat":"css","def":"A reusable label added with the class attribute and selected in CSS with a dot. Many elements can share a class.","ex":".trail-card  →  <div class=\"trail-card\">","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"clear","id":"term-clear","cat":"layout","def":"Makes an element start below any floated elements before it.","ex":"clear: both;","page":"floats.html"},{"term":"Combinator","id":"term-combinator","cat":"css","def":"The part of a selector that describes a relationship: space (descendant), > (child), + (next sibling), ~ (later sibling).","ex":".card > a","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Comment","id":"term-comment","cat":"html","def":"Notes in your code that the browser ignores. HTML and CSS use different syntax.","ex":"<!-- HTML -->   /* CSS */","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Contrast ratio","id":"term-contrast-ratio","cat":"workflow","def":"How different text and background colors are in brightness. Body text needs at least 4.5 : 1.","ex":"","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"Cross axis","id":"term-cross-axis","cat":"layout","def":"In Flexbox, the direction perpendicular to the main axis. align-items works along it.","ex":"","page":"flexbox.html"},{"term":"CSS","id":"term-css","cat":"css","def":"Cascading Style Sheets: the language that controls how HTML looks, from colors and fonts to layout.","ex":"","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"CSS variable","id":"term-css-variable","cat":"css","def":"A reusable value with a name starting with two dashes, read with var(). Also called a custom property.","ex":"--brand: #2340d9;  color: var(--brand);","page":"css-variables.html"},{"term":"Declaration","id":"term-declaration","cat":"css","def":"One property and its value, ending in a semicolon.","ex":"color: navy;","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Descendant","id":"term-descendant","cat":"html","def":"Any element inside another one, at any depth: children, grandchildren and so on.","ex":".card p","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"DevTools","id":"term-devtools","cat":"workflow","def":"Tools built into every browser for inspecting the HTML and CSS of a page. Open with F12, or Cmd+Option+I on a Mac.","ex":"","page":"devtools.html"},{"term":"display","id":"term-display","cat":"css","def":"The property that sets how an element is laid out: block, inline, inline-block, flex, grid or none.","ex":"display: inline-block;","page":"display.html"},{"term":"DOCTYPE","id":"term-doctype","cat":"html","def":"The first line of every HTML file. It tells the browser to use modern standards mode.","ex":"<!DOCTYPE html>","page":"document-structure.html"},{"term":"DOM","id":"term-dom","cat":"js","def":"Document Object Model: the tree of elements the browser builds from your HTML. JavaScript reads and changes the page through it, and DevTools' Elements panel shows it after the browser has repaired any errors.","ex":"document.querySelector(\"h1\")","page":"js-dom.html"},{"term":"Element","id":"term-element","cat":"html","def":"A piece of a web page: an opening tag, its content and a closing tag.","ex":"<p>Hello</p>","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"em (unit)","id":"term-em-unit","cat":"css","def":"A length relative to the current element's font size. Nested em sizes multiply.","ex":"padding: 1em;","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"Empty element","id":"term-empty-element","cat":"html","def":"An element with no content and no closing tag. Also called a void element.","ex":"<img>  <br>  <input>","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Fallback font","id":"term-fallback-font","cat":"css","def":"The next font in a font-family list, used when the first isn't available. Always end with a generic family like serif or sans-serif.","ex":"font-family: \"Lora\", Georgia, serif;","page":"web-fonts.html"},{"term":"figure / figcaption","id":"term-figure-figcaption","cat":"html","def":"Wraps an image, chart or code sample with its visible caption.","ex":"<figure><img …><figcaption>…</figcaption></figure>","page":"images.html"},{"term":"filter","id":"term-filter","cat":"css","def":"Applies visual effects like blur, grayscale or drop shadows to an element.","ex":"filter: grayscale(1);","page":"shadows.html"},{"term":"Flexbox","id":"term-flexbox","cat":"layout","def":"A layout method for arranging items in one row or column, with control over spacing and alignment.","ex":"display: flex;","page":"flexbox.html"},{"term":"Float","id":"term-float","cat":"layout","def":"Moves an element to the left or right so text wraps around it.","ex":"float: left;","page":"floats.html"},{"term":"Focus","id":"term-focus","cat":"workflow","def":"The element that keyboard input goes to. Tab moves focus; :focus-visible styles show where it is.","ex":"","page":"pseudo-classes.html"},{"term":"Font stack","id":"term-font-stack","cat":"css","def":"A list of fonts in font-family, tried in order.","ex":"font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;","page":"typography.html"},{"term":"fr (unit)","id":"term-fr-unit","cat":"layout","def":"A fraction of the free space in a grid container.","ex":"grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr;","page":"grid.html"},{"term":"Gap","id":"term-gap","cat":"layout","def":"Space between items in a flex or grid container, without margins on each item.","ex":"gap: 16px;","page":"flexbox.html"},{"term":"GitHub Pages","id":"term-github-pages","cat":"workflow","def":"A free service that publishes the HTML, CSS and JavaScript files in a GitHub repository as a website.","ex":"","page":"publishing.html"},{"term":"Gradient","id":"term-gradient","cat":"css","def":"A smooth blend between colors, used as a background image.","ex":"linear-gradient(135deg, teal, navy)","page":"backgrounds-borders.html"},{"term":"Grid","id":"term-grid","cat":"layout","def":"A layout method for rows and columns at the same time.","ex":"display: grid;","page":"grid.html"},{"term":"Head","id":"term-head","cat":"html","def":"The part of an HTML document with information about the page, like its title and stylesheet links. Not shown on the page.","ex":"<head> … </head>","page":"document-structure.html"},{"term":"Heading","id":"term-heading","cat":"html","def":"h1 to h6 elements that give a page its outline. Use one h1 and don't skip levels.","ex":"<h2>Conditions</h2>","page":"text-semantics.html"},{"term":"Hex color","id":"term-hex-color","cat":"css","def":"A color written as # followed by pairs of red, green and blue values from 00 to ff.","ex":"#2340d9","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"HSL","id":"term-hsl","cat":"css","def":"A color written as hue, saturation and lightness. Easy to adjust by hand.","ex":"hsl(225 72% 50%)","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"HTML","id":"term-html","cat":"html","def":"HyperText Markup Language: the language that describes the structure and meaning of web page content.","ex":"","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"ID","id":"term-id","cat":"css","def":"A unique name for one element, selected with #. IDs have very high specificity, so prefer classes for styling.","ex":"#featured  →  <div id=\"featured\">","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"index.html","id":"term-index-html","cat":"workflow","def":"The name for a site's home page, or the main page in any folder. Servers open it automatically.","ex":"","page":"publishing.html"},{"term":"Inheritance","id":"term-inheritance","cat":"css","def":"Some properties, mostly text-related ones like color and font, pass from a parent to its children automatically.","ex":"","page":"cascade.html"},{"term":"Inline element","id":"term-inline-element","cat":"css","def":"An element that flows within a line of text, like span, a or strong. Width and height don't apply.","ex":"display: inline;","page":"display.html"},{"term":"Inline style","id":"term-inline-style","cat":"css","def":"CSS written in an element's style attribute. It beats stylesheet rules, so avoid it.","ex":"<p style=\"color: red\">","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Label","id":"term-label","cat":"html","def":"Text connected to a form control. Its for attribute matches the input's id.","ex":"<label for=\"email\">Email</label>","page":"forms.html"},{"term":"Landmark","id":"term-landmark","cat":"html","def":"A major page region, like header, nav, main or footer, that screen reader users can jump to.","ex":"","page":"semantic-elements.html"},{"term":"Main axis","id":"term-main-axis","cat":"layout","def":"In Flexbox, the direction items are laid out in, set by flex-direction. justify-content works along it.","ex":"","page":"flexbox.html"},{"term":"Margin","id":"term-margin","cat":"css","def":"Transparent space outside an element's border that pushes other elements away.","ex":"margin: 0 auto;","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"Media query","id":"term-media-query","cat":"layout","def":"A condition that applies CSS only when it's true, usually based on screen width.","ex":"@media (min-width: 900px) { … }","page":"media-queries.html"},{"term":"Mobile first","id":"term-mobile-first","cat":"layout","def":"Writing the phone layout first, then adding min-width media queries for larger screens.","ex":"","page":"mobile-first.html"},{"term":"Nesting","id":"term-nesting","cat":"html","def":"Putting elements inside other elements. Inner ones must close before outer ones.","ex":"<p><strong>…</strong></p>","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Normal flow","id":"term-normal-flow","cat":"layout","def":"How elements are laid out with no layout CSS: blocks stack down the page, inline content flows in lines.","ex":"","page":"display.html"},{"term":"object-fit","id":"term-object-fit","cat":"css","def":"Decides how an image fills a box of a different shape: stretch, fit inside, or crop to cover.","ex":"object-fit: cover;","page":"responsive-images.html"},{"term":"Overflow","id":"term-overflow","cat":"css","def":"What happens when content is bigger than its box: visible, hidden, or scrollable.","ex":"overflow: auto;","page":"overflow.html"},{"term":"Padding","id":"term-padding","cat":"css","def":"Space inside an element, between its content and its border.","ex":"padding: 16px;","page":"box-model.html"},{"term":"Parent","id":"term-parent","cat":"html","def":"The element directly containing another element.","ex":"","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Placeholder","id":"term-placeholder","cat":"html","def":"Hint text shown in an empty input. It disappears when typing starts, so it can't replace a label.","ex":"placeholder=\"you@example.com\"","page":"forms.html"},{"term":"position","id":"term-position","cat":"layout","def":"Controls how an element is placed: static, relative, absolute, fixed or sticky.","ex":"position: absolute;","page":"positioning.html"},{"term":"Property","id":"term-property","cat":"css","def":"What a declaration changes, like color, margin or font-size.","ex":"font-size","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Pseudo-class","id":"term-pseudo-class","cat":"css","def":"A keyword with one colon that selects elements in a state or position.","ex":":hover  :focus-visible  :nth-child(2)","page":"pseudo-classes.html"},{"term":"Pseudo-element","id":"term-pseudo-element","cat":"css","def":"A keyword with two colons that styles part of an element or adds decorative content.","ex":"::before  ::first-letter","page":"pseudo-elements.html"},{"term":"Relative path","id":"term-relative-path","cat":"html","def":"Directions to a file starting from the current file's folder. ../ goes up one folder.","ex":"../images/logo.png","page":"links-paths.html"},{"term":"rem (unit)","id":"term-rem-unit","cat":"css","def":"A length relative to the root (html) font size, usually 16px. Good for font sizes.","ex":"font-size: 1.25rem;","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"Repository","id":"term-repository","cat":"workflow","def":"A project folder tracked by Git, with its full history of changes. On GitHub, it's often called a repo.","ex":"","page":"publishing.html"},{"term":"Responsive design","id":"term-responsive-design","cat":"layout","def":"Building pages that adapt to any screen size, using flexible layouts, images and media queries.","ex":"","page":"media-queries.html"},{"term":"Root-relative path","id":"term-root-relative-path","cat":"html","def":"A path starting with /, measured from the top of the domain. Breaks on GitHub Pages project sites.","ex":"/images/logo.png","page":"links-paths.html"},{"term":"Rule","id":"term-rule","cat":"css","def":"A selector plus its block of declarations. Also called a ruleset.","ex":"h1 { color: navy; }","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Screen reader","id":"term-screen-reader","cat":"workflow","def":"Software that reads a page aloud for people who are blind or have low vision. It relies on semantic HTML and alt text.","ex":"","page":"accessibility.html"},{"term":"Selector","id":"term-selector","cat":"css","def":"The part of a rule that picks which elements to style.","ex":".card h2","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Semantic HTML","id":"term-semantic-html","cat":"html","def":"Choosing elements for what content means, like nav, article or button, rather than how it looks.","ex":"","page":"semantic-elements.html"},{"term":"Sibling","id":"term-sibling","cat":"html","def":"Elements that share the same parent.","ex":"h2 + p","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Source order","id":"term-source-order","cat":"css","def":"Where a rule appears in the CSS. When everything else ties, the later rule wins.","ex":"","page":"cascade.html"},{"term":"Specificity","id":"term-specificity","cat":"css","def":"A score for how targeted a selector is: IDs beat classes, classes beat elements. Higher specificity wins a conflict.","ex":"#nav a  beats  .link","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"srcset","id":"term-srcset","cat":"html","def":"A list of image files at different widths. The browser downloads the smallest one that will still look sharp.","ex":"srcset=\"trail-400.jpg 400w, trail-800.jpg 800w\"","page":"responsive-images.html"},{"term":"Stylesheet","id":"term-stylesheet","cat":"css","def":"A .css file of rules, linked from the head of each HTML page.","ex":"<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Tag","id":"term-tag","cat":"html","def":"The text in angle brackets that marks where an element starts or ends.","ex":"<p>  </p>","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"Transition","id":"term-transition","cat":"css","def":"Smoothly animates a change from one style to another, such as on hover.","ex":"transition: background 0.2s ease;","page":"transitions.html"},{"term":"Type selector","id":"term-type-selector","cat":"css","def":"A selector that matches every element of one kind by its tag name.","ex":"p  h2  button","page":"selectors.html"},{"term":"Unit","id":"term-unit","cat":"css","def":"What a number measures in, like px, rem, %, vw or s. Most values except 0 need one.","ex":"16px  1.5rem  50%","page":"colors-units.html"},{"term":"URL","id":"term-url","cat":"html","def":"A web address, like https://example.com/trails/index.html.","ex":"","page":"links-paths.html"},{"term":"User agent stylesheet","id":"term-user-agent-stylesheet","cat":"css","def":"The browser's built-in default styles, such as margins on headings and blue underlined links.","ex":"","page":"devtools.html"},{"term":"Validator","id":"term-validator","cat":"workflow","def":"A tool that checks HTML or CSS against the official rules and reports errors with line numbers.","ex":"","page":"validation.html"},{"term":"Value","id":"term-value","cat":"css","def":"The setting given to a property in a declaration.","ex":"navy, 2rem, auto","page":"css-basics.html"},{"term":"Viewport","id":"term-viewport","cat":"layout","def":"The visible area of the browser window. vw and vh are percentages of it.","ex":"","page":"sizing.html"},{"term":"Viewport meta tag","id":"term-viewport-meta-tag","cat":"html","def":"Tells phones to use the real screen width instead of shrinking a desktop-sized page.","ex":"<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">","page":"document-structure.html"},{"term":"Web font","id":"term-web-font","cat":"css","def":"A font downloaded with the page, for example from Google Fonts, so everyone sees the same typeface.","ex":"","page":"web-fonts.html"},{"term":"Whitespace","id":"term-whitespace","cat":"html","def":"Spaces, tabs and line breaks. In HTML, any run of whitespace shows as a single space.","ex":"","page":"html-syntax.html"},{"term":"z-index","id":"term-z-index","cat":"layout","def":"Controls which positioned elements appear on top when they overlap. Higher numbers come forward.","ex":"z-index: 10;","page":"positioning.html"},{"term":"Client and server","id":"term-client-and-server","cat":"workflow","def":"The browser is the client: it asks for files. The server is the computer that stores the site and sends them back.","ex":"","page":"how-the-web-works.html"},{"term":"Domain name","id":"term-domain-name","cat":"workflow","def":"The human-friendly name of a server, like github.io. DNS looks up the numeric address behind it.","ex":"trailclub.github.io","page":"how-the-web-works.html"},{"term":"HTTP status code","id":"term-http-status-code","cat":"workflow","def":"A number the server sends back with every response. 200 means OK, 404 means not found.","ex":"404 Not Found","page":"how-the-web-works.html"},{"term":"Local server","id":"term-local-server","cat":"workflow","def":"A small web server running on your own computer, like VS Code's Live Server, so you can test pages the way the real web serves them.","ex":"http://127.0.0.1:5500","page":"how-the-web-works.html"},{"term":"Favicon","id":"term-favicon","cat":"html","def":"The small icon in the browser tab and bookmarks, linked from the head.","ex":"<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"favicon.svg\">","page":"head-metadata.html"},{"term":"Meta description","id":"term-meta-description","cat":"html","def":"A one-sentence summary of a page in the head. Search engines may show it under your title.","ex":"<meta name=\"description\" content=\"…\">","page":"head-metadata.html"},{"term":"Open Graph","id":"term-open-graph","cat":"html","def":"og: meta tags that control the preview card when a link is shared in chats and social posts.","ex":"<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://…\">","page":"head-metadata.html"},{"term":"Lossy / lossless","id":"term-lossy-lossless","cat":"workflow","def":"Lossy formats like JPG throw away some detail to make files smaller. Lossless formats like PNG keep every pixel.","ex":"","page":"image-formats.html"},{"term":"SVG","id":"term-svg","cat":"html","def":"Scalable Vector Graphics: an image made of shapes described in code, so it stays sharp at any size. Best for logos and icons.","ex":"<img src=\"logo.svg\" alt=\"Trail Club\">","page":"image-formats.html"},{"term":"WebP","id":"term-webp","cat":"html","def":"A modern image format that's usually smaller than JPG or PNG at the same quality, and supports transparency.","ex":"photo.webp","page":"image-formats.html"},{"term":"iframe","id":"term-iframe","cat":"html","def":"An element that shows another web page inside yours. Used for YouTube videos, maps and other embeds.","ex":"<iframe src=\"…\" title=\"Trail map\"></iframe>","page":"embeds.html"},{"term":"Embed","id":"term-embed","cat":"html","def":"Content from another site shown on your page, usually with an iframe the other site gives you.","ex":"","page":"embeds.html"},{"term":"sizes","id":"term-sizes","cat":"html","def":"Tells the browser how wide an image will display at different window widths, so it can pick the right file from srcset.","ex":"sizes=\"(min-width: 700px) 50vw, 100vw\"","page":"picture.html"},{"term":"picture","id":"term-picture","cat":"html","def":"Wraps several source elements and an img, so the browser can choose a different crop or format.","ex":"<picture><source …><img …></picture>","page":"picture.html"},{"term":"Art direction","id":"term-art-direction","cat":"html","def":"Showing a different crop of an image at different screen sizes, not just a smaller copy.","ex":"","page":"picture.html"},{"term":"Lazy loading","id":"term-lazy-loading","cat":"html","def":"Waiting to download an image or iframe until the visitor scrolls near it.","ex":"loading=\"lazy\"","page":"picture.html"},{"term":"clamp()","id":"term-clamp","cat":"css","def":"A value that grows with the screen but stays between a minimum and a maximum: clamp(min, preferred, max).","ex":"font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1rem + 3vw, 3.5rem);","page":"fluid-sizing.html"},{"term":"min() / max()","id":"term-min-max","cat":"css","def":"min() uses the smallest of its values, max() the largest. min() works like a max-width.","ex":"width: min(640px, 100% - 2rem);","page":"fluid-sizing.html"},{"term":"calc()","id":"term-calc","cat":"css","def":"Does math with mixed units. The + and - need spaces around them.","ex":"width: calc(100% - 2rem);","page":"fluid-sizing.html"},{"term":"Transform","id":"term-transform","cat":"css","def":"Moves, rotates, scales or skews an element after layout, without moving anything around it.","ex":"transform: rotate(10deg) scale(1.1);","page":"transforms.html"},{"term":"transform-origin","id":"term-transform-origin","cat":"css","def":"The point an element rotates and scales around. The default is its center.","ex":"transform-origin: top left;","page":"transforms.html"},{"term":"addEventListener()","id":"term-addeventlistener","cat":"js","def":"Runs a function whenever a certain event happens on an element. Pass the function itself, without parentheses.","ex":"btn.addEventListener(\"click\", openMenu);","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"Anonymous function","id":"term-anonymous-function","cat":"js","def":"A function with no name, usually passed straight into another function that will call it later.","ex":"btn.addEventListener(\"click\", function () { … });","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"Argument","id":"term-argument","cat":"js","def":"A value you pass into a function when you call it. It fills in one of the function's parameters.","ex":"hikeTime(6.7, 2.5)","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"Array","id":"term-array","cat":"js","def":"An ordered list of values stored under one name. Items are numbered from 0.","ex":"const trails = [\"Mesa\", \"Royal Arch\"];","page":"js-arrays.html"},{"term":"Arrow function","id":"term-arrow-function","cat":"js","def":"A short way to write a function. With one expression and no braces, it returns that value automatically.","ex":"const double = (n) => n * 2;","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"async / await","id":"term-async-await","cat":"js","def":"A way to write code that waits for a promise, such as fetch(), as if it ran top to bottom. await only works inside an async function (or a module).","ex":"const res = await fetch(url);","page":"js-fetch.html"},{"term":"Boolean","id":"term-boolean","cat":"js","def":"A value that is either true or false. Comparisons produce booleans.","ex":"const isOpen = miles < 5;","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"classList","id":"term-classlist","cat":"js","def":"An element's list of classes, with add(), remove() and toggle() methods. The usual way to change how an element looks from JavaScript.","ex":"menu.classList.toggle(\"open\");","page":"js-dom.html"},{"term":"Concatenation","id":"term-concatenation","cat":"js","def":"Joining strings together with +. If either side is a string, + joins instead of adding.","ex":"\"5\" + 5 // \"55\"","page":"js-strings-numbers.html"},{"term":"Conditional","id":"term-conditional","cat":"js","def":"Code that only runs if a condition is true: if, else if and else.","ex":"if (temp < 32) { … } else { … }","page":"js-conditionals.html"},{"term":"console.log()","id":"term-console-log","cat":"js","def":"Prints a value to the browser's console. The quickest way to check what your code is doing.","ex":"console.log(\"score is\", score);","page":"js-errors.html"},{"term":"const","id":"term-const","cat":"js","def":"Declares a variable that can't be reassigned. Use it by default; switch to let only when the value must change.","ex":"const name = \"Ava\";","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"Constructor","id":"term-constructor","cat":"js","def":"A function that builds objects of one kind. Its name starts with a capital letter, it sets values on this, and you call it with new.","ex":"const mesa = new Trail(\"Mesa\", 6.7);","page":"js-objects-json.html"},{"term":"createElement()","id":"term-createelement","cat":"js","def":"Makes a new element in memory. It appears on the page only after you append it somewhere.","ex":"const li = document.createElement(\"li\");","page":"js-create-elements.html"},{"term":"defer","id":"term-defer","cat":"js","def":"A script attribute that downloads the file while the page loads and runs it after the HTML is read, so your code can find every element.","ex":"<script defer src=\"script.js\"></script>","page":"js-intro.html"},{"term":"Event","id":"term-event","cat":"js","def":"A signal that something happened, such as a click, a key press or a form being submitted.","ex":"click, input, submit, keydown","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"Event bubbling","id":"term-event-bubbling","cat":"js","def":"After an event fires on an element, it travels up through each ancestor, running their listeners too.","ex":"","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"Event delegation","id":"term-event-delegation","cat":"js","def":"Putting one listener on a parent to handle events from all its children, using event.target to tell them apart.","ex":"list.addEventListener(\"click\", (e) => { … });","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"Event listener","id":"term-event-listener","cat":"js","def":"A function that runs when a particular event happens on an element. Also called an event handler.","ex":"btn.addEventListener(\"click\", save);","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"event.target","id":"term-event-target","cat":"js","def":"The element an event actually happened on. Compare event.currentTarget, the element whose listener is running.","ex":"e.target.textContent","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"fetch()","id":"term-fetch","cat":"js","def":"Requests a file or data from a server and returns a promise for the response. Doesn't work on pages opened from file://.","ex":"fetch(\"trails.json\")","page":"js-fetch.html"},{"term":"Function","id":"term-function","cat":"js","def":"A named, reusable block of code. It can take parameters and return a value.","ex":"function greet(name) { return `Hi ${name}`; }","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"Index","id":"term-index","cat":"js","def":"An item's position in an array or a character's position in a string, counting from 0.","ex":"trails[0]","page":"js-arrays.html"},{"term":"JavaScript","id":"term-javascript","cat":"js","def":"The programming language of the web. It adds behavior to pages: responding to clicks, changing content and loading data.","ex":"","page":"js-intro.html"},{"term":"JSON","id":"term-json","cat":"js","def":"JavaScript Object Notation: a text format for data that looks like a JavaScript object, with stricter rules (double quotes, no trailing commas, no comments).","ex":"{ \"name\": \"Mesa\", \"miles\": 6.7 }","page":"js-objects-json.html"},{"term":"let","id":"term-let","cat":"js","def":"Declares a variable whose value can be changed later.","ex":"let score = 0; score = 10;","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"Loop","id":"term-loop","cat":"js","def":"Code that repeats, usually once for each item in a list: for...of, for, or while.","ex":"for (const trail of trails) { … }","page":"js-loops.html"},{"term":"Method","id":"term-method","cat":"js","def":"A function that belongs to an object, called with a dot.","ex":"text.toUpperCase()","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"NaN","id":"term-nan","cat":"js","def":"\"Not a Number\": the result of math that failed, usually from trying to convert text that isn't a number.","ex":"Number(\"abc\") // NaN","page":"js-strings-numbers.html"},{"term":"Node","id":"term-node","cat":"js","def":"One object in the DOM tree: an element, a piece of text or a comment. Nodes have parents, children and siblings.","ex":"","page":"js-dom.html"},{"term":"null / undefined","id":"term-null-undefined","cat":"js","def":"Two ways of saying \"no value\". undefined means nothing was ever set; null means it was set to nothing on purpose. querySelector returns null when nothing matches.","ex":"","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"Object","id":"term-object","cat":"js","def":"A group of related values stored under named keys.","ex":"const hike = { name: \"Mesa\", miles: 6.7 };","page":"js-objects-json.html"},{"term":"Object literal","id":"term-object-literal","cat":"js","def":"An object written out in full with curly braces and key: value pairs, separated by commas.","ex":"{ name: \"Mesa\", miles: 6.7 }","page":"js-objects-json.html"},{"term":"Parameter","id":"term-parameter","cat":"js","def":"A name in a function's definition that stands for a value passed in when it's called.","ex":"function hikeTime(miles, pace = 2)","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"preventDefault()","id":"term-preventdefault","cat":"js","def":"Stops the browser's default action for an event, like a form reloading the page or a link navigating away.","ex":"e.preventDefault();","page":"js-events.html"},{"term":"Promise","id":"term-promise","cat":"js","def":"A placeholder for a value that isn't ready yet, such as a server's response. Use .then() and .catch(), or await, to get the result.","ex":"fetch(url).then((res) => res.json())","page":"js-fetch.html"},{"term":"querySelector()","id":"term-queryselector","cat":"js","def":"Finds the first element that matches a CSS selector, or null. querySelectorAll() finds every match.","ex":"document.querySelector(\".card\")","page":"js-dom.html"},{"term":"Return value","id":"term-return-value","cat":"js","def":"The value a function sends back to where it was called, using return. Without return, it's undefined.","ex":"return miles / pace;","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"Scope","id":"term-scope","cat":"js","def":"Where a variable can be used. Variables declared inside a function or block only exist there.","ex":"","page":"js-functions.html"},{"term":"String","id":"term-string","cat":"js","def":"Text, written in quotes: \"double\", 'single' or `backticks`.","ex":"\"Royal Arch\"","page":"js-strings-numbers.html"},{"term":"Syntax error","id":"term-syntax-error","cat":"js","def":"A typo in the structure of the code, such as a missing bracket or quote. The whole script stops before it runs.","ex":"SyntaxError: missing ) after argument list","page":"js-errors.html"},{"term":"Template literal","id":"term-template-literal","cat":"js","def":"A string in backticks that can include variables with ${ }.","ex":"`${trail} is ${miles} miles`","page":"js-strings-numbers.html"},{"term":"textContent","id":"term-textcontent","cat":"js","def":"The text inside an element. Setting it replaces the contents with plain text, which is safer than innerHTML for anything a user typed.","ex":"heading.textContent = \"Hello\";","page":"js-dom.html"},{"term":"Truthy / falsy","id":"term-truthy-falsy","cat":"js","def":"How a value behaves in an if. false, 0, \"\", null, undefined and NaN are falsy; everything else is truthy.","ex":"if (name) { … }","page":"js-conditionals.html"},{"term":"typeof","id":"term-typeof","cat":"js","def":"Tells you a value's type as a string.","ex":"typeof \"42\" // \"string\"","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"Variable","id":"term-variable","cat":"js","def":"A named container for a value, declared with let or const.","ex":"const city = \"Boulder\";","page":"js-variables.html"},{"term":"=== (strict equality)","id":"term-strict-equality","cat":"js","def":"Checks whether two values are the same, including their type. Use it instead of ==.","ex":"\"5\" === 5 // false","page":"js-conditionals.html"}]};
